A Republican strategy to hamper Obama's re-election
Republican leaders justified their policy for the need to fight electoral fraud.
However, the Brennan Center considers the latter a myth and claims that electoral fraud is less frequent in the United States than the number of people who die from lightning strikes.
In fact, Republican lawyers have found no more than 300 cases of voter fraud in the United States in ten years.
One thing is certain: these new provisions will negatively affect the participation rate.
In that regard, these measures will partly undermine the American democratic system.
Contrary to what is happening in Canada, U.S. states are responsible for organizing federal elections in the United States.
And in the same vein, a majority of U.S
This phenomenon has widened after the November 2010 elections, which saw the rise of 675 new Republican representatives in 26 states.
As a result, 180 bills were introduced during 2011 restricting the exercise of the right to vote in 41 states.
The new electoral laws require voters to present an identity document with a photograph, in addition to proof of U.S. citizenship.
Moreover, these laws reduce periods of early voting, invalidate the right to register as an elector on the day of scrutiny and withdraw their right to vote to citizens with criminal records.
Prior to the 2006 elections, no U.S
The State of Indiana was the first to demand it.
The U.S
Therefore, the republican authorities rushed to extend that practice to other states.
In recent years these authorities have supported bills in 34 states to force voters to file a photographed identity card.
It is important to keep in mind that, contrary to what happens in Quebec, American citizens do not have a universal identity card, such as the Social Security card.
In fact, 11% of U.S
On the other hand, five million new constituents in 2012 lack such an identity document.
But, often, obtaining the required identity document costs more than a hundred dollars.
Thus, new restrictions disproportionately affect young people, minorities and low-income people.
Indeed, 25% of African-Americans, 15% of people who earn less than 35,000 dollars a year, 18% of citizens over the age of 65 and 20% of voters between the ages of 18 and 29 do not have a photographed identity document.
And there's even more.
In many states students, voters who vote most for Democratic candidates can’t use the photo ID issued by their institution.
On the other hand, those same states authorize members of the fishing or hunting clubs who usually vote for the Republican parties to use the documents issued by those clubs.
Prior to 2004, no state required proof of citizenship to vote.
Arizona was the first state to file this requirement.
Since 2011, a dozen states have adopted laws requiring voters to prove they are U.S. citizens.
These measures clearly seek to limit Hispanic voting.
It appears that two out of every three Hispanic voters support the Democratic party.
On the other hand, Republican legislators supported in 2011 certain laws abolishing voter registration on the same day of scrutiny in eight states.
In addition, they limited the right of individuals and groups to set up an aid service for voters who would like to register.
These restrictions have consequences.
For example, in the 2004 general elections, voter registration campaigns contributed to the registration of around 10 million citizens.
Measures taken since 2009 resulted in the registration rate of new voters falling in 2010 compared to 2006.
In addition, Republican legislators adopted laws in five other states that favored the reduction of the voting period in advance.
For example, in Florida’s 2008 elections, 33% of voters who voted in advance were African-Americans, while the latter accounted for more than 13 percent of the state’s voters.
In the case of Hispanics, exactly the same thing happened.
These represent no more than 11% of voters, equivalent to 24% of citizens voting in advance.
However, whites, who represent 76% of the electorate, represent only 46% of citizens voting in advance.
Of course, Democratic legislators and their supporters have fervently objected to the adoption of laws that restrict voter registration.
In this regard, the Democratic governors have blocked several bills.
The U.S
In part, it was possible to limit the damage.
For example, only 16 out of 34 states adopted the law that required the presentation of a photographed identity card.
However, it is undeniable that the new rules adopted will complicate the exercise of the right to vote in 2012.
Democratic critics denounce the partisan character of the laws that have been voted on, since it is clear that they are intended to influence the results of 2012 in the key states.
A 2011 Brennan Center report shows that the states that have adopted such laws represent 171 of the 270 votes needed in the electoral college to win the presidency.
It is too early to affirm with certainty whether these legislative changes at the electoral system level will have a significant impact on the results of the 2012 presidential elections.
One thing is certain: these new provisions will negatively affect the participation rate.
In this regard, the measures will partly undermine the American democratic system.
Detection of Prostate Cancer: Taking the Test or Not Doing It?
Indeed, the PSA test sometimes has erroneous results, with false negative results or even false positive results, after which useless medical interventions are decided.
It is a more than sufficient reason to make men, already reticent of themselves, doubt about whether or not screening tests should be done.
Take the test or not?
We've given advice to two specialists.
Studies conducted in the United States detected a large rate of contagion among sample groups, making it difficult to interpret that data and make strong recommendations.
For its part, the European study showed some mortality difference between the patients who had been screened and those who had not.
The study, with a follow-up at the age of 12, also showed that there is between 30 and 40% chance of metastasis if the detection test has not been performed.
I recommend, therefore, the test from the age of 50, or from the age of 40 if your father has suffered from prostate cancer.
Men of African-American origin have a higher risk rate.
The key is to make a good decision once the cancer is detected.
There are aggressive cancers and other cancers that are indolent.
It is truly necessary to ensure that the patient understands the degree of risk of their cancer. They should be offered all possible options and not necessarily to treat prostate cancers that do not threaten life in the long term, in which case you should rather opt for an active surveillance of the disease.
Today many men who have been screened for cancer are untreated, since this cancer is neither aggressive nor life-threatening.
Instead, we suggest active surveillance of the disease and, if it progresses, we offer treatment.
We determine more and more often precise criteria to decide who should or should not be treated.
I recommend that the test be done in any case.
But it’s important that you talk to your doctor to determine whether or not the test should be done.
Movember has created, in collaboration with the International Society of Urology, a tool that allows to evaluate the pros and cons of the PSA test.
This document can be downloaded (for the moment only in English, but translations will be offered shortly) at the following address: http://ca.movember.com/fr/mens-health/prostate-cancer-screening
Prevent the disease
Unfortunately, there’s no miraculous prescription to prevent cancer.
Despite advances in research, the adoption of healthy living habits remains the best way to reduce the risks of disease.
We estimate that if everyone ate well and moved enough, 30% of cancers could be prevented.
"If no one smoked, that rate would rise to at least 50%," said André Beaulieu, a spokeswoman for the Canadian Cancer Society.
Instead, we assess that about 10% of cancers are hereditary.
Many others remain totally inexplicable.
For the Canadian Society against Cancer, the fight against smoking remains a priority despite the declining number of smokers.
Tobacco is linked to 85% of lung cancer cases.
It is also a risk factor for other types of cancers.
It greatly harms people's health.
"Today, there are still 1.5 million smokers in Quebec," spokesman André Beaulieu said.
A hopeful fact: 10 years after quitting smoking, the risk of dying from cancer drops by half.
The Weight
Faity and obesity also favor the onset of the disease, according to SCC.
They increase the risks of breast, colon and rectal, esophagus, pancreatic and uterine cancer.
"Research shows that the regular practice of lifelong physical activity protects against breast cancer."
The Food
The body also recommends limiting the consumption of red meat.
In large quantities, they escalate the risks of suffering colorectal cancer.
Charcuterie products should also be avoided.
Preservation of the meat for smoking, drying or salting can lead to the formation of carcinogenic substances.
"They can damage the cells of the body and lead to the development of cancer," they explain.
Vitamins
In recent years, several scientists have studied the links between vitamin complexes and cancer.
However, at the moment their investigations are inconclusive.
According to SCC, studies on vitamin E are contradictory.
While a decrease in the risks of prostate cancer was found in one of them, in another what was observed was, rather, an increase.
The effect of vitamin D on cancer has not been clearly determined either.
Moreover, M. Beaulieu insists on the importance of talking about everyone’s concerns and family history with their doctor.
"Doing the screening test doesn't cause cancer."
Higgs boson in detail
The announcement of Higgs boson’s likely discovery sparked a huge commotion last summer, and rightly so.
It is believed, in fact, that this boson is part of the mechanism responsible for the mass of everything that exists in the universe, nothing more and nothing less.
Moreover, it is the particle whose existence foretold the standard model: our best or "less evil" explanation of the nature and behavior of matter, which has not yet been observed empirically.
But for physicists it's not entirely clear that it's Higgs.
We know without a doubt that there is a new particle and that it is very similar to the Higgs boson predicted by the standard model.
In addition, new data unveiled this week at a major physics congress held in Kyoto seems to confirm that it is, although there is still more data to be completely safe.
Anyway, we assume it's Higgs, since the chances of being mistaken look a few. We'll see what it's about.
There is, in this great world, an inescapable and inescapable law which implies that two things cannot be found in the same place at the same time.
There's no way to break that rule and don't try too hard, because it'll end up hurting yourself.
Although the physics of particles is a very complex world, it has been shown that also in it there is a law of these characteristics: Pauli's principle of exclusion, which stipulates that two particles cannot occupy the same space at the same time if they are in the same "quantum state"; a "state" consisting, roughly, of some of its characteristics.
Physicists classify particles into two categories.
On the one hand we find the good citizens called fermions, who obey wisely at Pauli’s principle.
And on the other there are bosons, a dirty panda of anarchists who has no respect for anything, or at least not for this principle, which means that they can be found in the same place and at the same time.
According to the website The Particle Adventure of the Berkeley Laboratories that, by the way, is extraordinary, bosons are divided into two groups: mesons, of which we will not talk here, and the "particles of force", by which the great forces of nature are propagated and with which the Higgs boson would be somehow related.
These bosons, it might stand out here, aren't the exotic bugs we could believe.
In fact, if he manages to read this chronicle, it is thanks to an extraordinarily banal boson: the photon or the "particle of light" that acts as a "messenger" of the electromagnetic force.
Indeed, when a particle with an electric charge accelerates or changes direction, it "disorders" the electromagnetic field in that precise environment; a bit like when we throw a stone into a pond.
In this "disorder" an electromagnetic wave (of light, infrared, ultraviolet, etc.) arises, and this wave is nothing more than a photon; that is, one of the "force-bearing" bosons.
Most stable field
The same thing happens with the Higgs boson, with the difference that it is another field, the Higgs field, which needs to be "disordered" for this boson to appear.
However, the Higgs field is much more stable than the electromagnetic field. To activate it you need to reach extremely high energies, a little as if it were an icy pond in which only a thick rock could slide along the surface.
That’s why it takes an immense particle accelerator like CERN (the large hadron collider is a 27-km-churrel ring!) to reach such energy levels.
The analogy with the electromagnetic field is a new tool that explains the relationship between Higgs and mass.
In fact, not all particles or all materials interact with the electromagnetic field.
Somes, like magnets, do, but others don’t: a piece of paper, for example, won’t ever stand by itself attached to a fridge door.
And in the same way, not all particles interact with the Higgs field: those that do have a mass, while the rest (like the photon, by the way), do not have it.
Now, what "can they bring" all these investigations? asks Mrs. Plamondon.
For science, this serves to verify the validity of the standard model (MS), and also allows physicists to scrutinize the differences between MS observations and predictions.
For their part, many fervently want them to meet, because the slightest difference could open a door to a "new physics" that covers certain holes of the model.
This, it must be said, still has enormous deficiencies, such as that it does not propose any explanation relating to gravity (ups!) or to dark matter, which makes up about 80% of the matter of the Universe (another ups!).
But we haven’t finded such differences in CERN so far.
Repercussions
The repercussions of such day-to-day investigations are difficult to predict, but we don’t think we’re mistaken in saying it won’t have any.
For them to see: at the beginning of the 60's, the pioneers of the laser in the labs did not suspect at all the revolution that their work would provoke.
Scientific applications went through, but nothing more.
In fact, as physicist Willard Boyle, who worked in the Bell laboratories, where the laser was invented in 1960, and who made the first continuous laser (the former were pulsed) in 1962, at the beginning the laser was rather considered a "lab apparatus".
Imagine...
In addition, the applications can also come from all the instrumentation surrounding the research.
For example, Willard Boyle himself developed a light collector in 1969 in the course of his optics research.
Even though it wasn't his original intention at all, this catcher now serves as an "eye" for all of the world's digital gadgets, which earned him the Nobel of Physics in 2009.
That doesn’t mean, of course, that LHC’s activities necessarily want to transform our lives, but you never know...
Palliative care. The best way to die... | Le Devoir
In its Committee to Die in Dynignity, Quebec recently debated the delicate end-of-life issue.
Debate must soon be resumed as a bill prepares.
Now, within that essential domain, there is still a lot to do.
Le Devoir has tried to see him up close.
Just a few weeks ago, Mr. L. lived alone in his Montérégie apartment.
The prostate cancer he was destroying had given him a two-year respite.
"They had given me five years of life, but I've been like this," he recounts, one raisin, lying in his bed in the Victor-Gadbois de Beloeil palliative care center, to which he arrived the day before.
"But it's always a shock, we can't be prepared for this," he continues.
The disease has wreaked havoc on you: an enormous weakness that prevents you from going to the bathroom alone and even eating alone.
Sitting in front of an appetizing lunch, he accepts resigned to help him eat.
Brave, he even smiles, to talk to strangers who dedicate attention to him, bring him medicine and offer him a bath.
Bravery of Ordinary Death.
"My biggest wish is to be healed by diarrhea, it's humiliating," he confesses.
A few hours later, the team found a remedy for that evil.
"Over the course of our lives we learn that a man pees on his feet," says Pierre Brodeur, a psychologist at the Victor-Gadbois center.
Going back to their childhood, for certain people, is an unacceptable humiliation.
"That depends on the person's ability to accept that regression," he says.
Because, according to the opinion of the majority of people who work in palliative care, the great moments take place at the very heart of that regression.
All patients who enter the Victor-Gadbois palliative care center have cancer.
They have a maximum life forecast of three months.
In that state, the team of doctors and nurses around them no longer offer “healing” care.
Ms. A.’s biggest fear, 89, is dying “conscious and drowned.”
But illness has made him discover his children.
"I've got good kids," he adds.
"I don't have any more desires left in this life," she says, before agreeing to have a mask placed to help her breathe.
And he expects, in the next few days, a last visit of his son who lives in Italy.
Victor-Gadbois offers, with a group of volunteers, the care of the body and aid to food.
They are palliative care given to them when nothing else can be done.
To help make his death more comfortable.
In Quebec we have a palliative care bed for every 11,700 inhabitants.
It's very little, since we know we'll all end up dying someday.
Here, life continues in the best possible conditions, explains Dr. Christiane Martel, one of the doctors in the center.
Either at the level of physical, emotional or spiritual comfort.
The moribundo is offered mouth rinses with brandy or Pepsi, according to the lawsuit.
Diabetics are no longer controlled for their sugar rate.
Death is part of the everyday.
Last night we served a beer to Mr. X, who died over the course of the night.
This morning, his son finished the beer at the feet of the deceased.
“We help both patients and their loved ones,” said Nathalie Savard, director of care.
In the Victor-Gadbois center the days pass without ever being the same.
Next to a 94-year-old man savoring his last family reunion in the courtyard, well placed among his pillows while everyone drinks to his health, a 36-year-old dies tragically, surrounded by his parents, his wife and two children, after having tried everything to survive.
"For six months, there have always been three to five beds occupied by people under the age of 45 who are sick with cancer," Dr. Christiane Martel said.
53% of patients who are admitted to the Victor-Gadboisarrivent center come from home and 47% from the hospital.
Problem of access to palliative care
77% of Canadians simply do not have access to palliative care, that is, to the cares designed to relieve suffering when a patient arrives in the terminal phase of their life, whether at home, in the hospital or in a care center.
And several organizations, such as the Victor-Gadbois center and Grand Montréal’s palliative home care society, specialize almost exclusively in the care of cancer patients.
It is precisely this great relative lack in the health care of Quebec that causes many doctors specializing in palliative care to address the adoption of a law that regulates euthanasia and assisted suicide.
Since the month of October, a manifesto signed by eminences of palliative care, such as Dr. Balfour Mount and Dr. Bernard Lapointe, circulates to demonstrate their opposition to such an initiative.
According to Dr. Christiane Martel, Quebec’s healthcare system is not competent enough to ensure that anyone is entitled to quality palliative care before euthanasia is agreed to proceed.
"Recently," he says, "I've seen a patient spend 14 days in Emergencies suffering great pains without having done what is necessary to relieve her.
"I'm afraid patients want to die just because they don't get proper care."
And in parallel, many oncologists obstinate with their patients until the last day, despite the worst prognosis.
Hélène Richard’s hopes for survival were already under minimums when he left an exhausting chemotherapy treatment.
"When I told my oncologist that I wanted to stop the treatment, she told me that she was very sorry that she wanted to stop fighting," she says.
"However, she had told me that there was nothing else to do!"
There is no all-powerful care
Dr. Martel believes that 90% of the patients who request to die thank their caregivers who have not treated them in their demands once their pain has been relieved thanks to a palliative care team.
But it must be said that palliative care is not absolutely almighty when it comes to pain treatment.
According to Elsie Monereau, director of palliative home care in Grand Montréal, patients do not respond to pain treatments in 8% of cases.
At the end of life, doctors usually resort to palliative sedation, which serves to sleep the patient until the time of death, either sporadically or permanently.
We can’t pretend we don’t understand that part of suffering.
Increasingly, unrelieved disease is going to have the possibility to have that palliative sedation.
Sick people who aren’t relieved all say the same thing: “I want to die.”
But that doesn’t necessarily mean “I want euthanasia,” but “I want to be relieved.”
The conduct of this report has been possible thanks to a journalism scholarship from Canada’s health research institutes.
Spacious Real Estate Scandals in Quebec
Officials of the road network, BTP businessmen, fundraisers from political parties and specialists of the Italian mafia tell, day after day, what they know about a formidable "system", of which the construction industry, officials, politicians, trade unionists and mobsters are part.
An "industry" that came out too dear to Quebec taxpayers, especially in the years 1990 and 2000.
"It's funny how the system pulverizes once we've hunted the big media," says Jacques Duchesneau, a deputy in Quebec and former head of the Montreal police.
He led the outbreak of the scandal in 2011, which was made possible by an in-depth investigation into embezzlements linked to road contracts in Quebec, that liberal Prime Minister Jean Charest had consented only to delay.
"Duchesneau report" established a direct link between industry, clandestine party funding and corruption among officials.
"Since the beginning of the investigation in 2010, only the Ministry of Transportation would have saved a billion dollars in contracts," containing some of its instincts to keep a proportional share.
The Charbonneau Commission "has already dropped two mayors," he adds, as it hopes to “demonstrate the existing ploys behind the individuals.”
A permanent anti-corruption unit, created in 2011
The Permanent Anti-Corruption Unit, created in 2011, also applies with its team of controllers, researchers and government analysts.
This unit works in addition to the "Marteau squad's" cops, who, since 2009, have caused Montreal's "sewage cartel" to moderate when it comes to inflating contracts.
In recent weeks, the unit has proceeded to serially record allegations of fraud and corruption against municipal charges such as Frank Zampino and Richard Marcotte, mayor of a suburban city.
The next of the list would be Gilles Vaillancourt, who has just resigned from his position as mayor of Laval, the third city of Quebec.
It is suspected that repeated bribes have been pocketed in exchange for public contracts.
Engineers from the Montreal road network and businessmen of Italian origin, such as Tony Accurso and Lino Zambito, have been formally accused.
The latter has caused commotion in explaining to the commission the mechanics of the "system" of obtaining public contracts.
He himself paid for three years 3% of the value of the contracts obtained in Montreal to an intermediary linked to the mafia who transferred the money to Union Montréal, Mayor Gérald Tremblay’s party.
M. Zambito handed out money to the right and right during the 2000s, granting more than 88,000 Canadian dollars (about 68,000 euros) to provincial parties, especially the liberals in power.
He also admitted having organized a collection of illegal funds for former Liberal Deputy Prime Minister Nathalie Normandeau.
Inflation in the cost of sewage contracts
In Montreal, the "system" of corruption worked effectively.
Gilles Surprenant, a former public works engineer, explained it perfectly before the commission: in ten years he received gifts, travel invitations, golf tournaments, restaurants, hockey games and bribes from companies in the construction sector for a total of 736,000 dollars, all in exchange for sewage contracts whose costs inflated.
Other officials in the road network have admitted that they received money for inflating bills between 30% and 40% for false extras.
In addition, an organizer of the mayor’s party, Martin Dumont, accused M. Tremblay of deliberately turning a blind eye to a parallel budget and filling his coffers with dirty money.
In the wake of these revelations, M. Tremblay resigned in early November and led Montreal into a huge crisis.
Chantal Rouleau was one of Montreal’s first elected policies that sounded the alarm.
As mayor of the district of Rivière-des-Prairies, in the east of the island, she opposed since 2010 the sale of a municipal land acquired for 5 million dollars and resold for 1.6 million to developers in the middle of a real estate boom.
70% of election campaign money was dirty money
Of the investigation that will finally be carried out, she assures that she pulls "a thread to be able to understand the operation of the system, infiltrated by ants, to stop the gangrene and fish the culprits."
He adds that the process is "painful, but positive."
We are cleaning the wounds but it would take a clean research unit in Montreal and be very alert so that dubious practices are not repeated.
How to do the cleaning.
Regularly.
Jacques Duchesneau, for his part, declares that "officials have stolen hundreds of millions of dollars", but he is restless above all because of the role of "politicians elected in the mainstream of the ploy".
Estimating by 70% the proportion of dirty money that went to the financing of electoral campaigns in Quebec: "I've been told that this is nothing more than a pale reflection of reality."
The Quebec government proposes to limit donations to parties to 100 dollars, but that will not change the donation, according to him, "as long as electoral expenses are not strictly limited, there will be dirty money in politics."
It advocates a complete revision of the system of granting public contracts and party financing: "we can not fall lower; going to the bottom of things, with courage, will allow us to rebuild the house with more solid foundations, with more controls and laws."
If this story tarnishes the international image of Quebec and Montreal, M. Duchesneau invites those who laugh to look in the mirror.
