With Bitcoin, Pay and Sell Without Banks
Quite the opposite of the current monetary exchanges, based on central banks, identified transactions and processing burdens between the parties.
In addition, as often happens in technologies, the political vision is palpable: the conviction that the current monetary system, composed of banking monopolies, leads to financial crises.
In fact, Bitcoin, invented by Satoshi Nakamoto (a pseudonym), is both a virtual currency convertible to dollars, euros, etc. and a secure exchange protocol in BitTorrent’s way, which allows the exchange of peer-to-peer files.
There have been nearly 200,000 transactions thanks to 15,000 computers on the network.
And about a thousand websites accept bitcoins as donations or as a form of payment.
The price of bitcoin, after having reached the top of 30 dollars (23 euros) in June 2011, fell to 2 dollars five months later, before returning today to around ten dollars (trades are registered on bitcoincharts.com page).
It’s nothing impressive if we compare it with global changes in real currency or financial products.
However, the European Central Bank (ECB) became interested in a report on virtual currencies published in October.
Bitcoin was described as “the most successful virtual currency”, “competing with the dollar or euro” and “similar to conventional currencies”.
Bitcoin differs from other systems with types of virtual currencies such as "credits", used to advance in a video game and which can be earned playing or can be purchased (and even sometimes redeemed).
The social network Facebook also developed this type of system.
But in every exchange a central authority controls and treats changes.
With bitcoin, all network nodes are both ledger repositories, verifiers, currency issuers and buyers and sellers.
How does that network work?
Each transaction between two users is actually made between two e-mail addresses.
A user can choose a different address for each payment, thus ensuring anonymity.
A set of information linked to this transaction is signed electronically through a dual-key encryption system.
The network can thus verify the authenticity of the transaction.
Thanks to the contents of the file, it is also possible to ensure that the bitcoin changes contain in the public account book, which is disseminated throughout the network.
The key milestone is to write that new transaction in that book.
This goes through the resolution of a mathematical defiance launched to the computers and the winner, a kind of provisional central banker, will have the privilege of adding that complementary line.
It is a file chopping phase, that is, transforming a large file into a shorter and more unique numerical footprint.
Computers "take" the new transaction and add a number before "chopping" the set.
The goal is to find the number offered by a particular (zero footprint at the beginning).
Once that number is found, the other nodes easily verify that it’s the good one.
The transaction is then indestructibly linked to the chain of sets of the other transactions; any modification would change the footprint.
If, in order to commit a fraud, a user would want to pay twice with the same money very quickly (in less than ten minutes), the network would only acquit as valid one of the two transactions; the other would be orphaned, since both have different footprints.
The computer that solves the defiance takes 50 bitcoins.
To avoid inflation, that reward is divided into two regularly, with probability before the end of 2012.
The number of bitcoins in circulation is therefore limited to 21 million, but they are divisible up to the 100th, which leaves a wide margin.
The difficulty of this challenge is also revealed in each increase in calculation power.
The life of the network already has its ups and downs.
Websites that offer services for Bitcoin have been attacked and bitcoin have been stolen on deposit.
“The failure used doesn’t affect the same protocol,” says Pierre Noizat, who has just launched Paymium, a real-currency payment company that uses the Bitcoin network.
The ECB also takes into account the possibilities of money laundering thanks to this anonymous service.
But cash has the same defect.
Weight actors such as Wikipedia reject payments of this type.
Others, such as the Wordpress platform, accept them.
Recently, Adi Shamir and Dorit Ron of the Weizmann Institute in Israel analyzed the ledger and showed that about 80% of bitcoins don’t circulate.
In November "giant sales" were launched.
"30,000 dollars have been exchanged," rejoices Jon Holmquist, who works for Coinabul, which converts bitcoins into gold.
Pierre Noizat, author of a pedagogical book on that currency, believes a lot in the potential of this technology as well as in the transaction network.
His system, Paytunia, equals a credit card (in real currency) or a contactless mobile payment, but uses Bitcoin to validate transactions, which come out cheaper.
In addition, the bearer manages his identity and can, therefore, remain anonymous.
The system is easy to launch in stores, which do not have to install new terminals or software.
It is enough for them to supply an address that a phone can "photograph and recognize", says Pierre Noizat, who claims to have thousands of users.
There is a general movement of revision of hierarchical systems by more horizontal systems.
“It will take some time for Bitcoin to prevail, but in 2013 there could be a change,” he predicts.
On the other hand, in its report the ECB plans to reevaluate the various risks, today considered high, in the event that this currency succeeds.
We're out of Afghanistan.
And then?
French troops abandoned their zone of responsibility in Afghanistan (Kapisa and Surobi).
NATO and the Americans must leave at the end of 2014.
It’s time for the Afghan army to take back possession of its territory and for the Afghans to choose their future without expecting anything from us.
We have mostly punished the Afghan peasants, whom we have considered terrorists.
And to ourselves, with our 88 soldiers killed, plus the wounded and the mutilated.
The Taliban have foreign extremists and former refugee bosses in Pakistan, but often also peasants who reject the presence of the foreign army, as in Soviet times.
They want to defend their traditions, ancient and archaic, even if jihadists, Pakistanis, Arabs, Uzbeks or Tajiks come together.
Tolerates, sometimes even aided by the local insurgents, these will no longer be when Westerners become more discreet.
The departure of French troops from the Nijrab base, which I observed from the top of almond-planted hills thanks to French credits, was made in order.
Convoys of trucks and tanks returned to Kabul without any attacks, monitored by helicopters.
There will be no influx of Taliban in Kabul from the end of 2014.
Circumstances have changed since its irresistible advance between 1994 and 1996.
Kabul was then empty and the country engulfed in fire and blood because of struggles between different factions.
His takeover of the country had been perceived as a kind of liberation, the return to security.
Afghans have paid the price for the obscurantism of their peasants surpassed by the Al-Qaeda organization, but their situation is not better today.
The old Mujahideen, the current government and the current Taliban share a desire to keep women in a lower position.
The main personalities of anti-Soviet warfare returned to power in 2001.
They have become speculators, taking over government lands to resell them as plots for refugees who have returned from Iran and Pakistan, benefiting from huge U.S. subcontracting contracts.
They’re discredited; most of them haven’t struggled.
The people, as I have heard in the field, aspire to a government that is not made up of thieves.
Many young people want to leave, as well as those who have managed to take advantage of American generosity: capital flight is considerable.
Young people are tired of war and their ideologies.
They have lived through the modern world during their exiles in Iran or Pakistan and have appreciated its advantages.
With about 65% of its population under the age of 25, Kabul now has 5 millions, one-fifth of the total population.
In cities, state schools are full of both girls and boys.
It will be necessary to give work to those young people who will not want to return to the obscurantism of the old parties or to the corruption of certain leaders.
Everyone, including the armed opposition, is avid for mobile phones. Television and its Turkish leaflets featuring a modern world have big fans across the country.
The army is now present.
Will the authorities who want to run the country be considered legitimate?
Former anti-Soviet fighting commanders are already concerned with reconstituting the provincial militias, which will flee central power.
Afghanistan, a country of mountains, of strong local identities, should be able to benefit from a certain decentralization in the image of Western nations. However, the United States has wanted to transform it into a centralized state, with a strong presidential power, suppressing the position of the prime minister that existed since the Constitution of 1964.
President Karzai does not want foreign control, in particular on the occasion of elections scheduled in April 2014.
However, his country depended on foreign aid from the 50's and even earlier.
No industry has re-established its services, no reservoir has been fixed or important irrigation systems have been repaired.
Everything is imported and nothing is produced except fruits and vegetables.
Priority is left to private initiative.
In a ruined country after 30 years of war, there would have been a need for government control.
Rumor spread that Afghanistan possessed immense mining riches.
And that did nothing more than increase the feeling that Westerners were there to seize them.
Without energy to extract iron or copper ores from the land and no means of transport to export them through the mountains, there is no mining exploitation.
The Chinese have almost abandoned the copper mines of Mes Aynak, leaving international archaeologists (funded by the World Bank) to excavate the immense buddhic site and remain there as the most important employers in the province.
There will also be a need for Afghanistan and Pakistan, on which imports and exports broadly depend, to restore their normal relations.
French troops departed on November 20.
The new cooperation treaty provides for the continuation of traditional aid: girls' colleges, children's colleges, a French department at the university, the French Institute, cooperation in the military, legal, health and agricultural fields and support for the archaeological delegation.
Since 2009, in order to try to "earn hearts and spirits" and to carry out the impossible task of making aid and offensive actions coincide, a service of "civil and military actions" of the Ministry of Defense (Cimic), suppressed in 2012, brought and brought through a small French NGO numerous works of collective interest and agricultural rehabilitation actions in dozens of mountain towns.
These works, sweet to obtain local labor, have been able to contain the insurrection: irrigation, wells, drinking water, reforestation, fruit trees, sun protection and increase of cultivation areas.
What will we leave as a souvenir after two million euros of military expenditures?
A rather modest budget will contribute to the improvement of local living conditions, very harsh in those valleys often located more than 2,000 meters high.
The Embassy received dozens of lawsuits written with small agricultural proposals made by local communities in Kapisa province.
In order to free themselves from the insurrection carried out by foreign groups, something that farmers have told me that they want, it would be necessary to maintain a small civilian aid, well-controlled and that reaches them directly.
A Constitution with Forceps in Egypt
It's a new double or nothing from President Mohammed Morsi.
While Egypt is more divided than ever in terms of the constitutional declaration, which provisionally gives him full powers, he decided to play everything for the whole.
He caught everyone by surprise on Wednesday, when he announced that the Constituent Assembly would vote its final text the following day.
Just a week ago, the head of state had given two more months to the Assembly to finish its work.
And almost two years ago Egypt has been using a provisional text, several times modified, which weakens institutional stability and leads to judicial messes.
This new initiative has only increased the fracture in the country.
For his opponents, the president perseveres in his "autocratic delusion" and continues to "betray his word" and "tracing the right."
On behalf of his supporters, it ensures that this is a measure to end the institutional and political crisis faster and accelerate the transition process.
In the next 15 days a referendum should be organised.
A very short period of time that obliges the Brothers to renounce their plan to explain the text, article by article, to the Egyptians.
For the president, it is also a way to recover a popular and democratic legitimation while the controversy wreaks havoc throughout the country.
Mohammed Morsi seems convinced that the Egyptians will vote in his favor, as he stated in an interview with the American newspaper Time.
Considering that this recourse has been voted very quickly, it contains an ultimatum for the Egyptian people: "either they vote my text or I maintain the full powers", powers that will supposedly come to an end with the adoption of the Constitution.
It is in that strange environment in which 85 members of this Constituent Assembly, mostly Islamist, voted the text yesterday.
Most liberals did not come to the call.
In mid-November, a little before the constitutional declaration, they had closed the door because they felt that they could not assert their views.
Representatives of human rights, religious minorities and civil society did the same.
In order to achieve the necessary quorum, yesterday morning it was addeded to 11 people, substitute members, in a hurry.
Some of them are very close to the Muslim brothers.
As expected, the articles voted have been unanimously.
The commentators find it funny, on the other hand, that one of the few discrepancies of the day has been expressed practically at the time of prayer, so much so that several members of the committee came to think that the clock of the Constituentation was wrong.
The text, which was still being voted yesterday afternoon, consists of 234 articles.
Article 2, the object of all eyes, remains finally identical to that of the 1971 Constitution, which stipulated that “the principles of Sharia constitute the main source of law”.
The Salafist parties, for whom the establishment of Islamic law is a fundamental demand, hoped that "the principles" could be replaced by "the rules", which would have allowed a stricter application.
For the Islamists, that this article has not been modified is a guarantee of their goodwill and their respect for the other components of Egyptian society.
Liberals call it hypocrisy and consider that it is nothing more than an advertising ruse.
According to them, the Islamization of the Constitution is done through other articles.
They refer, in particular, to article 220, which confers on Al-Azhar University an advisory role, in particular for anyone who is to confirm their conformity with the laws of Sharia.
According to Egyptian specialist Sophie Pommier, it is disturbing because "the people called to pronounce themselves are not elected and have no democratic legitimacy.
Here we glimpse the premises of a theocracy.”
The fears of liberals are also heightened by the fact that the university’s next rector would be much less moderate than the current one.
"For now, there is no concrete religious implication.
With this Constitution we are within the framework of marital status.
Most jurists who have worked on that text are not ulemas but university students, some of them trained in the French system," explains Alexis Blouet, who prepares a thesis on the Egyptian constitutional transition.
But he recognizes that there may be some ambiguity around article 220, because the terms used mimic religious vocabulary.
"We refer in particular to the term iqh [Islamic jurisprudence, ndlr].
And the question that could arise in the future would be to what extent are the civilian judges competent to rule above all of this."
Beyond its religious character, the text voted yesterday has received many criticisms that refer to the extended powers it confers on the president of the Republic.
The Muslim Brotherhood will claim that there are few in comparison to what they were during the previous regime.
Another point of confliction: the powers conferred on the army.
According to the desire of the military, the revision of the defence budget will not be submitted to Parliament, but to a national defence council.
The prosecutions of civilians in military courts will also not be prohibited, as called for by associations for the defence of human rights.
They also showed their concerns regarding the text, which they consider liberticide.
The crime of blasphemy is maintained and, from now on, insults are prohibited, which could have serious consequences for freedom of expression, especially that of the press.
In addition, no article emphasizes the protection of women, emphasizes Heba Morayef of Human Rights Watch.
According to her, the only positive thing has been the prohibition of torture under article 36.
The word did not appear in the previous Constitution.
Although the Egyptian president was due to leave last night on television, demonstrations are scheduled for this noon.
Supporters of the head of state demonstrated on Saturday.
In Israel, Ukrainian tourists await the holy land, the navel of the world and a sea of brine
The Promised Land combines the majesty of biblical truths with modern comfort and pristine nature.
"AIF" identified the five most important reasons why Israel should be visited.
Reverence in the Holy Land
It is worth visiting the Jordan River where Jesus was baptized.
It is believed that anyone who enters this "baptismal stack" will receive divine grace.
And Galilee, the place of the miracles of Jesus: the conversion of water into wine at the wedding, walking on the waters, the appeasement of the storm, and the replete nets.
In this very place, Jesus appeared before his disciples after the resurrection.
But the greatest number of holy places are found in Jerusalem.
Believers roam here the Viacrucis or the Way of the Cross of Jesus (Latin "Vía Dolorosa").
It begins in the Fortress of Antonio, the Praetorium, where the trial was held, and continues through the streets of the Old City, goes to the Holy Sepulchre on Mount Gólgota, the place of the crucifixion, to the Anointing Stone and the burial place of Jesus.
In this same place there is also the symbolic Christian navel of the world, which symbolizes the salvation of humanity.
And the monastery of the Holy Cross in Jerusalem, built in the place where, according to a Christian legend, there was a tree from which the cross was made for the crucifixion of Jesus.
In Jerusalem there is also the holiest place for the Jews, the Wall of Lamentations; the remains of a temple destroyed by the Romans in 70 AD.
As it is tradition, people of different religions leave here notes with their desires, which will then come true.
Take a Vertical Trip
The ruins of the Masada Fortress was what remained of a secret refuge against the enemies, which Herod the Great ordered built in 25 B.C. for his family.
They are located on the mountain cliffs at a height of 450 m above sea level.
And getting there by their own means, only those who are engaged in escalating.
Others can reach this historic summit on the funicular.
And in the north of the country at an altitude of 1600-2040 m is the famous Hermón ski resort, where in the winter months there is a large influx of tourists.
Even there, from the foot of the hill, bus services are offered.
The total length of the ski slopes is 45 km.
According to an ancient legend, pagan gods supposedly lived at the top of the mountain.
Visiting Unique Museums
There are around 300 museums in this country.
In a single trip you will not be able to visit them all.
But it’s worth visiting at least five of them, the most interesting.
Among them is the Museum of Israel, located near the Knesset (Israeli Parliament).
It preserves the ancient Dead Sea Manuscripts or Qumran Scrolls, discovered in Judean desert caves, and around 500 thousand archaeological and anthropological pieces.
It's worth visiting the Art Museum in Tel Aviv.
In his exhibition of paintings he has canvases of Impressionists and expressionists such as Monet, Pissarro, Renoir, Cézanne, Sisley, Matisse, Modigliani, Chagall or Picasso.
In Acre you can visit the Al Basha museum-baño, which are some rooms of ancient Turkish baths with model figures of visitors and bathers of that time.
In Cesarea it is worth going through the exclusive Ralli private museum, where you can admire the sculptures of Rodin and Dalí.
There are no tour guides or souvenir shops there.
Entrance is free, and donations are not categorically accepted.
The fifth, the Holocaust Museum or Yad Vashem in Tel Aviv, showcases one of the most dramatic pages in history.
The most tragic section is the Children’s Memorial, built in memory of the 1.5 million children killed in concentration camps and gas chambers.
You go into it and find yourself in absolute darkness.
Stars are shining.
And the names of the Jewish children and the names of the countries in which they lost their lives sound.
Ukraine is also mentioned.
Health
Israel’s three main tourist areas are on the shores of the Mediterranean Sea, the Red Sea and the Dead Sea.
In each of them there are swimming pools, water parks, dolphinariums and oceanographic museums.
It is curious that in the Red Sea they are bathed even in the winter months, since the water temperature does not drop below 21 degrees and the air is heated up to 23 degrees.
The Dead Sea is even warmer and are bathed there throughout the year.
By the way, this sea, the most extraordinary in the world, is at the lowest point on the planet: at 417 m below sea level.
The blue waters there are a brine that they easily sustain even those who can't swim.
The surrounding landscapes are truly beautiful.
People come here to pass treatment courses with salt water, brine and healing mud and fortify their health in cases of dermatitis, allergies, asthma, eczema, arthritis, bronchitis, diabetes and to put their broken nerves in order.
Touching the Mysteries of Antiquity
They are preserved by the ancient part of Tel Aviv, the citadel of Jaffa, on the Mediterranean coast.
Through it passed the famous sea route linking Egypt, Syria, Anatolia and Mesopotamia.
The city is mentioned in ancient Greek and Egyptian legends.
According to legend, it was here that Noah built his ark and Perseus rescued the beautiful Andromeda with whom, in this same place, he led a long and happy life.
Tourists love walking through the narrow streets that bear the names of the Zodiac signs.
They say that if you touch the walls in the street of your "same" symbol, fortune will be favorable to you.
In Jaffa you can find newlywed couples who come from all over Israel, and even from other countries for photography.
And in Caesarea, the city of King Herod, you can walk through the Roman theater, "take" the fortress of the Crusaders.
In Roman times, Caesarea was the main city of Judea and the residence of the Roman procurators, including Pontius Pilate.
The carefully restored theatre is now used for evening concerts and opera shows.
Tourists remember
If you travel to Israel, don’t worry if your English leaves much to be desired: in this country about 30% of the population speaks Russian.
The money for this trip is not advisable to take it in euros, but in dollars, which can be easily changed to shéquels (currently 1-3.8 shéquels).
Urban transport consists mainly of the buses, but in Jerusalem there is fast tram and in Haifa is the only subway line in the country, which consists of six stations and connects the Lower City with the Upper City.
Actually, it's from an underground funicular.
Traveling on any type of public transport costs 6 shéquels and for this price you can tranship for 1.5 hours.
In Israel, according to Jewish tradition, the sabbat is celebrated on Saturdays.
From Friday night to the sunset on Saturday, markets and shops close and public transport interrupts their work.
But on Sunday morning a new working week begins.
In many cafes, restaurants and hotels only kosher food is served, which excludes pork, shellfish, flake-free fish, and dishes that combine milk and meat.
There is offered a wide selection of dishes based on beef and lamb, soups and desserts prepared with coconut milk, the traditional Hebrew cream called hummus, a great variety of sauces, the falafel, which are balls of ground chickpea, fruits and vegetables.
There are no stray dogs on the streets of Israeli cities.
Instead, there are many well-feded cats that “walk to their liking” imposingly.
At night you can see them sleeping even on the roofs of parked cars.
Purrers love crowded places and don’t reject treats.
Renting a car, depending on the brand, will cost from $37 (Hyundai Getz) to $188 (Audi A6, Volvo S80) per day.
In addition to the insurance, it comes out for $15 a day.
And the rental of a bicycle will come out for 15 shéquels a day.
Entrances to the museums cost an average of 30 shéquels.
Israel in figures
In 2012, more than three million tourists from around the world visited Israel.
Most of those who toured the country and rest were citizens of the U.S., Russia, France, Germany, Italy, the United Kingdom and Ukraine.
The Promised Land, from January to October 2012, was visited by 118.8 thousand Ukrainian tourists, an amount that exceeds 51% to the same index of 2010, before the cancellation of the visa regime on February 9, 2011.
"Big and powerful" will take you to Moscow: Immigrants Save for Language
While deputies and human rights defenders argue about the consequences that the law on the mandatory examination would bring, scammers have already appeared in the country who trade with "false" certificates
Every year, 13 million migrant workers arrive in Moscow, St. Petersburg and other Russian cities.
They are mostly citizens of Central Asian countries: Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and Turkmenistan.
Their only goal is to earn money to help their families who have stayed at home.
From 1 December, a law enters into force, according to which each working immigrant must take an examination to verify their knowledge of the Russian language.
For now, this rule applies only to those who intend to work in the service delivery sector, community service institutions, domestic services and retail.
However, over time, as promised in the Federal Immigration Service, exams will become mandatory for all immigrants.
In addition, it will not be enough to take the examination of the language, but also that of Russian history and the fundamentals of the legislation.
Knowledge of the language must even be confirmed to receive and renew the work permit.
Exceptions are citizens of countries where Russian is the official language.
Persons who managed to obtain their education certificates and diplomas prior to the dissolution of the Soviet Union in 1991 will not be subject to this law.
Utility, fatality and defence of rights
The network of examination centers will be sponsored by the Pushkin Institute of Russian Language, People’s Friendship University, Moscow State University, St. Petersburg State University and other Russian higher education institutions.
Immigrants will be able to run for tests in all cities, right now in Russia more than 160 such centers are open.
The initiative on the introduction of the examination was supported by deputies of the State Duma and the Federal Immigration Service.
But rights advocates, long before the law came into force, many times managed to ask this question to the press: And what will this really contribute?
Which changes will bring the obligatory knowledge of the language, both for the Russians and the immigrants themselves?
In the first place, the representatives of the Immigration Service respond, this will reduce the number of people who suffer "labor slavery".
Many people talk today about defending the rights of labour migrants, explains Victor Sebelev, head of the representation of the Russian Federal Immigration Service in Tajikistan.
Defending your rights must begin before your departure.
Only an organized selection system will solve the problem of immigrant workers in 90% of cases.
Immigrant problems are without profession, education, those who do not know the language or pass medical control.
Without the understanding of the language, Sebelev says, the future immigrant will be doomed to find unscrupulous people who, under the pretext of helping, put a "paid jacket" to a creepy and narrow covache, where he and dozens of others like him are consumed for months without food or documents, working 12 to 14 hours a day.
We received countless complaints from our immigrants.
"In their homeland they promise them one thing, but when they arrive they are deceived, they are deprived of their passports, they do not pay them the promised salaries," confirms the head of the Labor Immigration Directorate of the Tajikistan Immigration Service, Tolib Sharipov.
Don't be angry, boss!
However, in the meantime many citizens of the Central Asian republics who wish to work in Russia recognize that not only do they not know the language of the country to which they intend to go, but also in their own language they write with difficulty.
Of course, this is not so much his guilt but his own misfortune: there are few turkmenos, Uzbeks, Tajiks who can afford to receive normal primary education.
This is because their families even have nothing to feed the children, not to mention dressing them, wearing them and buying them what is necessary for the school.
Arriving in adolescence, these boys venture to work at the first opportunity.
It's difficult, if you don't know the language, they recognize.
"You're feeling humiliated and inferior."
However, with regard to language law, rights defenders point out an important point.
Exams will be conducted only for those immigrants whose status is legal.
If they don’t have that status, there will be no examination and, consequently, no official work.
Meanwhile, the majority of immigrant workers continue to live in Russia illegally.
"Welcome or forbidden the passage to strangers"
Many visitors claim that receiving official status in our country is not so easy.
The cause lies in bureaucratic obstacles and the difficulties of the language itself.
In addition, legalization costs money: twelve thousand to sixteen thousand rubles.
However, a false inscription can be made quickly and for only a thousand five hundred rubles.
Employees of the Russian police know that we usually carry false documents, we are not registered and extortion cases appear.
"We're asked by a hundred or two hundred rubles for cigarettes or tea," Tajikistan citizen Umed Hushkadamov told reporters.
"Come on, don't be stingy, buy a painting."
On the first day of the entry into force of the language law, it became clear that not only immigrant registration documents can be false.
Several "false" certificates of knowledge of language have already been seized by Federal Immigration Service officials.
Counterfeited documents had been printed on standard color printers.
Of course, they came into the hands of the new owners not as a gift: each immigrant, wanting to facilitate the task of passing the tests, paid for the document seven thousand rubles.
This is two and a half times more expensive than the official examination procedure, which will cost the immigrant worker three thousand rubles.
The main task for the near future is to protect the anti-corruption system so that “diplomas” cannot be bought, government officials and rights defenders converge.
So far, the authorities can promise immigrant workers who would not have been able to successfully pass the exam the first time longer to study the basic language course.
In addition, non-Russian-speaking immigrants will be offered jobs that don’t require active communication with people.
The Ministry of the Interior does not take drastic measures against the illegal arms trade
The percentage of crimes committed with registered weapons is quite small
The Ministry of the Interior proposes to implement the law with greater rigidity in relation to owners of weapons for civilian use.
This is the government’s reaction to the recent incidents: to fire gunfire during wedding celebrations in which fortunately there were no victims, and the massacre initiated by Moscow lawyer Dimitri Vinográdov, in which to squeeze seven people lost their lives.
The police want to prohibit the use and possession of traumatic weapons in public places and to raise the minimum age to obtain a license to carry weapons, from 18 years to 21 years.
This idea was supported by the head of the Duma’s committee on security and anti-corruption, Irina Yarovaya, who promised that reforms to the gun possession law will be brought to the State Duma in the near future.
Not everyone is happy with the fact that the Russian authorities are trying to counteract this problem by applying the "hard-handed policy".
An open letter has appeared in the network, whose authors, representatives of various social organizations of shooters, demand that such measures of "meaninglessness".
The percentage of vandalism with the use of registered weapons is minimal, criminal lawyer Vasiliy Lésnikov told the BBC news service.
According to statistics from the Ministry of the Interior, during six months in 2012, 142 crimes with the use of firearms were committed registered in organs of the Interior, compared to the total of 1168 crimes registered for the same period.
The perpetrators of the open letter are confident that stricter legislation in the civilian arms sector will not be an inconvenience for the offender to address the black market.
Now, according to his words, in Moscow you can find any weapon at a low price.
Meanwhile, the Ministry of the Interior states that the situation of the proliferation of illegal weapons is under control.
Providers: from factories to officials
The black arms market is supplied through various channels.
The main channels are years, says retired Colonel Victor Baranets, who for ten years worked in the Ministry of Education and in the Upper General Staff.
Screenshot of the website that accepts weapons orders
The first, "vestige of the army or military", that is, the weapons that were stolen during military actions in the Caucasus.
“The weapons were stolen by the Russian officers and by the Caucasians themselves,” Baranets says.
The next kind of "black weapon", those stolen by criminals and representatives of the security forces.
Baranets explains that this includes stolen weapons from police warehouses and those stolen directly by law enforcement officials.
Illegable weapons go on sale from military arsenals.
In many military arsenals there were often explosions.
"There are proven versions that some fires were premeditated to cover the flaw," says a former soldier.
According to Baranets, gun manufacturers also lay their grain of sand.
“Private arms factories have now proliferated that are not competitive in the international market and distribute rope weapons to the black market, including that of Moscow,” he said.
Another source of the black market is smuggling.
Lots of guns and machine guns come from poor countries like Kyrgyzstan.
"There they have their own production, sometimes craftsmanship, and a mafia has been formed that has organized the flow of weapons," explains the former soldier.
Where's the weapons coming from?
Experts have calculated the approximate involvement of each of these sources of supply of illegal weapons to the black market.
A report on this topic was prepared in 2011 by the Centre for Problem Analysis and Public Management Planning.
Specialists analyzed reports from the Ministry of the Interior and Rosstat, criminological bibliography and publicly accessible data of portals whose theme revolves around weapons.
The vast majority of illegal weapons, according to investigators, come from the armed forces and security forces.
Half of the black market’s weapons have arrived there “because of officials whose work is related to guns,” the report said.
According to investigators, 17% of weapons come from places where there are armed conflicts, 14% of cases are stolen during the production of weapons and 5% correspond to “illegal archaeology”.
The dependent of one of the weapons stores, who asked that his last name not be mentioned, says that the weapons found by illegal bulldozers have not been bought by anyone for a long time because they are too old.
More often, he says, suppliers head to military warehouses when they need a shipment.
Through some ensign, the supplier purchases a unit, for example a TT pistol.
They remove it from the record and pass it over the fence.
"It takes the gun to the city and sells it at 900 euros per unit with two chargers," he says
"But, of course, the police are aware of all matters, so periodically, when the crime discovery plan has to be fulfilled, they make test purchases from illegal gun merchants."
"Like in a luxury boutique"
The seller and the buyer are usually found through acquaintances.
"I visited websites and blogs until a guy who asked me to go to the "Begovaya" platform, where there would be a man who would take me to an "esquinita" where we could negotiate.
From the price of the gun, I only found out there."
Military commentator Viktor Baranets
“To get a gun I need a friend who has contacts,” says the shop assistant. “I have a friend like that, but I don’t trust him much.”
There are vendors in the markets, but there you also have to arrive, let’s say “from Mr. Ivanovih, who asked him to tell him that his daughter’s tooth had fallen out.”
I can even get a pair of steel mittens through a trusted person.
He also supplies them only to me because he knows I won't give him away.
Rookies are looking for weapons in different ways.
Former soldier Viktor Baranets was tested in the role of buyer of illegal weapons in the mid-1990’s, when he was preparing to publish an article on this issue.
Schemas, in his words, haven’t changed.
They gave him an album of photos where there was "not everything".
“I felt like in a luxury boutique,” recalls the soldier.
To the buyer, according to Baranets, they do not offer cat per hare: everything there can be tried.
I, as a potential customer, do not just buy; we go with the seller to the forest, where we put a target.
"They give me the opportunity to shoot and when I make sure that the gun fires well, we start negotiating," the expert describes the procedure.
Sofa tent
Web search engines lead to “Vkontakte” sites and groups, where it’s offered to buy weapons “for different purposes.”
This does not require any personal documents or encounters.
“It’s enough to have a certain amount of money,” they write in the headline “Buy a pistol or machine gun.”
There users place their orders and ask questions.
And they sell to minors?
"Without permission, of course", asks the user "Iván" (conserved spelling).
"Buy TT. Moscow gun", laconically asks his question "Fedorenkov".
The Federal Security Service has created a huge network of fictitious websites and two-handed capturing those who want to buy firearms.
And the people go, like starving fish towards the bait, and then, to extract coal in Siberia
Military and ex-military commentator Viktor Baranets
I’ve heard of these systems: Usually, the website is registered outside the scope of Russian laws.
People make the orders.
Buyers make the payment through an ATM.
"In response, they send him a photo and instructions to let him know where the weapon is hidden," recounts the press secretary of the NGO "The Right to Arms," Dmitri Kislov.
Viktor Baranets claims that if an order is placed online, you run out of a gun and put it in jail.
The Federal Security Service has created a huge network of fictitious websites and two-handed capturing those who want to buy firearms.
"And the people go, like hungry fish towards the bait, and then, to extract coal in Siberia," he says.
Makarov for 100 dollars
In the purchase of illegal firearms, according to experts, one can spend 100 to 900 dollars.
According to Dmitri Kislov of the NGO "The Right to Arms", a Makarov gun can be purchased for 100-300 dollars.
You'll have to wait a month and a half.
It's sent from military warehouses by mid-ranking chiefs.
According to the official statistics of the ministry, compared to the January-October 2011 period, the total number of these crimes in Russia decreased by 7%, which constitutes 22,900, and the number of identified cases of theft and extortion of weapons, ammunition, explosives and explosive devices decreased by 7.8%.
Employees of fast-food restaurants and supermarkets in the U.S
Up to a quarter of U.S. teenagers find their first jobs as cashiers at "McDonald's"
In recent days, in the U.S. there has been a wave of anti-low-wage protests that are paided in supermarkets of the Walmart corporation and restaurants in popular fast-food chains, such as "McDonald's", "Burger King", "Taco Bell", "Wendy's" and "Kentucky Fried Chicken".
For now no one dares to assert whether or not this wave will arrive at nine points, or if it is destined to lose strength and dawn in its early stages.
Protesting actions are supported by trade unions and a number of left-wing NGOs.
In addition to the increase in modest wages levied by Walmart workers and those in the fast-food system, the goal of protest organizers is the creation of workers’ unions in these companies.
For now, this sector of the economy has practically not been occupied by the trade union movement.
Forty-six cents a year?
Actions began last week, after Thanksgiving, the so-called "Black Friday". That day, in the U.S
That day, some workers of the Walmart corporation, which employs 2.2 million people in the world, abandoned their jobs and formed protest pickets alongside unions and left-wing activists at the corporation’s stores, whose products are aimed at people of modest and moderate income.
Walmart sells everything from diapers, hunting rifles and car batteries, to vacuum cleaners, eggs and milk.
The products in its stores are on average between 8% and 27% cheaper than those of larger supermarkets.
That’s why a lot of Walmart’s underpaid workers buy only at work.
The availability and assortment of products have made Walmart one of America’s largest corporations.
According to two critics, Walmart can afford to sell products at a very low price, in part because it pays little to its workers.
The latter also complain about poor working conditions, such as lack of forklifts or handheld scanners.
That "Black Friday" the participants of the rally demanded the increase in wages and complained that the cost of health insurance provided by the corporation went from 30 to 100 dollars per month.
For a typical Walmart worker, who receives 9.5 dollars per hour, this spending is excessive, they said.
Scientists at the University of California at Berkeley argue that if Walmart raises the median salary to $12 per hour, this will cost the corporation 3.2 trillion dollars.
This is about 1.1% more than you spend on wages now.
If Walmart places the wage increase on consumers’ shoulders, each store visit will result in just 46 more expensive cents.
In a year they’ll spend the ridiculous 12.39 dollars more than they spend now.
Walmath supporters satisfied that the protest took place only in nine states and did not cause any loss to the corporation.
"Black Friday" lasted in its stores from 8 p.m. Thursday until midnight the next day and, during that time, Walmart sold about 5000 items per second.
In total, its cash registers carried out "Black Friday" almost 100 million transactions.
The company’s representative, Dan Fogelman, stated in an interview with the left-wing Huffington Post website that they left their job “less than five” Walmart employees and that the protest had been nothing more than “another public relations stunt,” organized by the workers union.
"Free box!"
The protests continued this week in New York, in which the objectives were not precisely Walmart department stores (which in this progressive city do not appreciate them, and that’s why for now there are none), but the “McDonald’s” and other cheap restaurants.
McDonald’s says it sells billions of servings, and then doesn’t even grant you sick leave and don’t get paid for your honest work!
Jumaane Williams, New York City Council member
Currently, according to federal law and that of New York, the minimum wage is $7.25 per hour.
Fast-food restaurants increase it over time, but only slightly, and on average, ordinary workers receive 8.90 dollars per hour in New York.
No one earns less than them in this city.
How it’s possible to survive in New York with that money, for me it’s incomprehensible.
There was a time when almost a quarter of American teenagers went through McDonald’s and worked there, behind the counters, part-time after school, although they were still living with their parents.
Few were those who saw this as the main source of income or planned to stay there for a long time.
Now, I constantly stumble upon some interviews with McDonald’s workers who complain that they have to live on this salary, and sometimes even feed their children.
On the other hand, in a forum of the Wall Street Journal there is a comment whose author points out that it is irresponsible to have children if he does not know what to feed them.
Participants in the protests, which began at 6:30 a.m
They also demanded that in the fast-food sector, workers’ unions be created.
U.S
On the other hand, management rarely makes their lives easier.
But, for objective reasons, it’s easy for unions to cover the fast food sector.
One of the main ones is the excessive turnover of the staff.
Discordants
Noisy protests took place that same day at other cheap restaurants in Manhattan.
The culmination of the action took place halfway at McDonald's near Times Square, involving several local Democratic politicians, notably City City Council member Jumaane Williams, who said: "McDonald's says it's selling billions of portions, and then doesn't even give you a hospital or pay for your honest work."
The protesters received the support of other prominent New York Democrats, for example city mayor candidate Bill de Blasio, who said that "we must support fast-food workers so that they can receive fair wages and the economic prosperity that every New Yorker deserves."
As described by the New York Times, this has been the largest such manifestation in American fast food history.
But it involved only a few hundred people, even not all of them were employees of the fast food service, in which tens of thousands of people from New York worked.
It is not yet clear whether this will serve as a spark to provoke a massive movement.
"For now, deceiving the brain is not so easy"
The issue of augmented reality, so popular among fans of modern technologies, is lately perceived mainly through the prism of special glasses.
At the beginning of the summer, at its annual conference Google demonstrated a moving model and subsequently, in November, it emerged that Microsoft had also filed an application to claim the corresponding patent.
However, from a conversation with the head of the interactive 3D technology group in Microsoft’s Cambridge lab, Izadi Sharamom, it was elucidated that for the company’s scientists, the glasses already belonged to a past stage.
They are attracted by the perspective of manipulating virtual objects in the air with bare hands and creating open virtual spaces.
- In general terms, what's the subject of study of your research group?
- We are dedicated to person-computer interaction, but we also try to extend the limits of this interaction.
For now humanity in its great mass has been trapped in the stage of working with the pixels of the flat screen, sometimes it touches it with the finger.
But we want to look 5-10 years ahead and guess the cardinal changes in this interaction.
For example, the Xbox console and Kinect sensors represent a step forward and nowadays no Xbox sells without Kinect, as they are all interested in gesture control.
- What else does the future hold?
- Even though Kinect has taken interaction to the physical level, much of it still occurs on a flat screen, sometimes in 3D.
The introduction of information has improved (the system receives more data), and the output is still not very good.
We’re trying to change this, we’re working on truly three-dimensional visualization systems, based on different technologies, including design.
The world must be freed from our computer science, the physique, make it more tangible.
For this, however, it is necessary to recognize not only the user, but also the surrounding space.
Then we will be able to complete the real world with virtual objects in a more comfortable way.
First of all, to hell with those silly virtual reality helmets!
- What do you think about voice control?
A very popular detail, but don't you think they overestimate it?
- Of course you can't be considered a panacea, you're waiting to solve the issue of privacy, since you don't always want others to know about our actions and intentions.
In fact, all types of computer interaction are good, but each in their own field.
For example, for the control of devices in public places, we had a project in which we thought of gestures, but not in loose movements, but shorter.
In addition, the gestures were captured not by the camera of the device, but by a bracelet that recorded the movement of bones and muscles.
For now it is very large, but in theory it can be reduced to the size of a wristwatch.
But, in general, the future is for a combined type of control, e.g. gesture + voice.
- How's that?
For example, how would you ask me to pass this can of soda to you?
You'll talk and show it at the same time.
- I'm usually just talking.
- Oh, that's gonna be really hard to recognize.
- I mean, you want to force users to adapt to what you can or can't do at the moment?
- Not necessarily, but it's a mutual rapprochement.
I think that in the near future we will have to work mainly on the design of new types of sensors that can accurately determine the person’s reactions.
These can be, for example, laser sensors, as they provide good in-depth resolution, which is very important.
- If we're talking about your work with Xbox Kinect sensors, what complaints do you have about modern cameras?
Are you lacking resolution, depth or something else?
- In principle, the current generation is what can be the beginning of our work with three-dimensional recognition.
Of course, it would be good to get 8 megapixels at the speed of 1000 fps.
But the issue is not in the megapixels, but in the quality of the matrix and in depth.
From this last point of view, all current technologies aren’t good enough for us; this gives more work to algorithm designers.
In other words, we have to remember not only the resolution in the X and Y axes, but also in Z.
Also important is the speed, the number of frames per second.
Human movements are quite dynamic and today's 30 fps are truly scarce, especially for gestures.
Steven Bathiche, from our lab in Redmond, made an adjustable-delay contact sensor from 1 to 100 ms and it is worth mentioning that the modern sensors produced in series are closer to the second value (60-100).
Not everyone understands to what extent this affects human-machine interaction.
It would be very useful for my job an equivalent of such a device, but that does not require contact so that the frames per second are more.
- And you don't have to increase the number of cameras?
- The Kinect now has three "cameras", one of which is in fact the infrared ray emitter and the second is the reflected signal receiver.
But the third camera is actually a simple visible tuning fork sensor.
It is not used to determine the depth of the object.
Potentially, more cameras could solve the problem...
On the contrary, increase it and increase the required number of calculations.
It would be good to create a flexible Kinect analogue, play with the location curvature of the cameras and see how this would help determine the three-dimensional position.
- As far as I remember, Microsoft, unlike Google, hasn't presented its glasses to the public.
Don’t you think that, from the point of view of the daily use of augmented reality technologies, glasses are one of the most visible platforms?
- Of course, spending all your time holding up a smartphone in your hands, it's not very comfortable, but I think: the best option would be a "transmutable" augmented reality when, based on a cloud platform, you could go from glasses to smartphone,
Glasses are very personal devices, that’s their strength (private things only you will be able to see them), but also their weakness: an augmented reality based on glasses will not allow you to work on virtual objects along with other people.
Let’s imagine for a moment that manipulating virtual holographic objects into the air is available not only to Tony Stark from the movie “Iron Man”, but also to any simple mortal.
There’s a problem in this idea that critics often point out: there’s no tactile response!
Hands don't feel anything!
What answers does your group prepare for this challenge?
- In my lectures I often say that augmented reality is the seventh irruption in the interaction between man and machine.
I think the octave might well be the addition of tactile sensations.
In the meantime, one of the most interesting tricks is the use of second-hand in support quality for the image.
Record the touch perfectly!
But there are also technologies that are truly aimed at bringing tangibility to these "images in the air". For example, the interference of several ultrasonic rays aimed at a specific point where the fingertip is located gives a feeling similar to what one feels if someone was blowing it in the fingertips, but that feeling is still very weak.
There are also wrist bracelets that influence the nerve endings of the fingers, this also a promising direction.
- Have you tried to deceive the brain?
Make you think that he feels what it looks like he should feel the moment he sees something?
- That's a good idea, we haven't tried yet.
Here lies another challenge with which we will have to fight for a long time, how to make a person physically located in a very small space thinks that it goes to an open space, almost endless. We are also working on the idea of treadmills (not like those of gyms), mobile platforms, as well as that of giant balloons.
For now, deceiving the brain isn’t that easy, you still have to work for many years.
That’s what makes working with virtual reality appealing to researchers, that many things are in a state of total conception.
Taste personal subjective rather than culture - Rosbalt.ru
"Rosbalt" continues the project "Vanguardism of St. Petersburg", dedicated to the citizens in front, at the forefront of art and culture.
In this list of the best are brilliant figures of art and the St. Petersburg scene, whose achievements reach beyond the city: they often find recognition in Europe without Russia even knowing them.
"Rosbalt"'s new hero is quirky artist Kirill Miller.
The whole city meets Kirill Miller, a bearded man dressed in red from top to bottom, which can be found near the Russian Museum, near the Summer Garden or in fashion meetings and inaugurations.
Kirill Miller's paintings always attract crowds, wherever they're displayed.
Kirill Miller is one of those purely sociophilosophical storytellers from St. Petersburg, creators of new mythologies.
Kirill Miller is a brilliant representative of St. Petersburg avant-garde of the late 80’s – early 90’s.
In addition, he is an urban man, whose appearance in the streets of the city awakens in the inhabitants smiles and raises the mood to everyone.
Recently, he took in his hands an organillo and became the organillero of St. Petersburg, since he has matured to make such a difficult role, for his bohemian existence, his philosophy and his image.
- Kirill, why are you going around the city dressed in red and not yellow or turquoise, for example?
- I chose the color red because I'm a fashion designer and I dedicate myself to appearance and image.
In this world, the red color is a compromise between the artist, the creators of images and society.
Although in society, everything that is not a little gray arouses aggressiveness and agitation of ungood properties.
But my provocations are aimed at the start of a conversation.
The whole story of my provocative actions is an invitation to debate.
- When did you understand that you had to be an artist?
- In an exhibition of the Nevsky Palace of Culture, where my works were exhibited.
I realized that that was my path.
Then the wave of old unofficial artists was over, and the new, free like me, were still misunderstood.
I became friends with artists of the new wave, with those after those exposed in Gaza and Nevskiy, which led to "Pushkinskaya-10", and the new wave is over.
I immersed myself in the theater for the clothes, the music of all genres, but not for the lyrics.
- And all of this came together in your Art Clinic... - For me it was important to find myself in the cultural center of St. Petersburg, where the best creative minds should be gathered.
In 1995, I occupied territory in Pushkinskaya-10 and there, while there were no reforms, there was a musical artistic club, a bohemian club, the house of bohemia in St. Petersburg.
Many were born there: Noms, Tequila Jazz, I remember how Schnur brought "Van Gogh's Ear" to the project.
Schnur and his friends sang light songs under phonogram, wearing tight pants, among them was currently fashion composer Igor Vdovin.
When the group started playing live, it became "Leningrated."
Trachtenberg was an animator of many programs before Hali-galivskih.
We told Trachtenberg and it was the beginning of her great career; basic education and training received her from us.
Gallery "D 137", "Club Griboyedov, are the remains of the Art Clinic.
Our employees and assiduous customers went there.
I'm a hero of the last century, when culture meant something.
In 2000, the survey was done in the press, the award was "The people of our city".
In the nomination "Artist of the Year" I was chosen, so my culmination ended.
In these new times it is not comfortable to work; I am a man who likes the truth, honesty and culture of the last century.
In our times it is easy to become popular, but culture and popularity are separate. One can be popular but very unworn.
- There's something recognizable in your work.
- Many of my paintings are hits, they clearly express the relevance and the occurrence.
I'll make a programmed exhibition, "The Russian Museum in clowns."
Clowns are a timeless category.
I used to be sociable, but now being like this is painful and terrifying.
But in the clowns everything is smeared and the tragedy is eliminated.
I love the grotesque, I have grotesque ideas.
For example, the salvation of the world through a totalitary and obligatory change of clothes.
Now one is followed by clothes, orientating on the outside, but not inside the person.
And, maybe that person shouldn’t even shake his hand, but spit in his face.
Deceit will be eliminated when you change clothes.
- We recently saw you in the role of urban organillero. - A cultured city must have such a hero.
Who better than me to be!
- All right, but maybe commercial art can be beautiful?
Now commercial art must be scrupulous, sweet, tactile.
We are witnessing the disintegration of cultures.
Before the people were grouped in herds, the bohemians wanted one thing, the simplones others.
Now they've all split into microcommunities, it's hard to please everyone.
I'm not a hundred-dollar bill to please everyone.
Now you have to think about who you want to please.
Now, every cult hero has their 100 fans.
- And to hear Stas Mikhailov, they're coming for thousands!
- They're going to see them abandoned, there the sex-social sphere works.
And to a cultural event there are 300 people, 10 thousand will never come. As a result, less management, less money, everything withers.
I've got my fans, the most important thing is not to deceive them, not spoil what you've achieved.
When he was young he made such paintings that a collector had them hanging on the wall next to Falk and Larionov.
I started with paintings, with which people usually end up.
Now they often confuse concepts.
They say spiritual cultury, consumer culture.
There’s no culture in consumption, that’s another story.
I am a man of yesterday’s culture, I grew up under the example of artists who lived in poverty and died in poverty, they refused money just to paint pictures.
I am in favour of this culture.
- Kirill, what's missing from St. Petersburg?
Good experts in culture.
There’s one concept: culture officer.
But not all of them can devote themselves to culture.
When the kings were right everything was different, the kings perhaps didn’t understand the culture very well, but they knew it was necessary to keep the right experts.
There are very good consultants in Moscow now.
We have people in St. Petersburg who could well be experts, but they are closed, because experts at a higher level are needed to properly evaluate them and make their way.
Now subjective personal taste is thriving.
Erarta the same thing, but the difference is that they honestly say that not all modern art is accepted, there are some artists who have to look for other museums.
- What's St. Petersburg for you?
- St. Petersburg is not a cultural capital. In Moscow there is much more cultury, there is land there.
But on our stones it is difficult for art to grow.
We need cultural terrain and we now have more writers than readers, this is wrong.
In Europe there are a lot of people with curiosity who go to exhibitions and concerts.
In our case, this class is tiny.
We have to make art fashionable, as it was at the beginning of the last century.
The project will be implemented with a grant from St. Petersburg.
Giving birth in space
The earth is in danger.
Global warm-up or collision with a killer asteroid.
Caravans of spacecraft with humans on board depart in search of a spare planet.
To conserve humanity, the main question is asked: How to continue the species in conditions of weightlessness or on the spare planet itself?
I think there's little to choose from.
In fact, only two are true planets that can be colonized, at least hypothetically.
"These are Venus and Mars," says State Astronomical Institute Chief Astronomical Researcher Sternberg P.K. (IAES), Vladimir Surdin.
But if conditions on Mars are better for life, on the surface of Venus the temperature is 500 degrees.
You can only live at high altitudes or in the orbit of Venus... in space.
We began to address the problems of reproduction in space with the flora.
Experiments with plants have been carried out for half a century.
Four generations of peas grown in orbit were not differentiated in anything from terrestrial varieties.
Then came the breeding of insects in orbit, small fruit flies, drosophiles.
In 1979 quail eggs were sent into the space to see how the embryo develops in conditions of weightlessness.
We get a normal chick as a result.
But then the problems begin.
"The problem is that this chick has to find support, it has to stay on its own feet and start moving," explains Vladimir Sychev, director of the lab of the Institute of Biomedical Problems (IPBM) of the Russian Academy of Sciences.
Finding no support, the chicks randomly flipped.
And after 10 hours in the newborns there was a total atrophy of the instincts.
Chicks didn’t react to light or sound.
And the misfortune was that after four days they simply died.
"And twice we got the chicks born, but then we left it because it was impossible to work there with them," Vladimir Sychev says of the experiment with chicks in space.
The last time a biological "miniarch" flew into orbit was 16 years ago.
Experiments will continue in the spring of 2013.
However, only same-sex species will travel in the "Bion" biosatellite.
There was an experiment with rats, who were sent into space already with fetuses.
In principle, nothing supernatural was found.
"This was precisely in biosatellites, but this was a unique experiment, and studies like this must be carried out," Vladimir Sychev says.
After landing, the space rats had offspring.
However, it has not yever been possible to address the problem of reproduction directly in space.
It's a tough challenge.
Animals, by losing their usual habitat, simply can’t realize their sexual instincts.
Man, however, unlike small animals, in principle, can.
Homo sapiens has abstract reasoning and is able to create an appropriate emotional background.
Similar experiments are not carried out for ethical reasons.
But women have been traveling to space for 50 years now.
Tereshkova had the greatest risk.
And the most precious thing that humanity has is the female organism.
Our seagull flying, and no one on Earth could say: if it is after a flight to space.
You may or may not have a baby after this flight.
"After all, to this question no one has answered," says aerospace veteran Vakhtang Vachnadze.
In June 1964, only after one year of flight into space, the first female cosmonaut, Valentina Tereshkova, gave birth to a daughter.
The girl's father, Andrian Nikolayev, was also cosmonaut.
In 1988, the second cosmonaut, Svetlana Savitskaya, who was twice in orbit and even worked in open space, had a son.
However, the risk persists.
There are few, astronauts are numbered who after long flights have found everything well, and a healthy generation is growing.
"And if you can say more, it's dangerous even on orbital flights," summarizes the cosmonaut pilot, hero of the Soviet Union, hero of Russia, Valery Polyakov.
However, humanity must seek some new avenues in biotechnology, in radiological protection, in the creation of artificial gravity.
The hydrolaboratory of the Cosmonaut Preparation Center is a mandatory stage in the preparation for flight.
Under zero buoyancy conditions, cosmonauts practice their working skills in space.
Water simulates weightlessness.
If for an adult the water is a comfortable environment, although still hostile, for a newborn child it is their native element.
The small men-amphibian seem to confirm that life on earth arose from the ocean.
There is a connection between the fact that the baby is approximately 9 months inside his mother in the amniotic fluid, as it will be easier for him to get used to the water.
In principle, this is logical, because from the moment of birth to the moment of the first bath only 2 weeks pass.
“This is too little to forget something,” says Marina Aksenova, newborn swimming monitor.
In short, if weightlessness is a more natural state for a newborn, for women gravitation, the force of gravity is necessary.
In weightlessness the abdominal and pelvic muscles are decrepitated very quickly, the ejection capacity of the fetus is reduced.
Well, we omit that, relying on stimulants for childbirth.
You can, of course, give birth in a special camera, it will push you.
"And then what?" Valeri Polyakov asks this original question.
On the other hand, the child also needs artificial gravity.
Without a sense of gravity, the body does not generate the skeleton or the muscle system.
To a newborn in orbit it is impossible to wear it with a special suit for workouts, such as adults.
He will simply be deprived of what is vital to him.
"And this experiment that we're going to do together, by allowing the birth of a child in a strange environment, will result in us bringing to Earth a disabled person, a totally misfited person," said Igor Pestov, head of the bioethics committee of the Institute of Biomedical Problems at the Russian Academy of Sciences.
For now, the birth of children in space is a theoretical issue.
However, over time this will be a reality, when humans in their ships head to a distant planet that will be home to their space-born children.
NKÚ chief: The inspection of the "švarc" system has not found the culprit politician
With regard to public awards, the Czech Republic has good inspectors and quality legislation, even if its application limps.
Miloslav Kala, vice president of the Office of Supreme Control (NKÚ) in an interview with Aktuálně.cz
"The law will never be perfect", but its application must be correct. "This is, in my opinion, what we lack", thus describes the current situation.
Joint checkpoints by Czech and German inspectors also come to similar conclusions.
As an example of an incorrect practice, he mentions Petr Nečas' attitude towards the system of being autonomous only on paper, which is known as the "švarc" system.
The prime minister recently said that the SDD (Civic Democratic Party) would not bother businessmen with inspectors. So is it prohibited or allowed?
"The law must be regulated in some way and if it prohibits something, in that case, neither the president of the government can prevent the performance of the office that has the obligation to carry out such checks or demand obligations," Kala said.
In the meeting of the control commission in the Congress of Deputies, you spoke of the common project between the Czech and German team, within the framework of which the legislation relating to public awards in both countries was compared.
What exactly is it?
It is a parallel control that we began to carry out about two years ago.
Put in a simplified way: European legislation regularizes how public awards should be organised, this is followed by the legislation in force in each country and, later, the practice itself.
We have united everything and, although the control has not yet ended, we have obtained very interesting differences. Generally, the legislation of our country is "stricter and more complete", however, the concrete praxis in some parameters is better in Germany.
It is therefore confirmed that it is not enough to incorporate increasingly strict rules, but that it is necessary to concentrate on law enforcement itself.
What can this project actually bring? What do you think are the conclusions?
Precisely a common control of this type could contribute to us to stop investing efforts in the realization of the laws, the reduction and the improvement of limits, although this does not have such a positive impact.
The economy of savings means getting an adequate thing at a reasonable price (which doesn’t always mean that the price is the lowest) to avoid enrichment and, in the long run, criminal actions.
But not for the fact of reducing the limit of the awards means that we are going to achieve it.
Perhaps because the system is saturated with so much "paping", that those who want to seek failures in the system will be able to do it more easily than if the limits remained as high as now.
You talk about the problems of our country with the transfer of the legislation that regulates the practice of public awards.
How does the control system work in Germany?
Is an office similar to our NKÚ or organized differently?
As for the offices, like the NKÚ, there the Bundesrechnungshof (Federal Court of Accounts) acts, with an organization similar to ours, with a school too, the only thing is that in its case it is structured in a somewhat different way, but in principle both offices evolve in parallel.
The competencies are also to some extent analogous, however, while Germany has a federal organization, the courts of accounts meet even at this level. In this aspect it differs slightly from our system.
The Federal Court of Auditors (BRH) can only control federal money, which we call state funds here.
Public funds, which are administered by the regions and municipalities, there, in Germany, are controlled in the Federal Court of Auditors.
In terms of local legislation, is it simpler than ours?
Generally, I wouldn’t want to set a comparison without concrete data. However, Germany is still a model in some respects, but you can’t pinpoint exactly on what’s better.
Could it be better, for example, in terms of the quality of inspectors?
That's surely not, but, I repeat, I don't like to make this kind of comparison.
It must be said that even those countries we take as a model must face a large number of problems.
If it weren’t, it wouldn’t help them collaborate with our office, don’t you think?
With regard to our country’s legislation, what did it mean for its Office the amendment of the law on public awards? Is its incidence manifested in any way?
Bearing in mind that the period of validity of this amendment is relatively short, it has not yet been stated in control proceedings.
Since we carry out ex-post checks, a certain delay is required.
For now, it is observed in the process of preparation of future controls: we have initiated a new tool of "detection of non-economic risks", through which we have passed almost 14,000 public awards, which have been analyzed. The changes will be perfectly reflected here, since the limits are modified, the conditions of some types of the selection process are rectified and similar.
Do you think that the passage of this law is beneficial or that, rather, it is a mess for the bureaucratic system?
I think that this law is surely a step in the right direction and I hope so.
The problem that may arise is that the regulations "tighten" and that it is impossible to comply with it.
Already with the previous regulations, controlled persons were linked to the contractor (e.g., in the case of the regional operational programmes of the regional council), and each unresponsibled law on public awards meant the violation of budgetary discipline.
Is it made sense then to tighten the law in this way?
I don't think that way.
The system must exclude those who want to attack it or take advantage of it and not punish those who make a formal failure, which has no impact on decision-making.
Such a system will only create greater bureaucratic pressure.
And how do you get out of this?
We'll see where this initiated path leads us.
The prime minister has recently stated that the SDG wouldn't disturb businessmen with controls of the so-called švarc system, referring to being autonomous only on paper. What does this mean?
Is the švarc" system prohibited or permitted?
The law must be regulated in some way and, if it prohibits something, then not even the prime minister of the government can prevent the performance of the Office that has an obligation to carry out such checks and demand obligations.
You can say, "We changed the law and allow it," but you can't say we'll do like anything.
In the law on public awards, the regulations are quite strict as to the way it must act formally, and this is how it should be to protect public competition.
But, on the other hand, it is tragic to exclude the candidate with the best offer for not meeting the formal demands.
The law will never be perfect, but its application must be correct. This is, in my opinion, what we lack.
The roads have ice sheets, although they are mostly passable
In several places in the Czech Republic the roads have ice and snow plates.
Although most of the roads are passable, in some extreme caution should be exercised.
Karlovy Vary Region
Today in the morning the roads in the Karlovy Vary region were passable, but they had ice and snow plates.
The temperature drops and will oscillate between five and ten degrees below zero. During the day the thermometers will rise slightly.
It has ceased to snow in the region and in the lower areas the layer of snow is thin.
On the other hand, in the ridges of the Metallic Mountains the snow reaches almost 30 centimeters.
In some places the visibility was still poor because of the fog, according to information from the region’s road operations control center.
The R6 fast track and first-class road in the region are now walkable unrestricted.
However, it is advisable to exercise extreme caution, especially in some bridges whose surface can be frosty and slippery.
In addition, all second- and third-rate roads, including mountain roads, are also passable.
However, in some of them there are still stretches with traces of ice and rammed snow.
There is a need to increase attention on the road, especially in the higher areas.
Pardubice and Hradec Králové Region
On some roads in eastern Bohemia there is a danger of frost and in higher areas and in the mountains there may have been a layer of snet on the tracks, reports on its pages the Directorate of roads and highways.
Road operators warn drivers about the danger of ice sheets forming, especially in higher areas of the Pardubice region.
Frost can occur in the vicinity of Lanškrouna, Ústí nad Orlicí, Poličky, Svitav or Vysokého Mýta, and above all on second and third category roads.
Roads I/43 and I/34 in Svitav have been chemically treated.
There is snet especially on the roads of the Krkonoše to Orlické hory mountains.
There is a layer of snow rammed in the highest areas of the roads in the municipalities of Rychnov nad Kněžnou and Trutnov.
In the area of eastern Bohemia, the sky will be today between clear and partially cloudy, without precipitation.
Temperatures will usually range from three below zero to a positive degree, a weak wind will blow.
Pilsen Region
Roads in the Pilsen region are walkable this morning, although extreme caution should be taken and it will be necessary to adapt to time.
In the early hours of the morning there will be frosts, temperatures will range between three and nine degrees below zero.
After snowfall and the subsequent drop in temperatures, some pathways could also be covered in ice.
In some places drivers will encounter fog, but visibility will progressively improve.
This is what emerges from information from the road operations control centre in the region.
The D5 motorway is passable almost unrestricted, although it is recommended to exercise extreme precaution, especially on the road between kilometer 80 and 131.
The surface of most top-notch roads is dry and ice-free.
In the Pilsen and Tachov road area bordering Germany there may be stretches with ice sheets.
The second and third category tracks are wet, so, in this case, it is also warned of stretches with ice sheets.
Drivers should be more cautious, especially on the less busy tracks in the Šumava area.
Olomouc region
Drivers heading to the higher areas of the Olomouc region will encounter partially discarded snow.
After the chemical treatment, there was still snet in the port of Červenohorské and on the road towards Videlský kříž.
Road operators cleared the snow during the night; on Šumper’s road about three centimeters of snow accumulated, according to track managers.
In the rest of the region, most roads are passable unrestricted.
"In the highest areas of Šumper's road, there are remnants of snow.
Drivers at Červenohorské port will encounter half-discarded snow in the direction of Jeseník," a Šumper road administration operator confirmed today at ČTK.
On the ground and at night they also went to the Jesenická their colleagues and, after the chemical treatment, the clues of the higher areas are now clear and wet.
On the Olomouc road the roads are unrestricted walkables, in Šternberská the roads should drive with precaution in the forest sections, where the roads are still humid.
�Ústí nad Labem Region and Liberec Region
Road operators in northern Bohemia have reported complications on different stretches early in the morning.
Apart from some places where it has snowed or frozen, according to the police database the mountain road between Telnice and Knínice in the Ústí nad Labem region is closed.
Temperatures remain below zero, even in the lower areas, so ice and snow obviously remain in the roadway. Instead, in the lower areas, especially in the southeast of the Czech central massif, there are no problems and the roads are usually dry.
At the moment there have been no service complications.
Road operators have warned of the presence of ice around Štětí.
According to meteorologists, the conditions have been ideal during the night. During the day rain and snow is expected to melt and at night the sky will be clear and there will be ice.
Unfavorable conditions have been announced on the I/13 road on the border between the Ústí nad Labem and Liberec region.
The road between Telnice and Knínice had to be cut off by the branches of the trees that had arched on the road with the weight of the snow.
Šimon Ornest: In our concerts we want to accumulate positive energy
It is said that in just under a month the end of the world will come, what do you think about it?
It’s just one of so many other alarmist news that we like to hold on to.
The music group The Tap Tap we like to make jokes about this and we like to say that we are the only music group in the world that manages to gather enough positive energy to postpone the end of the world or to avoid it.
In addition, in December you organize a really original project: three concerts against the end of the world.
Can you explain it to our readers?
It is a state charity that we have been planning for two years.
We decided to take advantage of the marketing potential that has the end of the Mayan calendar enunciated for December 21 at 11:10 in the morning.
The previous day, on December 20 at 21 hours, we will organize 3 simultaneous concerts in Prague, Brno and Ostrava.
They will end at about the same time the Mayan calendar ends on Kiribati Island, in the Pacific Ocean, 12 hours ahead of our time zone.
Who came up with this idea?
Initially it was my idea, then we were forging the project all together with our designer Honza Augusta.
In addition to wanting to gather enough positive energy and avoiding the end of the world, we also want to reflect, together with society, on the state in which we will leave the world to our children.
On the occasion of the end of the Mayan calendar we have also created a unique collection of items: boots, T-shirts, bags and original keychains against the end of the world that can be purchased at www.e-tap.cz, another way to support us.
The group The Tap Tap, along with other performers, also sang the so-called anthem against the end-of-the-world, entitled "End of the World, Canceled".
Nowadays it is very popular on YouTube, can you hear in charity concerts?
Surely yes, and it will be just the last, well, as long as everything goes well and you don't end the world...
She'll be sung by all the performers at the same time at the three concerts.
The anthems will link to each other simultaneously thanks to the special live broadcast on Czech Television.
Tomáš Hanák has written the lyrics of this song and performs the starring role of Jesus in the video clip, where Xindl X also sings...
How did this collaboration come about?
We work together even with other personalities from the Czech cultural scene, apart from organizing many charitable events and concerts...
We make every effort to participate in this project.
It has been shown that most of them are interested and have fun working with us.
To what will the money raised in concerts against the end of the world?
To equipment for the educational center without barriers Studeo, which we completed in its sixth consecutive year with the civil association Tap in Jedličkův ústav.
Pupils from Jedličkův ústav school regularly receive the visit of teachers with whom they carry out fun and interesting activities.
The students do not have enough financial means to pay the teachers and we make an effort to make it possible.
Within the framework of the completion of the works in Jedličkův ústav, there will be an independent building to which we will move with this project.
Several groups and performers perform at each concert.
What are the selection criteria?
We try to develop a program that is attractive for all generations, including children.
For example, in Prague, Chinaski, Support Lesbiens, Illustratosphere with Dan Bárta, The Tap Tap, Marián Bango and Jiří Suchý will perform.
For more detailed information, you can consult www.kpks.cz.
Do you have any other similar "spectacular action" planned in the future?
In May we will perform for the first time at the Pražské jaro festival, so here we will surely prepare an attractive program with interesting guests.
Next year we would also like to play at the Bohemian National Hall in New York and me, personally, taking advantage of the fact that we are in the United States, I would like to complete it with performances in Washington and Chicago.
Your plans abroad are not precisely minimalist, so far you have acted for example in Madrid, Brussels, London or Moscow.
The Tap Tap is, however, a group made up of people with disabilities.
How is the logistics and organization of these trips?
It’s not that hard how it may seem at first glance.
There are five members in an electric wheelchair, the chairs must travel in the luggage compartment and we also carry a lot of weight, the boxes with the instruments...
However, until now we've managed well. ČSA and British Airways were perfectly prepared, which never ceases to surprise me.
Even in Moscow, where we have recently returned from, everything went smoothly.
Thanks to departures abroad you can compare the different attitudes of society towards the disabled and other aspects.
What have you met so far?
In Madrid, Luxembourg, London and other cities, everything works better than here; in Russia we have witnessed that, in the east, there is still a long way to go.
Unlike Prague, Moscow is full of barriers, there it remains uncommon for a person to travel alone through the city center in an electric wheelchair.
Something as ordinary as it is, for example, to give preference to someone in a wheelchair by elevator, there it is not.
Luckily, a civil partnership has been created that strives to thematicize the problems of persons with disabilities.
In which areas should we take an example of more developed countries?
There are many aspects in which we go from behind...
But it is important to mention that the improvement and improvement of the situation always depends on the people it directly affects.
In London and Madrid it is very normal for people with a severe disability to move on their own in public and be able to go to the service, to the museum, wherever they want...
But it is not very common that a large number of people with disabilities participate in social life actively, in this aspect, with The Tap Tap we go a little ahead!
A respectful or barrier-free society is one thing, but when the time comes when eminent athletes, artists, actors, politicians or lawyers arise, it will begin to really change everything.
Until now, they are isolated cases of people who have great willpower.
The Tap Tap group is currently very popular, but if we look a few years ago, what led you to form the group in 1998?
I entered as an educator in Jedličkův ústav, where I had a lot of young people around me who wanted to dedicate themselves to something.
And since I'm a musician, I play, among others, the saxophone, I founded an amateur group with a coworker.
And over time, as our moderator Láďa Angelovič says, we left a little hands (laughs).
However, popularity didn’t come until these last few years, or am I wrong?
It is true that we were quite helped by the fact of connecting with famous singers and also actively working to promote the group.
We came to the conclusion that the work that is not seen, is as if it does not exist.
Thanks to aid from the European Union, we can also afford quality teachers, equipment, etc.
Did you intend to go this far with The Tap Tap?
From the beginning I saw the potential that there was, that you can do something differently.
The show business is full of things that look like each other.
It is logical, in its own way, everything new is cautious and it takes a long time to be accepted.
There are few extraordinary things, but I dare to say that The Tap Tap is one of them.
The impression of someone who sees us for the first time is clearly compassion, it is a natural reaction...
But compassion is totally superfluous because people with disabilities aren’t abandoned beings who suffer and of whom we should have compassion.
There are people who are able to live a full life and develop, with the condition, of course, that they have the necessary means.
I always say that if something goes well for people with disabilities, then it’s not just an advance for them, but for the whole of society.
Did you help the fact that you're a discerning boss, as a lot of people describe you?
If we want to do quality work, we have to be rigorous in many respects and demand some discipline.
I think it's completely normal.
Some people arrive with a romantic vision and with their heads in the clouds and when they learn that they have to go two days to rehearse, go to the preparatory test and spend a lot of time traveling and at concerts, the initial euphoria abandons them quickly.
But that’s how it works everywhere, in every group that wants to work and get something.
Currently, The Tap Tap has twenty members.
How many of them were already at the beginning of 1998?
Only one: Láďa Angelovič.
We're an open group, people come in and out, you can't avoid it.
Whoever has will and desire will always find the doors open
It will be the day before the expected end of the world, on Thursday 20.12.2012 at 21 a.m
It will take place at the Incheba Arena in Prague, in Fléda de Brno and at Plynojem in Ostrava and 12 groups and other musicians from the Czech Republic will participate
The three cities will connect at the end of the evening through videoconferencing to sing together the song of The Tap Tap "End of the world, canceled"
The objective of the concert is to gather the necessary resources to finance multifunctional equipment of the STUDEO barrier-free educational center in Jedličkův ústav in Prague, whose cost amounts to 25 million Czech kroner.
Concert admission costs 400 CZK and children up to 12 years have free entry in the option of early sale in Bohemiaticket
Poland and the Cosmos.
At the meeting of ministers of the countries of the European Space Agency last week, Poland was accepted as a twelfth member of ESA. It is the second ESA member of the former Eastern European bloc, after the Czech Republic, which became a full member on 12 November 2008).
Poland initiated an intense collaboration with ESA in 1994 and during the following years participated in the agency's project commission.
However, Poland’s path to the cosmos began much earlier.
Polish enthusiasts were already engaged in flights to space before World War II, although their work was not always understood.
I recall, for example, A. Šternfeld's lecture at the Warsaw Astronomical observatory in which, on December 6, 1933, he presented his ideas on the pioneering study "Introduction to Cosmonautics".
The ideas of the young engineer (born 1905) left attendees indifferent and, years later, Šternfeld recalled that Dr. Jan Gadomski had been the only one who had shown interest in his work.
In 1934 Šternfeld won the Robert Esnault-Pelterie and André Louis Hirsch Prize in France for his "Introduction to Cosmonautics".
Dr. Jan Gadomski (1899 - 1966), mentioned above, later became a great driver of astronomy and cosmonautics.
He published hundreds of articles in Polish magazines and wrote a series of books dedicated to this scientific discipline.
Gadomski became a promoter of cosmonautics, he was known all over the world and his contribution to science was rewarded when his name was given to one of the craters of the moon's hidden face.
Already in 1925-205 a dresin was built in Poland that was to be provided with a rocket engine.
Unfortunately, it is unknown who was the author of the proposal and the details of the project are not known.
It is not clear if the rocket would be used for the start-up of the dresin or for braking.
The information about this dresin is limited to that collected in the press of the time.
From 1933 Polish artillery began to dedicate itself to missiles.
The research was funded by Komórka Techniki Uzbrojenia in collaboration with Professor Mieczyslawem Wolfkem and Prof. Gustawem Mokrzyckiegim.
From the preserved material, it becomes clear that the investigation reached the stage of practical testing.
However, with the arrival of German troops, the investigation was interrupted.
In 1937 the prototype of a photoelectric hunting missile designed by the engineer Rohoziński appeared in the specialized press and the following year was published the study "Rakieta - torpeda powietrzna and rakietobomba lotnicza", by the author Leliwa-Krzywoblocki.
Both projects were aimed at the military use of missile engines.
Shortly before the war, the Interim Scientific Advisory Committee (Tymczasowy Komitet Doradczo-Naukowy) was responsible for all projects of missile techniques for military use and coordinated all work.
The committee was founded in 1937, but after two years of activity, it was interrupted by the start of the war.
Other cosmonautical studies appeared in the Polish press after the war thanks to the work of the Polish Astronautical Society (Polskie Towarzystwo Astronautyczne).
In the November 1954 issue of Problemy magazine, society is referred for the first time with four very extensive articles dedicated to astronautics.
In one of them, Professor M. Subotowicze, the creation of a society dedicated to cosmonautics was proposed.
At that time, artificial satellite projects had already been launched and it was obvious that cosmic research was a discipline with future prospects.
Since the beginning of 1956 the Polish Society of Astronautics (PTA) aspired to be part of the International Federation of Astronautics (founded in 1951) and in the autumn of that same year the PTA became a full member.
The following year the first president of the PTA, Kazimierz Zarankiewicz (1902-19 1959), became the vice president of the International Astronautics Federation.
He held this position until his death in 1959.
Beginning in 1956, the PTA significantly participated in the development of the RM (Rakieta Meteorologizna) probe rocket, which was successful and became the first Polish rocket in which scientific research was carried out.
The first RM-1 type was completed in 1957 and was first released on October 10, 1958.
The rocket had a height of 1,800 meters, about 80 cm in length and did not reach 5 kg in weight.
Later, the RM-1A version was completed and in the summer of 1959 aerial tests of the two-stage RM-2 rockets in the Błędów desert began.
The rocket measured 1.4 meters and weighed about 11.5 kg.
Another type of rocket in development was to be the subject of real scientific study. The RM-34 rocket was going to have a heyday of 14.5 km and its mission would be to control the high wind.
In 1962, however, work was suspended.
The successor to the first RM-type rockets was the Meteor-1 rocket, which was built during 1962 and 1965.
It was designed as a two-stage rocket, with a total length of 510 cm and an initial weight of 32.5 kg.
Three types (called Meteor-1A, -1B and -1C) were developed, which differed from each other by the space available for scientific devices.
In the Meteor-1A rocket the space available for the volume was 0.4 liters, in the 0.34-liter Meteor-1B and the 0.62-liter Meteor-1C.
The maximum heyday in the three types was 37 km.
Between 1965 and 1968 the Meteor-2 rocket, whose first aerial tests began in October 1970.
The Meteor-2 rocket had an initial weight of 380 kg and was able to carry a useful weight of 10 kg up to 60 km in height.
The Meteor-2H and Meteor-3 version were later constructed.
It is also necessary to mention the entry of Poland into COSPAR (Committee for Space Research) in 1960 and the formation of the national committee of COSPAR two years later.
Within the framework of the Interkosmos programme, Poland joined the research of the cosmic space on Soviet artificial satellites and, in 1978, the Polish pilot Miroslaw Hermaszewski became the second intercosmonaut after Vladimír Remek.
Suspension of the law on social work is not the solution.
Last week the constitutional court suspended social work law.
The resolution sparked a heated discussion in society.
Of course it will be interesting to look at the existing issues from a broader point of view.
Liberal economic systems in the EU, as in the globalised world, are based on the principle of greater economic and less normative competition.
Consequently, this means that different economic forms and national economic systems are in permanent conflict with each other.
The cause lies in the principle of free trade and the free and limitless movement of private capital, along with rampant financial speculation.
The effect of large differences in the labour cost (of wages) produces economic tensions.
Under this concept it is necessary to understand the situation in which the manufacturer, with the importation of cheap goods, tries to shorten distances and for this "causes a decrease" of the price of the competitor with the aim of getting most of the market and, in this way, increase its profit margin.
On a large scale it means, for most entrepreneurs, to move production abroad, to buy there cheaper or to disappear. The result is high unemployment in those countries in which the price of labor, compared to other economies, is higher.
Because the private capital has no social responsibility and, with it, it is not responsible for the unemployment that this phenomenon generates, the social expenditure of the state necessarily grows.
This whole situation promotes the total disinterest of entrepreneurs by paying taxes and compensating for the economic and social damages they have caused in their desire to obtain profits.
The situation is universally so well-known that there is no need to present specific statistical data.
Unscrupulous practices of private capital create situations, in certain economies, in which the governments of these countries are forced to compete with the aim of artificially reducing the social level of the population itself and, in this way, attracting foreign capital to the country.
In other words, governments make private capital available to their own population regardless of social decline.
This is especially evident in the changes of the laws in force.
The aim is to compel the population to accept the dictation of private capital, especially with regard to wages.
This takes place, on the one hand, through the system of economic obligation in the case of long-term unemployment; on the other, through the restricted right of employees in terms of labor rights.
The result is increasing poverty along with the increasing differences between the poor and the rich.
For a number of years ago, there have been food delivery points in Germany for poor people who can’t keep up with their own work.
The number of people who receive this type of aid already reaches one million.
Within the framework of the increase in the competitiveness of the German economy, there is a rather frequent case: people employed with a salary so low that the state must pay the rest to reach the minimum necessary to survive.
Such a scandal was made public related to the situation of the auxiliary employees of the German federal parliament.
The savings measures of practically all the southern states of the EU lead, without a doubt, to the same situation. The people, pressured by an abysmal decline of their social level, are forced to emigrate as in the 19th century or to survive with a lousy salary on the edge of society, in the hope that one day it will reach the private investment capital country.
Do you need to wonder where it comes from?
In the event that it is other EU countries, then poverty passes from one country to another or it never arrives, since Chinese, Indians, Brazilians, Turks, Moroccans, Egyptians or Africans continue to work for a tiny part of a European salary.
This also affects all of Latin America.
Liberal theories and the media affirm even satiety that the state should not contribute capital to the economy, which directing the economy leads to its failure.
Private capital defends tooth and nail the thesis that the state should not intervene in the economy meddling in control.
Next, it is necessary to ask whether private capital does not influence its selfishness in the political march and, therefore, throughout the state.
In this case, the answer is clearly affirmative.
Proof is the existence of lobbies, in practice very powerful and omnipresent, in all states.
The result is a desperate situation that is manifested in the field of corruption, in mutual favours, in legislation that is virtually reprehensible everywhere but not punishable.
In Germany the situation is as follows: State ministries, in the absence of financial means, are responsible for the drafting of bills to private legal companies, which are often linked to industry.
These types of bills are then passed in the Bundestag, the German parliament.
In this way, all power emanates from the people, as announced in the constitutions of Western democracies, but from the strong financial groups that represent their own interests.
It is obvious that democracies with a liberal orientation find themselves quickly in a situation such as that described by Appiános in his work The Crisis of the Roman Republic in the time of Caesar and Pompeii: "The state had long disintegrated and the administrations were forcibly occupied."
In exchange for bribes, illegitimately obtained favors and help to finance stones or swords.
Bribery and corruption spread unimpeded and the people went to the polls with the vote spoken in advance"... "there were no people in character who aspired to a position in the administration, so once, because of this disorganization, the state spent eight months without consuls"... "It began to be serious that the only remembrance for such a serious situation was autocracy
The conclusion, just as in antiquity, is that today’s society is based on inconsiderated preferences of individual profit without taking into account common interests.
Private capital cannot, in its essence, understand and claim the benefits of the whole society.
The result is the same as that of then, an unprecedented decay of the elite, without an effort to reform it in depth.
The causes of the rise of fascist and communist regimes must therefore be sought in the rampant liberalization of economic systems in the 19th and 20th centuries.
With respect to the current situation, it is possible to consider the disappearance of these systems for the benefit of a liberal democracy, as a definite pause, pending the next coup.
The really demolishing fact is that today’s elite completely ignores the losses of hundreds of thousands in lives, social and humanitarian catastrophes that we have witnessed, as well as crimes against humanity, as we know them in ancient and most recent history.
Suspension of the law on public procurement is not the solution; at least not in the long term.
Under pressure from economic competition, both internationally and within the EU, the government of the Czech Republic will be forced to continue looking for other avenues to reduce the social level of the population.
This trend is therefore systemic.
The solution goes through concrete socio-political reforms, which reinforce both the participation of state capital and the influence of citizens in the government and weaken the monopoly position of private capital in society for the benefit of the state.
Israel: The Lab of Chaos.
"That nothing comes from violence and never could", Sting in his song Fragile and whose refrain reads "Let's do not forget how fragile we are" (let's not forget how fragile we are).
"If my children didn't want wars, there wouldn't be any" Gutele Schnaper, wife of Mayer Amschel Rothschild, on her deathbed in 1849.
After the latest waves of violence between Israel and the Gaza Strip, various reactions have arisen, as always.
One, on Israel’s side, argues in favor of the right to self-defense and pigeonholes in the role of terrorists. The other supports the Palestinians and alleges racism by the state of Israel, genocide perpetrated against Palestinian Arabs and calls Israel a terrorist state.
I do not want to focus on who is guilty of the massacres that are repeated periodically and who is the victim. After all, the citizens of today of Israel, including the autonomous territories, were born within the current political situation and did not experience the beginnings of violence.
My intention is to offer readers a look behind the curtain, a look to discover who takes advantage of the tensions that have dragged for 95 years (calculated from the Balfour declaration in November 1917) on a small land area in the Middle East.
Some of my considerations are based on historical facts, others on conclusions that I have reached on the basis of my own way of understanding the matter, on who or what group of people is the main driver of the events of modern history.
In the history of humanity, the history of power struggles is in the front row.
In each era we can find an Alexander the Great or a Napoleon.
But what is no longer so obvious is if it was always these characters who directed their steps autonomously or if behind their throne there was someone who oriented their actions towards a certain objective.
We must accept that we live in an age that concentrates the wealth of the world in the hands of a few individuals and that to this concentration of money and the power that this implies, has not been reached in a single generation.
Among these astronomically rich families stands out one that could be considered the most prominent (if there is still someone behind, I don’t know, but I don’t dismiss it): the Rothschilds.
You don't write much about them.
Of course.
In the 90’s of the 19th century they bought the first news agency (Reuters) to stop associating his name with very serious criminal acts. Power and wealth, or both, covered their backs, which for them always meant a strengthening.
