Movie Argo: If it gets raw, call Hollywood
In November 1979 a group of the Islamist student demonstration occupied the U.S. embassy in Tehran and took 52 graduates hostage.
His release was going to be in exchange for the dethroned Shah Mohamed Réza Páhlevi, who after the revolution fled to the U.S
For the American administration the situation did not offer any positive outlook: throwing the shah overboard could not afford it, since, if it did, it endangered the confidence of its allies in other countries.
But the release of the hostages in Iran, where the revolution had led to the constitution of the theocratic regime, was not within its reach.
It was a blow to the prestige of the United States, which later intensified further with the failed attempt to forcibly release the hostages.
Incarcerated diplomats were released 444 days after the Algerian government’s negotiating intervention.
In the U.S
Iran’s failure contributed decisively to Jimmy Carter’s loss to Ronald Reagan in the 1980 presidential election.
The film "Argo" by director and actor Ben Affleck themes an episode of this story, which achieved a small success for America.
Shortly before occupying the embassy, six employees escaped from the building.
After a complicated journey, they ended up at the residence of the Canadian embassy.
CIA, in collaboration with the Canadian authorities, managed to get them out of Iran, helping an uncovered story an outlandish point: they came out with Canadian passports, as members of the film crew that examined the location in the country for the filming of a sci-finction overproduction.
Combination of genres
The plan that "exfiltration" expert Toni Mendez called for Hollywood's collaboration.
For the lying to seem true, it was written about the film project in specialized magazines, press conferences were organized, the fictional production society even had a real office.
The details about the operation were kept secret for a long time, the film has been made from Tony Mendez's memories.
Affleck’s film is a unique combination of various genres.
On the one hand, a realistic political thriller; on the other, a so-called "caper movie", the story of an episode that went well, a deception (an example may be the Ocean's Eleven series).
In the film the moods are combined. We found on the one hand well-documented sequences of Tehran. In the sequence of credits there are emblematic photographs of the time of events, which were captured in the same way in the film; there are not many differences in this regard.
On the other, somewhat lighter Hollywood scenes, marked by irony and a slight exaggeration.
And in addition, scenes from the CIA headquarters and other institutions: sucked men who analyze the situation at the negotiating table, in the corridors of the office, by phone...
Ben Affleck has managed to restart his career in an excellent way.
The actor, who had been teased, has become a respectable director and his way of acting has ceased to be the target of ironic comments.
Argo is his third feature film, after "Goodbye, Goodbye" (2007) and the thriller "The Town. City of Thieves" (2010).
At the same time, it’s Affleck’s first film in which the action doesn’t take place in the city of Boston, where it was raised.
Likewise, the sensibility of capturing the atmosphere of the place was a characteristic that placed the directors of old films above Hollywood standards.
And Affleck proves it in Argo, where Tehran "wheels" into Canada.
The best scenes of the film take place in the streets, in the reconstruction of true events. The introductory sequence of the seizure of the embassy is impressive for its synoptical capacity that, at the same time, conveys the emotion of bewilderment and the lack of foresight that arises when, somewhere, the story takes a step forward.
Affleck and his collaborators achieve a similar effect on fictitious scenes (the impostor team in the Tehran bazaar).
Too much action in too many places
The director had to accept the fact that an explained story doesn’t offer much in the cinematic sense of the most intriguing scenes.
And he manages quite well. Sometimes he adds something to reality: here everything doesn’t come out so elegantly (the scene in which the crisis lurks at Tehran’s airport, the deviation of a call to America and the rampant search behind its runway become crazy).
Argo’s weakness is the lack of control that arises from the need to show too much action in too many places.
Alan Arkin and John Goodman have a very funny role as Hollywood collaborators, although their characters deserved more space and aren’t the only ones in this film.
For these reasons, Affleck’s film loses a bit of a dramatic stroke, but it is a film that can be followed with a moderate interest, valuing its realization and the retro style of a thriller that evokes the 70’s.
But let it collapse, that'll be difficult.
As an evocation of a true story and as proof of how exaggerated the notions of omnipresence and omniscience of the secret services may be, is sufficient.
Rules for inflating balloons, bananas and circus
Server www.bankovnipoplatky.com, which every year presents a survey on the most absurd banking fees, has now decided to announce the contest on "the regulation or the most absurd ideas of the EU workshop".
"It occurred to us after the last event, the EU plans to approve a 40 percent quota of women in management positions in large European companies," explained Právo Patrik Nacher, the organiser of the survey.
Among the nominated absurdities is, for example, the recent decision of the European court to unify insurance policies between men and women.
Until now, women were getting ahead of better prices on life insurance because they objectively pose a lower risk.
"Other incredible ideas from the EU workshop can be proposed by people until the end of this year."
The election will take place at the end of February 2013," Nacher reported.
Among the EU’s controversial regulations is, for example, the mandatory addition of biocomponents to fuel which, as a result, harms the environment; another prohibition is the use of lifelong mercury thermometers only because they contain a small amount of a dangerous substance or the guideline the size of the chicken cages, which has contributed to the considerable increase in the price of eggs this year.
It has been criticized the factual prohibition of using the expression "spream butter" or the removal of the classic light bulb from shops.
A quality banana should have a length of 14 centimeters.
The Union’s apparatus takes such decisions often under the pressure of either commercial or industrial lobby, whose petitions in Brussels are normally defended by some state or group of states (the Czech Republic, even with the threat of veto, claims the demands of its banks).
The interests of a lobby were behind the request, for example, that higher-quality bananas should have a length of at least 14 centimeters in the EU and could not present "abnormal curvatures".
The European Commission defended itself by saying that it only seeks to harmonise non-homogened national standards to date, which only complicated their marketing.
Standards referring to fruits and vegetables in the EU, despite opposition from some states, have softened on the basis that the existing directive entails a waste of groceries.
The candidate to win the poll could be last year’s U.S. prescription according to which inflatable balloons being marketed it is mandatory to warn that children under the age of 8 are not allowed to inflate them without the supervision of an adult.
The U.S. referred here to an American study according to which, when talking about toys, balloons are the leading cause of childhood death from suffocation.
A similar restriction applicable to the use of second-parents by children up to 14 years of age is also in effect.
Curious occurrences are born even in our country
Equally absurd is the regulation concerning European officials: anyone who has any official function in the EU cannot use the name of Macedonia. In order not to hurt the sensitivity of Greece, we must talk about FYROM (Former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia).
Bankovnípoplatky.com, in collaboration with the association of liberal economists Laissez Faire, nominated in the survey, among some absurd proposals mentioned above, the Union’s prescription on the volume of food supplies in the territory of a member state.
The EU determined the maximum volumes of food supplies that can be found in the territory of the Czech Republic on the same day of our entry into the EU.
Afterwards, the Czech Republic exceeded, for example, the volume set for canned mushrooms, which meant a threat of a substantial fine.
The organizers of the survey were also interested in the occurrence of paying those countries that do not have access to the sea and the idea of granting a grant for the application for grants.
This kind of ideas did not arise in Brussels but in Prague.
“We’re at a disadvantage because we don’t have a sea.
We asked the European Union for compensation," then agriculture minister Jaroslav Palas (ČSSD) said in autumn 2004.
He argued with the fact that in our country there was a lot of cereal and, as a result of so-called intervention purchases, the state had full warehouses and had to be exported.
According to Palas, given that the Czech Republic is far from seaports, the EU should pay us hundreds of millions of euros.
In the end, the European Commission was ready to help the Czech Republic and organised a public tender for the purchase of cereal from countries that do not have access to the sea.
The grant to receive a grant was proposed to the municipalities by the ministry for local development led by Minister Pavel Němec (US-DEU). In particular, it is intended for the drafting of grant applications from Brussels.
EU: Rare prescriptions are an exception
Member States’ guidelines are often the target of criticism, the EU’s efforts on regulations and, likewise, more effective functioning and development across the Union deserve rather, according to various experts, our recognition.
According to the experts, the most serious problem is, for example, the collection of EU grants for projects that have nothing to do with the deepening of European integration, but which the Member States have achieved in the budget negotiations.
The Czechs also have the sensitive fibre, just as to other countries of the Union, the fight for the right to the denomination of origin of their traditional products, although our country has not always emerged victorious from these struggles.
The Czechs, after six years of disputes with Germans and Austrians, successfully defended the denomination of origin of Olomouc's cottage cheese, but had to rename the national rum, whose tradition in our country dates back to the 19th century, and was renamed "tuzemak".
Only products made from sugar cane distillate may be referred to as rum, but not those made from sugar beets.
In the official list of protected designations of origin of the EU, together with feta and gorgonzola cheeses known all over the world, the marzapan of the German city Lübeck or the ham of Parma, there are, for example, the biscuits of Karlovy Vary, the Pohořelice tent and of Třeboň and, even, the variety of hops of Ž
Like protected seas of the EU, you will be able to be proud of Pardubice gingerbread and Hořice v Podkrkonoší wafers.
People want me to save the republic, but I'm an amateur, Okamura says
Senator, how did you think of running for election for president?
It's not about being a senator or president.
If in our country everything were fine, then I would not present my candidacy.
But I can no longer bear to see how you steal in the last twenty years in our country, how thieves go unpunished, taxes and retirement age are increased.
He had no ambitions to be a politician.
When I see something I don’t like, I’m trying to find a solution to change it.
And since I’m already forty years old and my candidacy is independent, the only possibility I have to change things is to introduce myself as a senator or president.
The Senate has already arrived, but soon after it has set its way to the presidency.
Aren't you turning your back on people who voted for you?
I always said that my candidacy for the presidency depended on the results in the senate elections.
Later I specified that if I was elected senator, I would present my candidacy for the presidency.
But my goal is not the function; the function is the tool with which I can realize my own ideas.
That's why I need the greatest possible influence and the strongest possible mandate.
The problem is not only that we are a nation that we criticize in the bar or in front of television, but we trample on anyone who wants to initiate a change.
To this is added the media, which discredit you and confuses freedom of speech with the freedom to lie.
From me it is said that I have bribed journalists or that I have been an advisor to Jiří Paroubek.
Let’s talk about his ideas.
It is presented to the presidential struggle with financial thesis and criminal responsibilities of politicians and the declaration of property above twenty million.
To do this, the law must change.
As president he does not have this competence and only the senate as a whole can propose laws.
How do you want to figure it out?
As a citizen, I have been part of a pressure group so that the guiding craft would be recognized as self-employment, and we achieved it.
The problem lies in politics: if someone arrives with a good proposal, either from the right or from the left, it is purposefully rejected and this affects citizens.
As an independent candidate, I have the greatest potential to gain the support of all parliamentary groups.
Without pigeonholes or party dogmas, I have the ability to choose something from each party that is better for our country and apply it.
Is he considered rather a man of the right or of the left?
From the Czech point of view, I have the impression that people consider me most left-wing.
It doesn't matter to me, whether it's a bit of the left or a little right.
What I care is that we get ahead.
I don't care if someone's from the right or the left, I want to bring people together.
I always openly support any good solution, regardless of whether the proposal comes from the KSČM or the SDG and, in the same way, I will be against bad ideas.
It's joking when it's said that you're populist.
But you'ren't proving it with what you're saying?
When you create a business plan for the company, you also have certain goals and ideas.
And you're trying to get closer to them.
There will be those who call it populism, but all the proposals I talk about already work in the world or acquaintances experts talk about them.
But without the support of parliament he only has slogans left.
With this he cannot survive in long-term policy.
Or do you think that if you turn to people and talk to them, you will get, for example, criminal and financial responsibility?
I don't have a choice.
Convincing politicians, journalists and civil society and trying to put them on my side to achieve it.
If he were elected president, there would be no problem in organizing a live television broadcast in which he would ask the leaders of parliamentary parties to legitimize criminal and financial responsibility for politicians, officials, judges and state prosecutors.
And eventually, they would have to communicate why they don’t want it.
If there’s a strong character pointing to disputes, you can put pressure on the political scene.
Let’s take by case the direct election of the president, which has been an achievement of public pressure.
At the same time I affirm that I am only an amateur, I am neither a genius nor an intellectual.
For my opinions and ideals I try to look for allies.
I've just started into politics and try to get as much support as possible for my program.
I'm trying to move things forward myself, but in case it doesn't work out, six years from now I'm going to leave it and go back to the private sector.
It sounds a bit like Okamura is going to save the Czech Republic.
I'm no savior.
I know that with a person I don’t do anything, that’s why I’ve visited a series of well-known personalities, just in case they’d be encouraged to present their candidacy to the senate.
I went to see Radim Jančura, but he refused because he had a lot of work.
For my part, at least I have supported investigative journalist Jana Lorencová, who brought to lead fraud with light heating oils.
I introduced myself on the basis that people are really dissatisfied, now I'm on the scales.
Sixty percent of the population did not go to the polls and, those who did, voted the representatives of the establishment.
In the senate we are two of the independent sector.
People chose the senate, so it will be difficult to bring about changes.
However, I will defend my own ideas, such as the direct election of mayors or governors.
Are you considering forming your own party?
I haven’t considered it yet because I don’t have time for it, to check the blameless past of each of the potential members of the party, nor do I have money.
I don't even have money for the presidential campaign, there's only 20,000 kronor in my transparent account.
You don't have money?
He talks about property statements, how's his?
I've estimated my private property at about 60 million.
In Prague I have a land valued at 25 million, a flat valued ten million, another floor appraised at eight million, a collection of works of art worth ten million, an Aston Martin of about 3.5 million and a Škoda Superb valued at one million, and in the account I have a couple of millions.
By the way, I have an Aston Martin because it was my dream of youthus. I always liked James Bond, who drove this car, was gallant with women and, at the same time, fought against evils and injustices.
Do you drive an Aston Martin, your property is valued at 60 million and has no money for the campaign?
You say you want to change the republic but you don't want to put too much money on your side.
It's not very convincing.
I don't have 15 million for the campaign.
Do I have to borrow?
In the campaign I have already put 2.5 million.
The fact that I don't have any sponsors shows that there's not too much interest in my program.
And I don't have an obligation to pay the campaign with money out of pocket.
The expenses of the campaign essentially cover what I will receive as a senator’s salary.
For example, I couldn’t afford English school, which costs 30,000 kroners per month.
If I just wanted to make money, then I wouldn't run as a candidate.
So you’ll stick with your business to be able to live?
Didn't you say before that you were going to leave this activity?
This has nothing to do with a higher salary.
As I have promised, in part I have left my activities.
For example, the representative of my travel agency will become my replacement in spring.
People would want him to be the Samaritan to save the republic.
But I also need to live something.
How much do you usually earn monthly as an entrepreneur?
Between 200,000 and 400,000, which I continue to win.
If he were president, then he'd leave business.
You can consult the entire interview in Saturday’s Práva edition.
The architecture studio MVRDV demonstrates that the real adventures are not only in the head - in the example of Spijkenisse's library and the newly finished "mountain of books" - 2 photos
"I think the building is fun, has a futuristic look, and there's finally something interesting to see," says Lisette Verhaig, a passerby passing through the street.
Stefan Spermon, an IT technician in a large company located near the library, said: "Furthermore, the building is beautiful."
But I wonder why we need a library today.
We all have internet, tablets and e-books.
No one walks into those old-fashioned libraries for pleasure, doesn't they?
Spijkenisse, a dormitory city located at the gates of Róterdam that hardly deserves a fleeting visit, boasts the dubious honor of having risen with a record to forget.
The population of 80,000 has the lowest literacy rate in the Netherlands.
To counteract this shameful situation, some years ago it was decided to somehow contribute to the general culture and copy the seven fictitious bridges that are represented in the euro banknotes in the form of decorated reinforced concrete miniatures.
The success of the educational offensive was limited.
And that’s how policymakers decided that there was only one possibility to improve the statistics: you had to build a library!
Winy Maas, of the Dutch architecture studio MVRDV, a teacher in the art of bar diagrams and responsible for fun buildings while cynical, took the subject with his usual serenity and appeared at the presentation of the contest in 2003 with five books under his arm and a smile on his face.
And as the jury looked perplexed around and shrugged, Maas proceeded to stack the books by size forming a pyramid and concluded his brief speech with the following words: "Deemed fellow citizens:
Here it is, my proposal for Spijkenisse's mountain of books, the so-called 'Boekenberg.'"
Nine years later, the mountain of 30 million euros rises in the planned place.
This is part of the revitalization project, which is completed with an underground car park, a supermarket, a post office and a couple of residential and townhouse buildings.
At the beginning of November the mountain of books was awarded the second prize "Best Library of NL 2012".
In addition, the project is also nominated for "Dutch National Wood Award 2012".
In this way, the small town lacking in history, which to date had nothing more to offer than a post-modern pedestrian area and an incredibly ugly town hall behind whose white facade one would rather intuit a dairy plant, now has a work of contemporary architecture.
But above all, Spijkenisse now has the first public cultural building in its history.
The Long Road to the Book
The first impression is that it is a paradise of books under a cheesemaker.
It is true that there is an elevator that transports us right to the center of this mountain massif, but the real lovers of space and reading do not use it, but prefer to merge with their topography at every step of their climb to the top.
The fully glazed interior is luminous and easy to encompass with view, annealed brick flooring and elegant streetlights unequivocally support the public condition of the library.
The urban atmosphere is perfect.
When you look up, you can visualize the park’s benches, dogs and boys and girls playing football.
Books, books and more books.
"Normally bookshelves are arranged along the façade and in the center opens a huge dark room that is usually somewhat uncomfortable and impersonal," says Winy Maas.
What we’ve done is turn upside down the classic spatial configuration and take the reading area from the inside to the outside.
The interior of the mountain is very well exploited: in the middle are the offices, the Internet library, the chess club, the environmental education center and the maintenance room.
Special mention deserves the black shelves that assume the functions of mural cladding, parapetus and railing at the same time.
Sight, touch and smell are strange.
Even architects and civil engineers more a day shake their heads when they hear about this unknown material.
"We wanted to work with recycled materials," Joop Trouborst, head of the Spijkenisse city, said.
And that's how one day, in a Frisian farm, we come across the right by-product from agriculture.
For many years, in greenhouses and in the fields of the Netherlands a plastic fabric of few millimeters of thickness is used as a basis for holding the roots of crops.
It is a cheap and efficient material.
This fine woven lasts two seasons and then discarded.
For the library this fabric was pressed, for the first time in such quantities, to get plates four centimeters thick.
With heat and pressure, the so-called agricultural plastic (KLP) changes color and becomes a dark, homogeneous and sturdy material that smells like a mixture of new car and sneaker.
After 105 steps you reach the top of the mountain.
At the end of this continuous route of almost 500 meters around the mountain awaits us the reward: from the café you not only have a fantastic view of the city, but in it you can also enjoy some tasty Dutch croquettes and their ficus planted in huge pots.
These create a pleasant atmosphere while maintaining atmospheric humidity suitable for this mountain of literature.
Donations for a New Soul
"Although it is difficult to believe given its large glazed surfaces, this building is an exemplary project when it comes to ecology," says Trouborst.
Necessary heating and cooling are obtained from geothermal energy.
Even though the mountain of books is situated under a huge glass pyramid, even on the sunniest days the sun only shines for a few hours inside.
Huge wooden trusses located transverse to the glass façade serve to shade and absorb most of the sun’s rays.
This creates a comfortable indoor climate.
Rest of it's an automatic blind thing.
Stefan Spermon, the skeptical worker in the Information Technology sector, has already dared to set foot in the new library.
Lisette Verhaig has also been there.
Likewise, traditional Chinese medicine teacher Cynthia Bogarde has visited the library. She calls the "Boekenberg" as Spijkenisse's "late soul".
The reason is that, on the occasion of the inauguration that took place a couple of weeks ago, all the inhabitants of the city were invited to donate a book from their private collection.
In this way it is intended to fill, at the moment, the optical gaps of a library that does not yet have its complete archive; at present it has 70,000 specimens.
The concept went deep.
Shelves are packed.
“There’s nothing worse than a half-empty library,” says architect Winy Maas.
"I think that thanks to our initiative now all the inhabitants have a certain link with this building.
Everyone knows that his book is part of the library.
Even if it's only there to adorn.
In this way, the MVRDV study has achieved a success in that prevailing discipline that in specialized slang is known as "creation of an identity".
The city of Spijkenisse has written a piece of the history of literature.
Regardless of how young and uneducated it may be.
Finally a starting point to shape your identity.
Szabo: "The Germans must play a more leading role"
Germany abstained in the vote on the admission of Palestine.
According to Stephen Szabo, an expert on U.S.-Europe relations, this puts Berlin in a rather tricky diplomatic position.
Deutsche Welle: Earlier in the week, Germany hinted that it would vote against the proposal to grant United Nations observer status to the Palestinian territories.
Berlin eventually abstained.
Why?
Stephen Szabo: Germany doesn’t support what Israelis have done in Gaza.
Now, due to the special relationship that unites the country with Israel, Germany must be careful.
But at the same time, I don’t think it supports the U.S. position either.
Germany wanted to prove its independence without being too critical of Israel.
During the uprising that took place in Libya in March 2011, Germany also abstained where it was to establish a no-fly zone.
It has finally been declared by NATO.
Is it difficult for Germany to adopt a clear stance when important international issues are at stake?
Yes, that's right.
This is because at the moment he is reorienting his foreign policy, moving away from a policy led, so to speak, by the United States and focusing on German foreign policy.
And what makes things even more difficult is that Europeans do not have a coherent and uniform policy.
Therefore, the Germans are at a crossroads.
They are expected to play a more independent role, but they are not used to doing so.
I think they’re stilling what their role will be, but they’ve already started the road to “normal” foreign policy.
A foreign policy similar to countries such as France or Britain.
What specifically does "normal" foreign policy mean from the German standpoint?
It is a policy that, when it comes to national interests, is willing to defend positions other than those of the United States or its European partners.
I believe that German foreign policy finds a strong motivation in economic policy, that is, in exports and in their relations with certain regions, such as Russia, China or the Middle East.
German economic interests are to some extent different from those of other great powers and for that reason Germany must protect its interests.
Have these economic interests influenced the position taken regarding the conflict in the Middle East and the UN vote?
On the one hand, Germany has large markets in the Middle East, especially in the Gulf.
For this reason it must be careful and not alienated from Arab public opinion or Arab elites.
This is important, of course.
But I wouldn’t give it so much importance since it’s not a totally one-sided relationship.
However, they do play a pivotal role in German considerations.
Has Germany undermined its relationship with the U.S
I believe that in Europe, and even in the United States, the German position enjoys an enormous degree of understanding.
For this reason, I don’t think there was such a drastic rupture as in the case of Libya.
Perhaps with this Germany has managed to gain some respect.
After all, all this hasn't done but shown that it's a country they're taking seriously as an international actor and whose interests need to be taken into account.
There are differing views on the Palestinian initiative in Europe.
Instead, the U.S. has been clearly in favor of a veto.
Is there a disparity of views between the U.S. and many European nations?
These disparities have always existed due to U.S. domestic policy.
I believe that the Obama administration secretly shows a great understanding towards the European position.
But, because of the political situation that is experienced here, it is clear that the government cannot publicly defend that position.
I believe that the true disparity of opinions does not really have the magnitude attributed to it.
If we look at relations between Obama and Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu, we see that Obama is not at all excited about the policy he develops.
Is it difficult for Germany to reconcile the close relations it maintains with Israel and the United States, on the one hand, with the position of its main partners in the EU, on the other?
I think that's precisely what makes it so difficult for Germans.
Of course it would be a bit easier for the Germans if there was a coherent and homogeneous European policy, but that is not the case.
Therefore, they do not have the possibility of being part of a higher entity, but they must boost things from their own position.
That’s exactly what they’re doing with the euro now.
I believe that in the future Germany will take a leading role in forcing Europe to adopt a European stance.
Of course this isn’t easy for Germany, either simply because of the relationship it maintains with Israel.
This has always been a tricky topic.
But I think the Germans are clear that they must play the role of independent figure.
Does Germany see itself in the role of an important international actor? Does Germany really want to take a leading role?
Or maybe it’s still difficult for you to accept that leadership?
Germany has not yet become accustomed, it is always somewhat uncomfortable and, for obvious reasons, the country still struggles to play a more important role.
If we look at the euro crisis, for example, we see that whenever Germany positions itself as the main actor, certain anti-German sentiments are unleashed.
It's not easy for Germans.
It is an old problem and no one wants to be surrounded by enemy countries.
Seen in this way, Germany is in a much more difficult position than the United States.
It must show a receptive attitude towards different neighbours and opinions and that is not easy.
U.S. influence on European policy is increasingly diminishing, but the EU doesn’t currently cover that gap, who remains then?
Germans will have no choice but to assume a more leading role.
Even if they don’t like it, if they find it unpleasant or if it makes them an even more unpopular village, c’est la vie!
Stephen Szabo is Executive Director of the Transatlantic Academy in Washington, an institution in which academics and political experts from Europe and North America jointly analyze the challenges facing the transatlantic community.
Szabo is also a member of the German Marshall Fund, where he has specialized in German politics, U.S. foreign policy and transatlantic relations.
"Protection of brands" in China: when Puma and Armani suddenly become Chinese brands
Armani is a well-known brand worldwide and Polo Ralph Lauren as well.
What is Armani Polo?
Behind this name hides a denomination officially registered in China but which has nothing to do with the original companies.
However, it enjoys protection as long as the real eponyms do not lodge a lawsuit.
And even then it's not clear they're going to be right.
“Foreigners find it increasingly difficult to protect their trademarks in China,” said Thomas Pattloch, a lawyer at the Taylor Wessing law firm specializing in protection rights violations in the Far East.
Every week a new case arrives at my table.
Imitators simply add a couple of letters to be able to register their marks.
In this way, Gucci becomes Lu-Gucci and instead of Prada Prada-Kny is registered.
German companies are also legally copied in the same way, as has happened to sporting goods manufacturer Puma.
Pattloch opens a file cabinet in which it stores seats in the Beijing brand office.
On September 14, 2010, a Chinese company applied to register the Zegna DF Puma trademark, an artificial name that was also served as the Ermenegildo Zegna fashion house.
That the Chinese are world champions of copying and violating intellectual property rights is well known.
In large cities there are multi-storey department stores dedicated exclusively to the sale of counterfeits.
However, Pattloch’s cases are of another nature. On behalf of his clients, he fights against the fact that the trademark office officially grants Chinese companies the right to take advantage of a denomination that is already registered elsewhere.
The Chinese call it "Bang Ming Pai" or accompanying brand.
The word has its origin in the Chinese term "Bang Dakuan",
it refers to women who have "fished" a rich man.
Chinese authorities don't feel guilty
"It's detrimental to the business, you have to fight it," Pattloch demands.
"The mark makes waters, the monopolistic position disappears and the damage the image suffers is enormous."
The financial losses and procedural costs of the affected sectors are millionaires, especially in the case of emblematic luxury products.
According to data from the market analysis company CLSA, with a volume of 15,000 million euros a year China is, in size, the third market for luxury goods and the one that grows at the fastest pace.
However, achieving the annulment of registrations of dubious legality in the registration of trademarks is complicated and also involves a lot of money.
Proceedings can be extended up to nine years and the result is uncertain.
Pattloch talks about cases in which the court dismissed a lawsuit because so much time had elapsed that the name object of the lawsuit had already become a “market reality.”
“If the plaintiff is unlucky, he may even have to pay money to the plagiarist because the process has harmed him’s brand in China,” Pattloch said.
Sometimes this is the law of the jungle.
Some of the most well-known cases have to do with graphic elements.
In 2009 Daimler-Benz lost a lawsuit against construction machinery maker Sany, the company that has recently been sucked by German concrete pump manufacturer Putzmeister.
That’s why today the Chinese can still use an emblem very similar to the Mercedes star.
Volvo buyer Geely initially used a white and blue logo reminiscent largely of BMW; the matter was settled in court and Geely had to modify it.
Clothes maker Lacoste lost in China against imitators from Hong Kong and Singapore whose logo represented a crocodile looking in the opposite direction from the original.
The Chinese authorities don’t feel guilty.
The CTMO brand office in Beijing recognizes that in 2010 they have had difficulties due to a shortage of equipment and staffing.
Last year, however, "the work-emergency situation returned to normal."
In this way, the number of unprocessed claim processes has been reduced by 22%.
Almost 57,000 of these cases were resolved, 75% more than the previous year.
However, 81,500 claims are still pending resolution.
Struggling this costs a lot of money
As is usual in China, the figures are surprising.
Last year the CTMO registered more than 1.4 million trademark protection applications, almost a third more than in 2010.
It would be a new record and means that China leads the list of new registrations around the world for the 10th consecutive year, according to the brand office.
It seems that this also happens with the number of valid logos, a figure that amounts to 5.5 million in total.
In 2011, 1.8 billion yuan would have been pocketed in rights.
In other words, this means that on average each enrollment costs 1,280 yuan or 160 euros.
Fighting this costs a lot of money, as evidenced by the case of the German family business Freudenberg.
For more than seven years, the group has fought in court against a Chinese imitator.
While the Germans have managed to stop the illegal manufacture of imitation car parts,
The imitator has managed to secure the Chinese rights over the Freudenberg brand.
"We ourselves have provoked this situation because in Germany surnames are not protected," says Hanno Wentzler, President and CEO of Freudenberg Chemical Specialities in Munich.
The CTMO trademark office also dismissed the appeal of the Muniquoise.
In the following two instances Freudenberg has been right, but the opposing party is still fighting today.
You have to be very careful
Now the case has reached the doors of the High Court of Justice, where it is still pending resolution.
Wentzler is confident that he will be able to resolve the issue favourably and praises the professionalism of the courts.
However, he also states that "the procedure is extremely expensive, both in terms of money and time."
It is difficult to estimate internal costs; the business archive has had to review files up to one hundred years old in order to provide evidence.
Five years ago Freudenberg offered the opposing party a "six-figure sum in euros" with the aim of reaching a friendly agreement, but was unsuccessful.
"This shows how much we care," Wentzler says.
Dangers lurking in the Far East threaten even to affect Europe.
specifically if imitators secure rights to unprotected trademarks there.
Thus, a Chinese manufacturer tried to register in Germany the Freudenberg name for shoes and leather products.
The group has long since left this commercial area, however it managed to prevent registration.
"You have to be very careful," Wentzler says.
Both he and lawyer Pattloch advise German companies to be extremely cautious about doing business with China.
It is not enough to rely on international law on trademark rights, but what needs to be done is "to also register in China everything that may be susceptible to protection", as Wentzler points out.
Otherwise, the topic can be much more expensive than a simple record.
In fact, in the event that Freudenberg lost the last assault of his particular battle, then he would have to pay the opposing party licensing rights by his own name, as Wentzler explains.
That or in the segment in question would drive us out of the market.
World AIDS Day: Dancing, Singing, Helping
In Heidelberg he rehearses the Imbongi choir and in Swaziland the AIDS orphans rejoice
The story of a link capable of exceeding much more than 8,733 kilometers away.
First you start with dancing: cowboy boots, basketball shoes, heels and moccasins look for the compass on the parquet floor, and find it quickly.
One, two, three, four.
Only then do the voices of the singers (contralto, bass, tenor and soprano) slowly begin to rise, swirling, haunting and following each other.
And Fiete Hopf, the 29-year-old director, threatens to get out of his boxes by trying to put order with his energetic and gentle gestures.
It is Monday afternoon and in the music room of the Heidelberg Institute of Medical Psychology the Imbongi choir rehearses a new song.
Also have come the fifty singers, between 23 and 69 years old, from the human geneticist to the master of home.
"Om'Obani" is not an easy piece, each choir voice has a different text and also in a barely well-known foreign language: Zulu, a language spoken by eleven million people in South Africa, Botswana, Lesotho, Malawi, Mozambique and in some areas of Swaziland.
Self-Help Help
According to an estimate by UNAIDS, the United Nations AIDS programme, there are approximately 34 million people infected with the HIV virus worldwide.
23.5 million of them live in South Africa.
There are 245,000 AIDS orphans in Swaziland.
More than 40% of the population is already HIV-positive.
The Voices for Africa association has found in Germany godparents for the 180 AIDS orphans of the people of Esitjeni.
Seventy of them attend high school.
For 15 or 20 euros per month you can sponsor a child.
This contribution will cover the tuition of the school, the uniform and will guarantee you one hot meal per day at the Gogo Center.
Imbongi means "story narrator" or "panegyrist" in Zulu.
In this room nobody knows how to speak this Bantu language fluently, but singing they do know.
For ten years this choir rehearses songs in this soft foreign language that sometimes manages to transport them to their place of origin: southern Africa.
8,733 kilometers away in a straight line from Heidelberg, in the northeast of the kingdom of Swaziland, there is a village called Esitjeni that depends on the voices of this German choir.
Forty percent are infected
Approximately 2,000 people live there, some still in simple mud and straw huts, a large part of them are children.
More than 300 no longer have parents because they have succumbed to AIDS.
Esitjeni is a small example of what affects Swaziland: the highest rate of AIDS infection and the lowest life expectancy around the world, according to Unicef.
The population does not see circumcision with good eyes, a practice that has been shown to halve the risk of contagion.
More than 40% of the inhabitants of Swaziland are carriers of immunodeficiency virus, and dying at just over thirty years old has become usual.
During a joint trip to Africa in the spring of 2005 the choir discovered this hidden population, but what they saw above all were many children on the streets who lacked not only the protection of their parents, but the main thing: food, clothing and training.
Without school title, the chances in a poor country are more than scarce.
At first it was only the particular commitment of a few that made it possible for a child to go to school and enjoy a hot meal a day. All this for a few euros.
However, a year later the choir founded the "Voices for Africa" association, which has since professionally dealt with AIDS orphans in Esitjeni.
Facts About Sexually Transmitted Infections
What are the main sexually transmitted diseases?
STDs of bacterial origin include syphilis, chlamydia and gonorrhea.
The most common Viral STDs are AIDS, human papillomavirus, genital herpes or hepatitis.
Crabs and scabies are among parasite-borne STDs.
What are the main groups affected?
Syphilis and gonorrhea occur most often in men who have sex with other men.
The Robert Koch Institute starts from the basis that at least four out of five cases of syphilis registered in Germany originate in sexual contact between men.
In the case of heterosexual adults the most common sexually transmitted diseases are chlamydia infections, trichomones, human papillomavirus, candidiasis (fungus) and gonorrhea.
HIV transmission among heterosexual adults in the country is relatively scarce; however, approximately 20 per cent of new HIV infections are recorded in this group.
Among young people, chlamydia infections are much more common than in other population groups.
According to European surveys, three-quarters of all infections affect young people between 15 and 25 years old.
In our country, human papillomavirus is also added to the most common diseases among youth.
How has the number of infections evolved?
Not all sexually transmitted diseases are subject to mandatory declaration.
According to the Robert Koch Institute, the number of syphilis infections has doubled from 1,697 cases in 2001 to 3,698 cases in 2011.
The number of new HIV infections has been trending since 2007.
Approximately 2,700 cases were recorded in 2011.
This is one-tenth less than in the previous year.
What symptoms indicate that it is venereal disease?
Infectious diseases can lead to ulcers in the genital area, discomfort when urinating, flow, abdominal pain, as well as blisters or warts.
They often do not cause pain or cause other symptoms and are therefore not detected.
How can one protect?
Condoms may reduce the risk of contagion, but do not offer one hundred percent protection.
Some pathogens of sexually transmitted diseases can also be transmitted through dirt infections and physical contact.
For this reason, experts recommend regular medical examinations, especially if you frequently change sexual partners.
If they are diagnosed early, most STDs can be cured and it is possible to avoid any late consequences.
Through collaborations, donations and, last but not least, the fees charged by the choir for their performances throughout Germany come to raise enough money.
"In total we have already sent about 200,000 euros to Esitjeni," said Annette Lennartz, president of the association.
In Zodwa Dlamini village itself a self-confident woman with leadership manages the money sent from Germany.
It takes care that orphans find good accommodation, for example with one of the grandmothers.
"Gogos", as older women in Zulu are called, are the pillars of the people.
Some of them have up to 14 orphans in their care, they offer them a roof to live and take care that the children go to school every day punctually and dressed in their uniforms.
Those without anyone come under the protection of Khanyisile, a single woman who, like the two cooks who are in charge of cooking every day for more than 200 hungry children, receives a salary from the association.
In addition, "Voices of Africa" has launched a sewing school, built two chicken coops and, in collaboration with the U.S
This is not obvious, since, despite how obvious this disease is throughout the country, it is not usually talked about openly.
A king with 14 wives
“AIDS is an absolute taboo,” says Annette Lennartz, “because it has to do with sexuality.”
Something strange in a country where the king officially has 14 wives.
The last absolute monarch of Black Africa, King Mswati III, is known for his opulent lifestyle and full of excesses.
Polygamy rather than democracy.
This officially unaccepted way of life is largely responsible for the rapid spread the HIV virus has experienced in recent decades.
But it’s also due to the countless seasoners who walk the country from one side to the other and who take him wherever they go.
According to Annette Lennartz, "in any corner you can get free condoms, but these are barely used.
It’s another culture, here they only accept skin-to-skin contact.”
To foster cultural exchange, the Imbongi choir travels every two to three years through southern Africa singing songs that speak of melancholy, combative spirit, hope and confidence in the black race, songs that many at the southernmost end of the African continent still remember from the apartheid era.
A bus full of whites singing songs in a black language; so much recognition not only brings with it an excellent atmosphere and a lot of joy, but has also managed to rip out a couple of tears from some other rabid border soldier.
The trip always takes them to Esitjeni, where singers visit sponsored children.
Although this small population barely appears on the maps, in the Ezulweni River valley it is well known.
"Come to Esitjeni, there the light shines," the locals say.
And after traveling the 8,733 kilometers straight back to Heidelberg to the rehearsal rooms of Bergheimer Street full of singers marking the passage, one realizes that it is also possible to see their light shine from there.
Messenger: NASA Discovers Ice on Mercury
The Messenger probe has found evidence of the existence of ice in Mercury.
The ice sheet is probably up to 20 meters thick.
NASA, the U.S. space agency, has provided evidence of the existence of ice in Mercury.
Despite being the planet closest to the sun, there is also icy water, as is apparent from three studies published on Thursday in the specialized journal "Science".
The Messenger probe would have found evidence that an ice sheet has formed in the area of the planet permanently in the shade.
It seems that it is at least 30 centimeters and probably even up to 20 meters thick.
The water comes presumably from comets or, with great probability, from asteroids that once collided with Mercury.
No one relates the discovery of ice to the possibility that life exists on the planet, says the chief investigator of the Messenger probe, Sean Solomon.
The temperature in Mercury can reach up to 426 degrees Celsius.
However, the findings could help clarify how water and other components of life have come to other areas of the solar system.
Completely inadvertentely for the inhabitants of planet Earth, the probes, telescopes and small robots such as the Phoenix are dedicated to exploring the depths of the universe.
From time to time they send images to Earth: small windows to the immensity of space.
The image comes from a camera developed by German researchers at the Max Planck Institute.
The eight planets that make up our solar system in addition to the dwarf planet Ceres.
Like Pluto, which revolves around the sun just behind Neptune, according to the new definition of the term published by the International Astronomical Union in 2006 Ceres is no "planet".
This section of an infrared image taken by the Spitzer telescope shows a "family portrait" in which infinite generations of stars appear: the oldest stars are shown as blue dots, although the most difficult thing to recognize is the pink color of "newborn" stars.
This star-forming region, unromantically referred to by W5 scientists, was discovered by the Spitzer telescope in the constellation Cassiopeia, at a distance of 6,500 light-years.
This sunny image of an extincting star was taken by NASA’s Spitzer telescope.
The doughnut-shaped ring is made up of the material the star spits out in its extinction process.
In the huge Trifid Nebula, 5,400 light-years from the Earth, new stars are formed from gas and dust.
NASA’s Spitzer telescope took this photo of the galactic star-forming zone.
The Pleiades, a group of stars also known by the nickname "The Seven Sisters", are an object visible to the naked eye in the night sky.
However, with the telescope you can better appreciate the colors.
The Helix Nebula appears as a huge red eye appears in this infrared image.
It is located 700 light-years from the constellation Aquarius.
Its similarity to an Earth's continent has earned it the nickname of North America.
The combination of normal photography and an infrared image gives rise to a spectacular color.
This budding star could be captured in all its splendor for the first time by Spitzer telescope infrared detectors.
Saturn and its rings: how they originated remains one of the great unknowns of astronomy.
It’s probably the remains of one of Saturn’s moons that disappeared without a trace of 4.5 billion years ago.
One of the largest and sharpest photographs taken by the Hubble Telescope: the Whirlpool Galaxy.
Depending on the shades, some images of spiral galaxies become true works of art.
The image published by the European Southern Observatory shows the Trifid Nebula located several thousand light-years in the constellation of Sagittarius.
The name Trifida comes from the Latin term "trifidus" (divided into three), since dark lines of dust divide the burning heart of this zone of stellar formation into three lobes.
In the constellation of Ofiucho astronomers were able to photograph signs of a cosmic carambola: 400 million light-years from Earth the nuclei of two merging galaxies pounce towards each other to end up colliding irremediably.
This star formation was captured by the Hubble telescope in the spiral galaxy M83.
Those who do not like technical abbreviations can refer to it with the nickname of Molinillo Austral.
The Hubble Space Telescope photograph shows a section of the Iris Nebula in the Cepheus constellation.
The nebula located at 1,400 light-years is composed of dust particles whose size is between ten and one hundred times smaller than that of the dust particles we can find in a house.
This image was created from optical images and X-rays made by different telescopes.
It shows a ring of black holes located 430 million light-years from Earth.
This group of galaxies, dubbed Arp 273, served for a model time for NASA’s Hubble Space Telescope.
Scientists call the larger spiral galaxy UGC 1810.
In this nebula is the group of brightest young stars in our Milky Way.
This stellar-forming zone constantly produces new stars.
In this star cloud, associated with the Rosette Nebula, new stars also continuously arise; 5,000 light-years from Earth.
In this very bright galaxy with a small black hole there's still no dust, only gas.
The researchers assume that it was formed shortly after the big bang, when in the universe you could find above all hydrogen.
Our window into the universe: the most important telescopes
It appears that the telescope was invented by Hans Lipperhey in 1608, before Galileo Galilei used it a year later to observe the stars.
Since then, the mirrors of the optical telescopes have not stopped growing and the images they offer us have more and more depth.
For 30 years, specifically from 1947 to 1975, the Hale telescope located at the Palomar Observatory near San Diego was the largest in the world.
His mirror, in the picture, had a diameter of five meters.
In Arizona, the Large Binocular Telescope is erected.
This allows to observe the cosmos through two mirrors, each with a diameter of 8.4 meters.
The interior of the Gran Telescopio Canarias located on the island of La Palma is huge - only the mirror has a diameter of 10.4 meters.
The Southern African Large Telescope mirror in South Africa is segmented to save costs.
Despite this, it reaches a diameter of approximately eleven meters.
The drawback of this more economical construction is that the telescope has a fixed elevation angle, so its mobility is limited.
The Hobby-Eberly telescope in Texas also has a fixed elevation angle.
Its peculiarity is its enormous ability to collect light.
Despite having a comparatively smaller mirror diameter, this capacity is comparable to that of the world’s largest mirror telescopes.
With the Arecibo radio telescope (Puerto Rico) the researchers can explore the universe in search of signs of extraterrestrial life.
The radio telescope has a diameter of 305 meters.
In the "Exterrestrial Intelligence Search" (SETI) you can help anyone who owns a computer, providing processing capacity.
View of the European Southern Observatory (ESO) located in the Chilean Andes.
Here rises the Very Large Telescope, a telescope that lives up to its name.
With a total of four mirrors, this telescope can also illuminate the mid-infrared spectrum.
ESO will also host the so-called E-ELT (European Extremely Large Telescope).
Its primary mirror will cover 42 meters and will consist of a total of 1,000 reflective elements.
Until 2018 it is not expected to be able to take pictures.
Until 2007 the two Keck telescopes located in the Hawaiian volcano of Mauna Kea were considered the largest in the world.
These have two mirrors, each with a diameter of 10 meters.
Keck telescopes are part of the Mauna Kea Observatory, which along with them explores the sky with the help of the Subaru telescope and the IRTTF.
In Mauna Kea it is planned to build a new telescope of enormous proportions and with a mirror diameter of thirty meters.
This is a picture of what it would be like.
However, the most important information about the cosmos comes from the hand of the Hubble Space Telescope.
Since April 24, 1990, it has sent images of distant worlds.
Since March 2009, the Kepler Space Telescope has been looking for extrasolar planets, mainly those that are habitable.
On February 2, 2011, NASA announced that 1,235 candidates for planets had been located since the start of its mission.
The image shows the last preparations prior to the launch of the Kepler space telescope.
The James Webb Space Telescope (JWST) won’t launch into space aboard an Ariane 5 rocket until 2018.
The primary mirror of this space telescope has a diameter of 6.5 meters.
One of the functions of this telescope will be to look for the light of the first stars and galaxies formed behind the big bang.
The researchers start from the base that at Mercury’s south pole there’s also ice.
However, there is no reliable evidence, since the Messenger probe revolves around the planet in an orbit much closer to the north pole.
Radar measurements have been pointing to the existence of ice in Mercury for decades.
Thanks to the Messenger (2004) probe, the first that orbits around Mercury, now scientists are sure it is so.
Drinking butter every day and living 168 years
To the south of Azerbaijan many people reach biblical ages.
There is even a museum dedicated to longevity.
A search for footprints in a country in which 97-year-olds are still considered young.
To the south of Azerbaijan many people reach biblical ages.
There is even a museum dedicated to longevity.
A search for footprints in a country in which 97-year-olds are still considered young.
The tour of the mountainous region of Talysh could be described as savage and romantic.
The minibus advances with great noise through the sinuous road that passes next to wooded hills, rivers of great flow and the simple houses of local farmers.
Everything is green and juicy, you might even think you’re in the Black Forest.
However, we are in the southernmost area of Azerbaijan; from here the border with Iran is just a few kilometres away.
Here live the "talyshi", a town that is not known much, apart from that they dominate perfectly Persian and Azeri and that they are very longing.
Our final destination is Lerik.
This small town boasts perhaps too mastic architecture designed in the Soviet era that doesn’t seem to fit at all into such a quaint mountainous landscape.
European tourists barely reach this place, since the road from the capital of Azerbaijan, Baku, is too heavy.
To travel the 323 kilometers that separate both urban centers, it takes eight hours because the road is one-way.
The astonishing wealth that the country enjoys thanks to Caspian oil has not yet reached this hidden region.
However, Pilata Fatulayeva (48) is convinced that Lerik has what it takes to become a tourist attraction.
"Baku became famous in May on the occasion of the celebration of the Eurovision Song Contest and next year we will celebrate the Most Elderly People Festival in the world," Fatulayeva said.
It is the director of the Museum of Longevity, perhaps the only one that exists in the world.
Here the lives of eight dozes of talyshis of the place that have exceeded 100 years of age is documented. Fatulayeva points to a black-and-white image.
That's my grandpa, who lived 120 years.
A son of his died at the age of 136
However, the undisputed star of the museum is sheepherder Şirali Müslümov, who appears to have lived 168 years.
However, there is no birth certificate certifying it.
And if we take into account that the documented older man lived 122 years, it seems that M�üslümov's age is quite suspicious.
“He was born in the region in 1805 and died in 1973,” Fatulayeva explains.
The man was married on three occasions and had 23 sons. Apparently he still had time to conceive one more daughter at the age of 136.
Has Şirali Müslümov then been wrong in his age in a couple of decades?
However, Rembrandt Scholz, a gerontologist at the Max Planck Institute in Rostock, has also heard of this Central Asian’s advanced age.
"In some regions of China, Japan or the Hunza valley in Pakistan there are also very elderly people," Scholz says, "even in Sardinia there are many older men."
Due to the lack of documentation, it is impossible to provide scientific evidence about age; especially when there is no civil registration.
Every day glasses filled with melted butter
It is a fact that, surprisingly frequently, the inhabitants of the region around Lerik usually reach a biblical age.
There are currently 20 people over 100 years of age.
Why do so many elderly people live here in the south?
Azerbaijani tour guide Farid Mugimzadeh explains that it is the special genes of the talyshis.
The director of the museum, Ms. Fatulayeva, believes instead that the cause resides in food.
However, from a nutritional point of view, it does not seem logical that precisely the high-calorie diet of the talyshi, who love meat, bread and especially dairy products and many of whom, in addition, drink a glass of melted butter daily, is so healthy.
Or maybe it’s the natural way of life that keeps people young? In Cengemiran, a small settlement not far from the city of Lerik lives Rubaba Mirzayeva.
At 97 he is relatively young for what is styled by these latitudes.
Mirzayeva, who claims to have 143 descendants, lives in a simple wooden house typical of the entire Caucasus region.
Sitting on the floor, it tirelessly moves a huge butter tub.
Eight people live under a roof, including one of Mirzayeva’s sons and a daughter, both became grandparents long ago.
They also jump back and forth two small children.
In the kitchen they prepare tea for the guests, a tea that they serve in typical glasses with bulging shape.
Mirzayeva’s teeth are white and perfectly lined up and under the handkerchief hides two long braids of dark blonde hair that her son proudly uncovers us.
I've always washed my hair with milk, that's why I've never fallen out and it's maintained its color.
“I haven’t ever used a shampoo either,” Mirzayeva says.
The pension is enough to live
He has always eaten only what he produced in his own farm: tomatoes, potatoes and peas.
All my life I've never gone shopping for a supermarket.
Then she tells us about her husband, who was in the army.
The worst was the time after World War II.
However, everything returned to his course when the "dear father", Heydar Aliyew, took control of the situation.
This kind of propaganda sounds weird in the mouth of an older woman.
However, in Azerbaijan the cult professing to the paternal figure of the nation, who directs his country as a dictator, knows no limits.
He remained in power until 2003, at which point he ceded the witness to his son Ilham.
At least the elderly in Azerbaijan don’t know what poverty is.
Mirzayeva charges 230 manat (approximately the same amount in euros) in monthly pension, an amount with which you can live well taking into account the situation here.
And perhaps Mirzayeva's gray son is right: "in our culture the elders are highly respected."
They live in a great family, they are loved, they receive all the necessary care and they are happy.
As if this wasn't reason enough to stay alive many years...
In the Constitution the expression "Human Rights" is missing
The revolution has returned to Cairo.
Demonstrations that rival each other in Cairo highlight the tremendous division in which the country is plunged.
The future constitution, with Sharia as a basis, is very controversial.
The Egyptian president spared no pathetism.
“We must overcome the transition.
And that happens is my responsibility, before the people and before God," he said on public television.
His speech was addressed to all the people, especially the Coptic Christians, liberals, enlightened Muslims, and seculars.
All of them, hopelessly plunged so far into confused opposition, are afraid.
Fear of a Nile theocracy for the grace of the mighty Muslim community.
As Mohammed Morsi explains, almost asking for forgiveness, he has had to temporarily annul the competences of the Constitutional Court and expand his "in order to save the revolution".
However, neither the Egyptians nor the entire world know very well what this 61-year-old engineer with a doctoral degree awarded by the University of Southern California really wants to save.
Do you intend to deprive the judiciary of your power?
In fact, the 234 articles, whose approval has been forced by a constituent assembly composed of 100 predominantly Islamist members at a 15-hour night meeting, are truly worrying.
As in previous constitutions, the draft also states that jurisprudence is based on the “principles of Islamic law.”
However, what does that mean of "principles"?
This was and remains open to interpretation and it seems that the Islamists will take advantage of this vague formulation, as well as the margin of legal manoeuvre it carries, in order to impose a stricter interpretation of Sharia.
This is at least what is suggested by an added new article: in all matters relating to Sharia it is obligatory to consult Al-Ashar University, the country’s most important Islamic institution with great power throughout Sunni Islamism.
It doesn’t have to be like this, but this may mean that the clergy would control the legislation, something that – in fact – would result in the incapacitation of the judiciary.
Much of the draft constitution is open to interpretation
What is also problematic is that it intends to maintain military jurisdiction.
During Mubarak’s dictatorship these courts served to exert a harsh crackdown on the opposition.
After the fall of the dictator up to 11,000 civilians were detained in military prisons.
As the draft states, the state must protect "the true essence of the Egyptian family" and "promote its morality and its values".
It is such an imprecise formulation in legal terms that with the help of this article state institutions could even control the contents of cinematography and literature.
Speaking of course, it’s nothing but censorship.
Of course, no article explicitly establishes equality between men and women.
Instead another forbids offending or slandering the Prophet Mohammed and his legacies.
However, it is not clear what an offense is and how it should be sanctioned.
Equally doubtful is the formulation prohibiting the "offense for people"
Is it enough with a cartoon of the president or a joke about a lawyer?
Question of interpretation, like many other things in the draft that has been submitted to Morsi for signature and that according to his words, "very soon" will be presented to the Egyptians to be submitted to a referendum.
"Revolution has returned"
The opposition has been closing ranks in the face of the superiority of the Islamists for weeks.
Ten thousand people gathered in an unusual harmony on Friday afternoon in Tahrir Square of the Cario and swore to make the Act fail before it can take effect.
"The revolution has returned and we will end up winning," says Hamdin Sabbahi, who ranks third in the presidential election lists.
The Nobel Peace Laureate and former director of the International Atomic Energy Agency, Mohamed El-Baradei, declared that the draft constitution should be "in the garbage can of history."
Through Twitter he accused Morsi fans of wanting to lead a “coup against democracy.”
"When he convenes the referendum, we will go to his palace and overthrow him," says opponent Jasser Said.
"We'ren't tired yet, the blood of our brothers hasn't yet been expiated," opposition politician Chaled Ali was summoned in Egyptian media.
And several judges announced that they did not wish to oversee the referendum, so it would not be considered valid.
"The Qur'an is our constitution"
For its part, the well-organized Muslim community called for a counter-manifestation; just in case not in Tahrir Square, but after a prayer on the other side of the Nile, before the University of Cairo.
Many women covered with a veil and supporters of the Salafists took part in it to the cry of: "the people demand the application of the divine law."
Morsi was demanded to "cleanse the country", at the same time proclaiming: "the Qur'an is our constitution."
A struggle for the sovereignty of the symbolic Tahrir square, where it all began, would have provoked a situation similar to that of a civil war.
It is clear that Morsi’s supporters didn’t want to risk something like this happening.
The Muslim brothers claimed that both those who are opposed to the draft constitution and supporters had expressed their views loud and clear.
Now the time would have come to let the people decide at the polls what direction the country should follow.
In this sense, it seems that the draft of the Islamists is guaranteed the majority.
"The expression 'Human Rights' doesn't even appear"
Hafez Abu Saeda is furious with this forced constitutional process that should actually have lasted until February and included all social forces.
This 48-year-old human rights lawyer and president of the Egyptian Human Rights Organization (EOHR) defended the Muslim brothers when they were held in prison or had to face trial during the Mubarak dictatorship.
Not because he shared his ideology, but because human rights are indivisible to him.
For this reason he was beaten, convicted and imprisoned.
"And now the expression `Human Rights' doesn't even appear in the new constitution," he laments in a conversation with the newspaper "Welt am Sonntag."
The expansion of Morsi’s powers to the three powers of state caused this lawyer to resign himself.
The measures adopted are flagrant violations of the rules of the democratic game and will lead Egypt to a new dictatorship.
"Instead of strengthening civil society, what the president has done is to abolish it," Saeda laments.
However, democracy cannot function without civil society organizations.
Saeda feels abandoned, also by the international community, which observes the ideological struggle that is taking place on the banks of the Nile with a mixture of curiosity and tension.
This could turn against him.
A protester in Tahrir Square warns: "you're conceiving a monster you won't be able to control."
Norwegian Rakfisk: the world’s most smelly fish?
Norway’s five million people enjoy one of the highest standards of living, not only in Europe but in the world.
Is it possible that the success of the country is related to its avidity for an extremely smelly fish?
Take a series of excessively cured cheeses.
Place them in the middle of a mountain of dirty and wet football clothes.
Just leave it there for a week.
You can then smell the paralyzing aroma of rakfisk, one of the delicacies of Norway.
I am in the small town of Fagernes, about three hours from Oslo.
It's snowy. The landscape is spectacular and that smell, always present, floats in the air.
Rakfisk is salting trout that is allowed to ferment in water during, depending on how smelly you prefer, up to a year.
As it darkens and gets colder, the Norwegians flock to a festival held here in Fagernes, dedicated to this delicacy, so to speak, captivating.
"You eat raw and then you drink an aquavit glass of a drink," says Havard Halvarsen, a city firefighter who is also what is known as the "Rakfisk General", who is in charge of organizing the festival.
Everywhere we see people eating this fish that is kept in small cubes and drinking from a drink significant amounts of the drink.
"Some people like aquavit more than rakfisk," Havard says.
Drinking can kill the smell.
I'll try a few pieces.
If you can avoid passing it near the nose, it’s not bad; it’s not very different than a portion of sushi you’ve traveled a few miles by bus.
Rakfisk is a product that comes from a very different era, in which poverty plagued Norway and in which, before refrigeration existed, fish was stored during the autumn submerged in water and salt in airtight barrels.
Later, in the middle of winter, when it is well-fermented, it is removed and, evidently with the inactive senses due to alcohol, it is eaten.
Just one generation ago, thousands of Norwegians were forced to leave their country in search of work, and emigrated mainly to the United States.
Currently, the population is increasing rapidly; more than 13% are immigrants, attracted by the abundance of jobs, high wages and a complete system of social services.
A stream of inhabitants of Sweden, the old rival and even too much richer than Norway, arrives in this country to work.
Rakfisk is considered something important, an essential, albeit quite smelly, part of Norway’s past.
It is among the most expensive dishes that can be purchased.
But, in fact, everything is expensive. A beer cane or a sandwich costs you a whopping €10 ($14) each.
Norway does not usually appear on the agenda of international news and most Norwegians like it to.
Here people are still unwilling to pronounce the name of Anders Breivik, the far-right racist who shot and killed 77 people, including men, women and children.
Instead, they refer to the shooting as "the event of July 22."
The Norwegians find it very hard to believe that in their peaceful country one of their own was able to commit such cruelty and murder.
After much of Norway’s current wealth, there has been the growth, from the early 70’s, of one of the world’s largest oil and gas industries.
"But oil isn't the only reason we're doing so well," says Anna, our waitress, as she spends rakfisk trays and, with her long blonde mane and surprising blue eyes, represents the image of Norwegian well-being.
"We are, as you say, prudent people."
Its English, like that of most people in this country, is impeccable.
"We don't like bragging, we don't like ostentation."
Norway has managed its oil wealth very cautiously; everything, except a small percentage of the money from this industry, is invested in a special fund for the benefit of future generations.
While everyone was wasting money they didn’t have during the years preceding the financial crisis around the world, Norway kept the wallet tight.
“As long as we can ski in winter and hiking in summer, we’re happy,” Anna says.
"And eat rakfisk," he adds with an unwrapped laughter.
I'm in the snow queuing to eat something; I've had enough rakfisk.
Now a moose burger is definitely something different and quite succulent to savor.
But at night, that smelly fish comes back.
The hotel in which I stay is one of the many venues where a rakfisk dinner is held in which diners vote the best fish, or perhaps the one that most tests the nostrils.
A TV has connected live with a presenter who wears a bow tie and is surrounded by rakfisk dishes.
Looks like the Eurovision contest.
"What are your scores for the best fish upstairs in the mountains, Thor-Juergen?"
“Here’s our score, Havard.”
Laughter and applause.
A man falls out of the chair, perhaps vanquished by aquavit.
Or maybe it's the vapors of all that fish.
Mexico: Enrique Peña Nieto faces a tough start
As incoming Mexican President Enrique Peña Nieto prepares to take office, BBC correspondent Will Grant discusses the challenges facing the new president and the different expectations of its population.
Traffic in Mexico City is especially bad today.
In a city that, in the best circumstances, is already congested, a steel encirclement has been installed since Monday that cuts off several of the main roads that lead to the city and that is causing enormous chaos on the roads.
The objective, however, was not to prevent workers from reaching their workplace, but to prevent protesters from reaching parliament.
On Saturday, the new president of Mexico, Enrique Peña Nieto, will receive the presidential band and assume the management of the nation.
He is faced with a complicated task.
Mexico has obtained good economic results during the outgoing government of Felipe Calderón, but the country is immersed in a war against drug trafficking that has already claimed about 60,000 lives in six years.
“My administration has a great commitment to the Mexican people to reduce the violence,” Mr. Peña Nieto told U.S. President Barack Obama earlier this week in the Oval Office.
"I will propose a new security strategy that will allow us to achieve that goal."
Before rubbing shoulders with the U.S. president, Mr. Peña’s previous political experience was as governor of his home state, that of Mexico.
A very populous state with rapid growth, which surrounds the capital; opinions about the new leader are divided into this territory in which he used to move.
A sincere man
In the bucolic city of Valle del Bravo, for example, he is fondly remembered.
Its residents attribute the merit of the growth of tourism in the place and the construction of infrastructure.
To get to the city you can drive on one of the new roads of Mr. Peña Nieto, which is a huge improvement compared to the cracked and bumpy roads that were rehabilitated.
You can also see plaques with its name at the entrance of a modern sports center and an impressive interactive museum on climate change.
"We are confident that he can make a real and lasting change," says his friend and political ally Gabriel Olvera Hernandez, deputy of Mr. Peña Nieto's party, the PRI.
"Particularly, when it comes to security and economics, we look forward to the interesting and real change our country so badly needs."
After an uninterrupted period of 81 years in power, the PRI was ousted by Vicente Fox in 2000.
Deputy Olvera admits that, after 12 years outside the presidential palace of Los Pinos, within the party there are many expectations regarding Enrique Peña Nieto.
It also rejects the qualification that the opposition lacks the essence of the new president.
"He is a very sincere man, very committed and with an excellent view of the country.
He’s a fantastic statesman and above all, he’s a person who knows how to listen.”
However, on the other side of the state, that’s not the impression many people have about their former governor.
In Nezahualcoyotl, also known as Ciudad Neza, the contrast with the cobbled streets of Valle del Bravo could not be greater.
Hidden under stretches of elevated highway is, in many ways, a suburb of Mexico City itself.
And the problems of the municipality are also hard and urban.
Earlier this year the military was called upon to assist in the fight against drug gangs operating in neighbourhoods and violence against women is particularly serious.
In a desert plot, located next to a garbage dump, the bodies of dozens of murdered women have been abandoned in just the last two years.
More than 1,000 women were killed in the state of Mexico while Mr. Peña Nieto was the governor, a much higher rate than that of Ciudad Juarez, unfortunately famous for his violence and a place that is synonymous with the murder of innocent women.
Critics of Mr. Peña Nieto say, at best, that he did not know how to address the problem of femicide while he was in office.
The most critics accuse his administration of turning a blind eye.
In a concrete house, typical of the deteriorating neighborhood, Irinea Buendía has to make efforts to hold back tears while showing me photographs of her deceased daughter, Mariana Luna.
According to the official version of events, Mariana committed suicide in 2010.
However, her family thinks her partner murdered her.
"When I came home, it seemed as if his body had been cleansed," Mrs. Buendía recalls.
"There were signs that she had been beaten and the rigor mortis had already appeared."
While his mother tells the story, a photograph of Mariana observes from the walls, next to a cross in which a single word is read: Justice.
However, that’s exactly what the family say has been denied.
"The state authorities have treated me like I'm an old gossip, a troublemaker, a whimper.
What they want is for you to simply accept what they are saying and to shut up.”
"But that can't be like that when there have been so many irregularities and so much negligence," he says.
The presidential band that President Peña Nieto will receive on Saturday will be accompanied by enormous responsibility.
Tens of thousands of families have been affected by violent crimes in Mexico over the past six years and the new president has vowed to give them priority during his tenure.
"I hope he's the same kind of president he was when he was governor," says PRI deputy Olvera in Valle del Bravo.
However, that is precisely what the families of the victims of Ciudad Neza most fear.
Bradley Manning did not report ill-treatment, prosecutors say
Prosecutors try to deal with Bradley Manning’s allegations of abuses during his stay in prison
Trial focuses on Manning's period at Quantico Military Prison in Virginia
The defense wants the case to be dismissed based on Manning’s imprisonment being severe
The army soldier is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents.
Prosecutors tried to prove Friday that soldier Bradley Manning, accused of the largest leak of confidential information in U.S. history, missed multiple chances of denouncing ill-treatment he now alleges he suffered while under military detention.
During the prosecution's interrogation of Manning at a pre-trial hearing in Ft. Meade, Maryland, Attorney Commander Ashden Fein claimed that records of the weekly visits by officers of the unit that Manning received during nine months of detention in Quantico, Virginia, do not show any complaints about the treatment he received.
The interrogation, which was carried out during a hearing in the wake of the defence's request to dismiss the case since its incarceration had been harsh and that this had already meant sufficient punishment, came a day after Manning had testified that he had thought of suicide while in detention.
The army intelligence analyst, arrested in June 2010, is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents while on duty in Iraq.
Later, WikiLeaks published the material online.
WikiLeaks has never confirmed that Manning was the source of information.
At Friday’s hearing, Fein reviewed with Manning the forms officers filled out after visits to Manning during his arrest at Quantico military prison, where he was jailed for maximum imprisonment from July 2010 to April 2011.
Officers would've asked Manning questions and noted his answers.
When Fein asked about the forms on Friday, Manning acknowledged that he had described the treatment given by the guards as "excellent" and the treatment received in the facilities in general as "very professional".
The forms do not show any allegations of ill-treatment, even though officers asked Manning directly about his treatment, Fein said.
Manning replied that he had expressed his concern about some issues and that the officers who were visiting him had talked about these problems and had indicated to him that they would be addressed, but that they did not record these issues in the register.
“They wrote ‘without incident’ (after talking about the problems) but this doesn’t necessarily mean that I wouldn’t have mentioned any problems,” Manning said.
Army Colonel Denise Lind also asked Manning why she had not denounced the treatment she received during the January 2011 meeting with advice to examine the suicide intentions she had expressed on a form months earlier.
Manning replied that his intention during that meeting was to obtain the reduction of the "injury prevention" regimen under which he was.
The military said it had placed him under that restrictive regime, which is one degree below that of surveillance for possible suicide, to ensure his protection and the safety of other people.
"I wanted the staff to know that I was fine and (wanted) to stop being under injury prevention ... so I could enjoy what, from my point of view, is a better quality of life," Manning said.
Manning testified Thursday about his arrest in Iraq and his transfer to Kuwait, where he was held for nearly two months before being transferred to the military prison at the Marine base in Quantico, Virginia, in July 2010.
He said that he had considered committing suicide in Kuwait and once lost consciousness due to the heat.
He said the fact that he was not allowed to know what was happening to him or what was going on in the outside world was very distressing.
"My world was reduced only to Camp Arafjon, to that cage," Manning said Thursday.
“I thought I was going to die in that cage.”
Manning claimed that, once moved to Quantico, he spent most days in a small cell, at least for 21 hours and often for 23 hours, without company.
Manning said that he was only allowed to have a mattress, a blanket, flip flops, some clothes and his glasses.
He explained that he was trying to move all the time, as it was forbidden to sleep or even lie down during the day.
Manning claimed that he always had a light coming from outside pointing to his eyes while he was sleeping.
He said that if the guards couldn't see his face when he turned around at night, they would wake him up so that he would turn around again.
Manning’s lawyer filed in January 2011 a formal claim on Manning’s treatment.
Manning was transferred to Fort Leavenwort military prison in Kansas in April 2011.
On Friday the judge asked Manning about an allegation she had made in Thursday’s statement that, after being forced one night to sleep naked in her Quantico cell, she was forced during a morning recount to stand naked in front of guards and other prisoners.
Manning had testified that he had never been given the opportunity to cover with the blanket during counting.
During the interrogation of the judge on Friday, Manning said he deduced because of the order given to him by the guard who should drop the blanket he might have covered with, but acknowledged that no one had ordered him to drop it.
Manning testified Thursday that he had been forced to sleep naked the night before because of an attempt to prove to an officer that he posed no danger to himself.
Manning claimed that he had told the officer that he could have used the elastic of his underwear or his flip-flops to self-harm but that he hadn’t.
Manning testified that his underwear, flip-flops and glasses had been removed from his cell that night.
Her lawyers are hopeful that the judge will at least take into account the experiences she went through during her stay in prison and drastically reduce her sentence in the event that she was convicted in the war council, which is scheduled to start early next year.
The defense said it plans to make Manning plead guilty to misdemeanors and defend other allegations deemed too extreme.
The hearing is scheduled to resume this weekend and prosecutors are expected to argue that the conditions of detention were justified.
The Pentagon has maintained that Manning was held in accordance with the rules of application to any detainee in maximum custody in Quantico.
Charges against Manning include cooperation with the enemy, illegally provoking the publication of information on the Internet, leakage of national defense information and theft of property or public records.
If found guilty of all charges, he could face life in prison.
My Mexican-American Identity Crisis
He claims that many were forced to leave Mexico due to a lack of opportunities
Mexicans tend to criticize those who left; they say they remind Mexicans of those difficult times
Navarrette comments that Mexican-Americans are trapped between two worlds.
On a recent trip to Mexico City, he had barely gone down the esplanade and arrived in the immigration processing area and was already totally confused.
Some posters indicated the path to two queues: one for "Mexicans" and one for "Foreigners".
I stayed there a few seconds without being sure where I should go.
Growing up in Central California, my entire life I’ve been called “Mexican.”
It’s about ethnic tachygraphy, just as my Boston friends refer to themselves as “Irish” or my New York friends call themselves “Italians.”
Later I decided on "Mexican-American".
But this was Mexico.
And, in my grandfather's homeland, shorthand or screenwriters weren't necessary.
He was simply an American.
I'm talking Spanish. Well enough to handle myself during an interview in this language.
But I don’t have a native’s vocabulary level and I can’t get rid of my American accent.
So I took my U.S. passport and put myself in the queue of foreigners.
I thought at the time this week when Mexican President-elect Enrique Peña Nieto visited the White House to meet with President Obama.
On the agenda of the day, as long as the leaders of these two countries meet, it consisted: immigration, drugs and trade.
Peña Nieto was also looking forward to the growth of the Mexican economy, one of the reasons why at the moment it is as likely that a Mexican will stay in Mexico as he ventures to go to the United States.
He wants to partner with the United States and Canada and create in North America a European Union-style trading block.
In addition, Peña Nieto vowed to continue fighting the drug cartels in Mexico, although he did not give details.
For Mexico, the relationship with the United States is complicated and is full of rancor.
Probably most Americans never remember that in 1848 the United States invaded Mexico and forced its leaders to cede half of their territory at gunpoint.
But for Mexicans, who think in centuries, not in minutes, the elements that remind them are everywhere.
So, at the moment when a U.S. official says anything that entails the slightest criticism of Mexico, you begin to hear complaints, both in the press and among the Mexican elite, about how Americans are usurping the sovereignty of their neighbors.
And the sons of Moctezuma are put on war.
And yet, for Mexico, the relationship that really poses quite a challenge is what they have with the more than 35 million Mexican-Americans who live in the United States.
Shall we talk about rancor?
There's a lot.
In Mexico there are winners and losers, people for whom the country provides opportunities and others for which it is not.
The only reason why there are so many people of Mexican descent living in cities such as Los Angeles, Las Vegas, Phoenix, Denver or San Antonio is that, somewhere in our family tree, there was a person, maybe the father or mother or one of the grandparents, to whom the door of opportunities was closed in Mexico and had to march north.
And most of the time that person fitted into a certain profile: dark skin, a low level of studies, coming from a poor people, etc.
We are your descendants and we are loyal to you.
Not to Mexico.
And, although maybe we are living now the American dream, having gone to good schools and having had good jobs, we can never lose sight of what we are experiencing is that, the American dream, and not the Mexican one.
Sometimes our identity may not be clear, but our allegiance is.
It's for the United States.
In addition, we are aware that many Mexicans belonging to the elites of the ruling class do not like us.
Feeling is mutual.
They see us as a reminder of a humiliating defeat and they look over our shoulders like we’re from an inferior category that isn’t Mexican enough.
Our Spaniard will never be good enough and our ties to Mexico will never be strong enough.
In his view, our existence is synonymous with failure.
If our families hadn’t failed in Mexico, they wouldn’t have left.
And now we wouldn't find ourselves trapped behind this silk curtain, living well in the United States but, still, as lonely souls.
My wife, who was born in Guadalajara and came legally to the United States when she was a child, reminds me that there is this friction between Mexicans and Mexican-Americans because the former know more solidly who they are and the latter this annoys them.
Although she is a U.S. citizen, she is considered part of the two countries.
In the meantime, many Mexican-Americans I know feel they’re not part of either.
We love to hear the Mexican group The Northern Tigers, but also Bruce Springsteen.
You've got the best of each country, but you're not rooted in any of them.
They see us as Americans in Mexico.
And in the United States we are considered Mexicans.
Now, to complicate things even more, so I’ve found out during my trip, some Mexican leaders and some intellectuals want to reconnect with the diaspora.
They want Mexican-U.S. people to work as improvised “ambassadors” of Mexico, representing their interests in the United States.
We would tell our fellow Americans how fantastic it is to visit that country and put pressure on political leaders to strengthen their ties with Mexico.
Yes, of course...
That won't happen.
There's too much rancor.
And, with wage inequality, galloping corruption and drug violence, many of us aren’t so sure it’s a fantastic country.
I'm afraid you're going to be left alone with this idea, friends.
It's fair.
If some Mexicans are not yet prepared to forgive the U.S. for how they treated Mexico a century and a half ago, then they have to accept the fact that some Mexican-Americans are still resentful of how their relatives were treated much less.
Hmmm.
Maybe we're more "Mexican" than I thought.
Old Battles, New Middle East
The ceasefire between Israel and Hamas may well be an unlikely basis for peace
Is there one day a lasting peace between Arabs and Jews in the Middle East?
Another bloodshed that seems to indicate that any hope is in vain.
Amid useless and usual discussions about who started first, piles of buildings have been reduced to rubble; more than 140 Palestinians, most of them civilians, and six Israelis have been killed and, for the first time, missiles coming from Gaza have fallen near Tel Aviv, Israel’s metropolis, and the holy city of Jerusalem.
But, even though Israelis and Palestinians appear to be anchored in their former conflict, everything that surrounds them in the Middle East is changing.
The Arab Spring has left everything to pieces whether they like it or not, Palestinians and Israelis have been trapped in the regional uproar.
Perhaps this will make your fight even bloodier than before.
However, there is reason to think that his lethal stalemate could unravel.
A war that is neither lost nor won
At first glance, optimism now seems very difficult to justify.
Even if the ceasefire agreed on Nov. 21 remains, this week’s clashes have bolstered hawks on both sides.
The leaders of Hamas, the Islamist movement that has ruled Gaza since 2007, will claim to have forced the Israelis to retreat, even though Gaza has been beaten well.
And despite having killed some of its leaders and put the 1.7 million inhabitants of Gaza under pressure in one of the most miserable and populated corners on the planet, Israel has not been able to wipe out Hamas.
In fact, Hamas is gaining ground in the West Bank, the other part of Palestine currently governed by its relentless Fatah rivals, the most moderate Palestinian faction.
In addition, Hamas leaders could come to the conclusion that time plays in their favor.
Since the Islamists have been gaining influence all over the Arab world, Hamas has made rich and powerful friendships.
Turkey, a renascent regional power that had previously been Israel’s closest Muslim ally, has embraced the cause of Hamas. So has Qatar, one of the richest and most dynamic states in the Gulf.
Exultant members of Hamas say the Islamist crescent is encircling Israel, from Lebanon to the north, where the Hezbollah-militia party dominates, passing through Syria, where rebels of an increasingly Islamist trend could overthrow Bashar Assad, until they reach Jordan, where Hamas allies are threatening the king.
Especially on the southern flank of Israel, the growth of the Muslim Brotherhood led by President Muhammad Morsi in Egypt, by far the most populous and fundamental of the Arab countries, has changed the balance of the region.
Hosni Mubarak, the lay despot who ruled in Egypt for 30 years until his fall in 2011, didn’t have too much time for Hamas.
Instead, the Muslim Brotherhood are Hamas-brother cousins and their leaders depend more on popular opinion.
In future diplomatic actions, Hamas may emerge as a protagonist who cannot be excluded, not even Israel and the United States.
In the meantime, Israel’s hard wing will draw the opposite conclusion.
As far as the military issue is concerned, they have put Hamas in place.
Israel’s Iron Dome anti-missile system has proven effective and many of Hamas’ missiles have been destroyed.
The Israelis will sleep more peacefully for some time.
Regarding diplomacy, the U.S. is stronger than ever; many European countries also blamed Hamas for starting the latest wave of violence.
Above all, Israel has prospered. Especially under the rule of Benjamin Netanyahu, a prime minister who has largely ignored the peace process.
Although rockets fired from Gaza have killed about 30 Israelis since 2004, Israel has not had too many suicide bombers, in part thanks to the West Bank barrier, the main piece of an aspiring territory to become a state of Palestine, and it protects Jewish settlements that continue to rise even though, under international law, they are illegal.
Mr. Netanyahu, whose Likud party has joined an even tougher-line group led by Avigdor Lieberman in the run-up to elections on Jan. 22, is in a good position.
Why pamper those twisted Palestinians giving them their own state?
If they really ruled in the West Bank, wouldn't they fire rockets just like their compatriots have done in Gaza?
It's better to have them behind that wall and give them a good blow if they raise their heads.
The hard wing may be imposed, although the Arab Spring could alter its calculations.
Although Islamists who take power in Egypt and elsewhere don’t have too much appreciation for Israel, their priority will be to address internal difficulties.
Israel’s defense budget is greater than that of its four Arab neighbors together.
Starting a war with the superpower of the area will hardly help the new Arab governments to improve their economies.
That Pragmatist Mr. Morsi has worked with Barack Obama to get the cease-fire is a good omen and could mark the beginning of something.
Israelis should also think long-term.
With the rest of the Arab world becoming more democratic, depriving Palestinians of their right to self-determination means creating a duster that will one day explode in the territories occupied by Israel, in the same way that this week a bus exploded in Tel Aviv.
Repression is already undermining democracy in the Jewish state and demography worsens the situation as the Arab population increases.
Breast missions against Gaza every two to three years to reject Hamas will pay an ever-increasing diplomatic toll.
Both sides need a push of people outside the conflict.
The solution remains the one proclaimed by sensible people from both sides, the majority from the outside world and this newspaper: two states and the cession of territory by Israel for security reasons.
Hope, small in the short term, is that the ceasefire will give a little more momentum to outsiders who are fighting for this cause.
Egypt, which must begin curbing the entry of arms into Gaza, along with Turkey and Qatar, is in an unprecedented situation to convince Hamas to accept the idea of a Jewish state based on the 1967 borders, with land exchanges and a shared Jerusalem.
Arabs oblivious to the issue should also pressure Hamas and Fatah to come together.
That would contribute more to the creation of a Palestinian state than the impending attempt to obtain a virtual status category at the UN.
Mr. Obama also has an important role when it comes to bringing Israel to the negotiating table.
During his first tenure he omitted to present his own plan for peace.
Again in the White House, he seems just as reluctant to devote his attention.
This attitude is a deplorable vision of the future.
A stable Middle East is of vital interest to the United States.
This implies a peace agreement between Israel and Palestine.
Generic tobacco packaging law goes into effect in Australia
Warnings about the risks of smoking and diseased body parts look on packs of a dull green color, which are the same for all tobacco brands
The Australian law on tobacco and generic packaging, a pioneer in the world, enters into force and replacing the logos and colors of the brands with a wrapping of a muted olive green, appalling images of body parts with diseases and representations of sick children and babies because they are children of smoking parents.
With the exception of the different images and health warnings, the only difference between the packs, mandatory since Saturday, are the names of the brands, all of them printed in a type of small font.
It is the strictest regime in the world applied to tobacco packaging.
Australia’s federal government claims that the goal is to deter young people from smoking by removing traits that make this habit glamoure.
Rely on studies showing that if a person hasn’t started smoking before the age of 26, there’s a 99% chance they’ll never.
"You can see that boys, even from an early age, understand the message that the tobacco company is trying to sell through its brand," Federal Health Minister Tanya Plibersek said, citing studies showing, for example, how girls linked the logo of a wreath to the idea of being a princess.
Although Australia has one of the world’s lowest smoking rates and these changes won’t affect the benefits of multinationals too much, other countries are considering taking similar action.
The tobacco industry has exerted enormous pressure against the law.
Tobacco companies claimed that the law would encourage the black market for tobacco, in which cigarettes would be cheaper and more accessible.
"Legislation will have serious involuntary consequences," said Scott McIntyre of the British American Tobacco in Australia.
It will increase the amount of tobacco that counterfeiters from China and Indonesia will bring to sell it on the streets of Australia.
Other people say their business has benefited following the passage of the law.
Sandra Ha of Zico Import Pty Ltd, a small family-owned company, claimed that the demand for pitilles and silicone covers to cover up the nasty images of the packs had skyrocketed almost two months ago, when the British American Tobacco, Britain's Imperial Tobacco, Philip Morris and Japan Tobacco lost a recusal to the law in the Supreme Court of Australia.
Ms. Ha stated that Zico had sold up to 6,000 units to wholesale establishments and was waiting for more stocks.
“It’s a good deal for us.”
According to the experts, the possible obstacle is the popularity of social networks among the demographic sector to which the plan is directed.
Following a series of Australian laws banning television advertising and sports sponsorship and forcing most sellers not to put cigarettes in sight, tobacco marketing has gone online.
Australia has banned Internet advertising to local companies and websites, but cannot restrict it on foreign pages.
“If you’re marketing tobacco and you just have this little window left to promote your products, the online space is the most compelling place to be,” said Becky Freeman, a public health researcher at the University of Sydney.
Freeman noted an increase in reviews of brands coming from "walking citizens" posted on social media websites such as YouTube, Twitter and Facebook.
You might wonder if it’s just a private citizen who really loves Marlboro cigarettes and has bothered to make a video or if any marketing companies are behind it.
Australia’s British American Tobacco indicated that the industry focuses more on what new regulations entail than on marketing.
Industry has even paid for Ukraine, Honduras and the Dominican Republic to recuse the new rules; these countries are asserting to the World Trade Organization that trade is being restricted unfairly, even though none of these countries have a significant volume of trade with Australia.
The WTO is likely to deliver a resolution in mid-2013.
Plibersek claimed that the government had held talks with other countries that are considering applying similar tobacco packaging laws.
In 2001 Canada became the first country that introduced the obligation to include images in the packs.
They are currently mandatory in more than 40 countries, including Brazil, Turkey and Ukraine.
In Britain, New Zealand, South Africa and India, tougher laws are being considered.
In Australia many smokers are challenging.
"Photos don't affect me.
I just don't listen to them."
"You take the cigar and store the package," says Victor El Hage as he buys a package with the image of a tumor in his mouth.
"Honestly, there's only one reason I'd quit smoking: for my daughter."
James Yu, manager of the King of the Pack in central Sydney, says the uniform packs make it more complicated for them to be placed on the shelves.
"Normally it took an hour to unfold a delivery; now I'll take four," Yu says.
“The government should have totally banned tobacco and then, that’s it’s done, it’s all right, it’s over and we’ll close the store,” he says as he raises his hands up.
In a constantly connected world, it’s not that bad to get a little bored
During this year’s Thanksgiving holidays, I unexpectedly had to spend five hours at an airport because the plane we were flying on suffered a mechanical problem and we had to wait for another plane to arrive.
So I had a lot of time to think about boredom.
I won't lie to you.
Spending half a day at an airport waiting for a flight to arrive is quite boring, even if you have the means to be entertaining, such as books, magazines or iPhones, without forgetting the duty-free stores.
But there are more and more academics and child development experts who praise the benefits of boredom.
According to them, it’s okay for both us and our children to get bored from time to time.
Boring forces the brain to look for interesting options and may foster creativity.
Since, currently, most of us are connected to this or that screen, we do not experience the benefits of boredom.
So, should we accept boredom in good spirits?
Yes.
And no.
But we'll talk about it later.
In the first place, like many people, he assumed that, now that we have more time for leisure, boredom would be a relatively recent phenomenon.
But, Peter Toohey, professor of Greek and Roman History at the University of Calgary in Canada, and author of "Boredom: A Lively History" published in 2011 by Yale University Presses is not so.
"Indeedly, the history of boredom is very extensive," he says.
In the walls of Pompeii there are graffiti in Latin on boredom dating back to the 1st century A.D.
We must also consider how we define boredom.
"The problem is that it's already been defined and discussed in a variety of different ways," says John D. Eastwood, a professor of psychology at the University of York in Ontario, Canada.
After examining the research published and raising the idea before a discussion group made up of 100 people, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues defined boredom as the experience of "wishing, but not being able, to participate in an activity that fills you."
He states that the difference between boredom and apathy is that the person doesn’t do something but wants to do it.
He points out that with apathy there is no desire to do something.
The main experience of boredom, he says, is "the alteration of the care process, associated with a low mood and a feeling that time passes slowly."
Boredom may sound terribly like depression.
However, Professor Eastwood says that, although they may be related, people who are bored tend to see the problem as a matter of the environment or the world, while people who are depressed consider that the problem they have.
Sometimes we think we're bored, when what actually happens to us is that we have trouble concentrating.
In their study "The Unengaged Mind: Defining Boredom in Terms of Attention", published in the September issue of the journal Perspectives on Psychological Science, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues referred to an earlier experiment in which participants listened to a tape of a person reading a journal article.
Some groups listened in the adjoining room a full-fledged TV show that was unrelated, others listened to it with low volume, so it was barely noticeable, while the third group didn’t listen to the audio at any time.
The group who listened to the show with low volume showed more boredom than the other two groups; they struggled to concentrate, but they weren’t sure why and attributed it to boredom.
“When you’re trying to focus on a difficult or interesting task, disruption of attention can lead to boredom,” said Mark J. Fenske, professor of neuroscience at the University of Guelph in Ontario and one of the study’s authors.
“On the other hand, when you’re doing something boring, like spotting faulty parts in a manufacturing line, distracting music can help you not get bored.”
"In fact, we currently know that moving or doodling, actions that are often interpreted as a sign of boredom, can actually help combat it, as they make that person more physically alert."
“Research shows that children who are allowed to move learn more and retain more information than those who are forced to stand still,” says Professor Fenske.
We all know what it’s like to get bored at some point: the delay of an airplane, a monotonous orator, an especially boring film.
But some people are more likely to get bored than others.
To help measure it, some researchers developed in the 80’s a “boring trend scale.”
The scale includes issues like “many of the things I have to do are repetitive and monotonous” and “there are so many things I’m interested that I don’t have time to do everything.”
Stephen Vodanovich, a professor of psychology at the University of West Florida, says that, using such scales, researchers have found that boys are more likely to get bored more often than girls, and specifically need more external and more varied stimulation.
But, in general, teens are a type of group that gets bored quite easily.
In 1991, Reed Larson, professor of human and community development at the University of Illinois, conducted an experiment in which he contacted 400 teenagers and his parents seven to eight times a day through a search.
He found that 32 percent of teens said they would get bored while in school or doing homework, while 23 percent said they were bored when they weren’t in school.
On the other hand, 3 percent of parents said they were bored.
Professor Larson said he didn’t know if the current boredom percentages, 21 years later, would be higher or lower.
But he said what he did know was that "adolescence is a period in which boredom reaches its highest levels", largely because both children and adolescents are not given great control over what they want to do.
So, going back to my question from the beginning: Is it good to get bored?
Sometimes it’s not, since, taken to the extreme, it can cause us to expose ourselves to absurd physical risks, such as placing bets or falling into substance use as a method to combat it, as the investigations demonstrate.
On the other hand, many philosophers and writers talk about the connection between boredom and creativity, according to Professor Vodanovich, who has been studying this issue for more than two decades.
“Boredom is the brain’s way of telling you that you should be doing something else,” says Gary Marcus, professor of psychology at New York University.
But the brain doesn't always know what's most appropriate.
If you’re bored and using that energy to play the guitar or cook, that will satisfy you.
But if you watch TV, it may satisfy you in the short term, but not in the long term.
"So, if your child is bored and you give him an iPad, he may no longer get bored, but he won't have learned to entertain himself or dose himself," Professor Fenske says.
And "this ability to dose applies to other situations," he says.
Your child not only learns to entertain themselves, but learns to control themselves better in other areas.
I don't think we really want to praise the boredom.
But we shouldn't be too critical of him either.
Our objective should be, rather, to feel comfortable away from the constant rumour of activities and technologies.
Professor Eastwood agrees.
"In our view, we need to get bored more, but boredom is too distressing and disturbing desire to relate it to something that's worth it," he says.
He points out that what people are really looking for is a way to disconnect and enjoy a downtime.
“In an environment where we’re constantly overstimulated,” he says, “it’s hard to find ways to focus our attention when the noise stops.”
Colorado: No Instruction Book for New Marijuana Consumption Law
Anthony Orozco, a 19-year-old student at a community college and footballer from southeastern Colorado, is facing criminal proceedings for something that will soon be legal across the state: the possession of some marijuana stones and a pipe he used to smoke it.
Orozco says that one day in September, he and some friends were driving through Lamar, in the plains close to the Kansas border, when police stopped them.
The police found marijuana in the car and some time later Orozco received a judicial subpoena for possession of drugs and related items (minor crimes that each carry a fine of 100 U.S. dollars) in which he was informed of the date of the trial.
“They treat us like criminals,” Orozco says.
And they are?
In weeks of uncertainty following Colorado’s vote to legalize small amounts of marijuana for recreational use, the response in hundreds of minor causes of drug possession depends less on the law than on location.
Hundreds of misdemeanors for marijuana possession are being annulled both here and in Washington state, where a similar measure was approved.
Police departments have ceased to bring charges against those over the age of 21 for small-scale possession, which will be legally approved once the law went into effect in the coming weeks.
But Colorado’s more conservative district attorneys have pledged to move forward with open marijuana possession cases and are still citing people.
At the same time, several cities, from the suburbs of Denver to the western mountains, are voting to prevent authorized marijuana stores from being opened in their communities.
"All of this is changing so quickly that I don't know what's going to be next," said Daniel J. Oates, police chief in Aurora, just east of Denver.
Those in charge of the regulations in Washington state are also circling the issue.
And they continue to seek advice to establish a system of production, manufacturing, distribution and sales licenses; all this before the deadline arrives, December 1, 2013.
They say that, for better or worse, Colorado goes ahead of most states when it comes to regulating marijuana use, first for therapeutic purposes and now for recreational use.
"Colorado has a more regulated market, so it will be a good guide," said Brian E. Smith, a spokesman for the Washington State Alcohol Control Commission.
But Mr. Smith admits that any place or system can only make suggestions about what might work.
"There is no real precedent that serves as a model," he says.
Washington’s law, dubbed I-502, goes into effect on Dec. 6, which also leaves a year during which it will swim between two waters, since the licensing system in that state won’t yet exist, but possession will be legal.
In addition, there are some sensitive mechanical issues that must be resolved during that period, such as how to maintain a balance between the state’s requirement to offer “adequate access” to authorized marijuana and its prohibition to carry out cannabis businesses within a radius of less than 300 meters from schools, parks, children’s play areas or children’s centers.
“Nowhere will it be more difficult to locate an authorized cannabis business than in urban areas, especially in the Seattle metropolitan area,” says Ben Livingston, a spokeswoman for the Center for Legal Cannabis, a recently created research group.
On November 21, Oates, Aurora’s police chief, sent an email to his police officers informing them that the city prosecutor would stop bringing legal action for minor misdemeanors for possession of marijuana to people over the age of 21 and that the police would stop bringing charges against those crimes “with immediate effect.”
Police Chief Oates said they would enforce city regulations that regulate the cultivation of marijuana for therapeutic purposes and that they would continue to persecute traffickers and drug dealers.
In Weld County in northern Colorado, District Attorney Ken Buck represents a stricter view.
After the vote, he assured that his office would continue to pursue cases of possession of marijuana, mainly to pressure consumers and that they decide to receive treatment.
Currently, 119 people are facing charges for possession of 56 grams or less of marijuana, although many of them have other charges.
“Our office has an obligation to bring legal proceedings against those faults that at the time they occurred were a crime,” Mr. Buck said in a statement.
The response has been tricky even in places such as Mesa’s rural county, where voters rejected the marijuana legalization initiative.
Grand Junction police, the county’s largest city, no longer cite adults for possession of small amounts.
Pete Hautzinger, county district attorney, supported this decision, but also decided not to dismiss any of the outstanding cases for possession.
"I don't think I'm wasting my time by continuing to enforce the law until it changes," he said.
Even though 55 percent of voters supported the measure, it was never considered that bringing marijuana use for recreational use into the government and the judicial system would be easy.
The contradictory reactions that arise throughout the state show a marked ambivalence among officials regarding the great environmental experiment in this state.
“It’s about a cultural barrier with district attorneys,” says Sean McAllister, a Denver attorney who represents the defendants for possession of marijuana and is a local spokeswoman for the National Organization for Marijuana Law Reform.
"It's been so long taking people in court that, in reality, they still can't accept that it's legal," he said.
Being the first states to consider small amounts of marijuana in the same way as if it were alcohol, Colorado and Washington are preparing to become nationwide testing cases for drug legalization.
While attorneys and state officials have a new frontier of legal sales, they also look forward to receiving indications from the federal government, which still envisages consideration of the sale and cultivation of marijuana as federal crimes.
Advocates of marijuana legalization are hopeful that the Justice Department will give way.
Although there have been some prominent arrests of patients and marijuana vendors for therapeutic purposes, the federal government has mostly allowed marijuana-saling businesses to exist for these purposes in Colorado, Washington and 16 more states.
“Although anti-drug agents are likely not knocking doors to confiscate a small drug bag, they will likely be reluctant to allow state-regulated stores to run for the sale of marijuana for recreation use, allowed under the new law,” said Kevin A. Sabet, a former Obama administration anti-drug policy adviser.
A number of cities in Colorado don’t wait for federal authorities.
Even before election day, some local governments approved moratoriums on any new marijuana store, even though it will be about a year before any of them can open.
Last week, the western city of Montrose adopted a six-month ban and is likely to approve next week.
“We don’t want to put ourselves in a situation where we authorize someone and then we have a serious problem at the federal level,” said Bob Nicholson, a member of the city.
“Our community voted against this amendment.
We’re studying in favor of what the community voted for in favor of what the state voted for.”
There are many questions about it.
Petronella Wyatt: "I was harassed until I was kicked out of Oxford for being a Tory"
You don't attack college students because of their views just now
I don’t remember any time in my life when I haven’t been dreaming of getting a place at the University of Oxford.
Both my father and my elder brother had been in which I assumed it was the best place in the world to learn, a Greek symposium of the contemporary era that fostered the two pillars of civilization: free thought and tolerance.
However, within two weeks of occupying my place at Worcester College in the late 80’s to pursue the career of History, I had already packed my bags, which triggered the first scandal of my life.
My father collapsed and started crying.
My friends were baffled.
The Evening Standard newspaper claimed that I had dropped out because it didn’t tolerate some college classmates having sex in the next room.
Writer A. N. Wilson announced joking that I had left because I was forced to drink in chipped cups.
The truth was less fun.
I ran away from there.
Yes, I fled, because I had been subjected to systematic harassment and intimidation.
Not because of my extravagant name, or the fact that I came from a private school.
I was harassed for only one reason, that in the cradle of the alleged illustration was somewhat intolerant and barbaric: my father, the late Woodrow Wyatt, was a prominent adviser to Margaret Thatcher and I supported the conservative party.
You might be wondering what that's coming at now.
Well, several reports indicate that a new generation of center-right students is suffering similar persecution.
The institutionalized and growing hatred towards students of the Conservative party is such in Oxford that, last week, a group of them claimed the same level of equal rights that homosexuals, the disabled and ethnic minorities have.
Conservative members of Corpus Christi College’s JCR (Undergrading Students Association) claim they are “often severely separated, personally attacked and treated as unpleasant people” for their political ideas.
They want to create a position on their college’s equal opportunity committee to make sure they can freely express their opinions.
Their situation was not favored by a recent BBC2 documentary called "Wonderland: Young, Bright and on the Right" about student politics and featuring the Tories as eccentric and neo-Nazis.
He showed graduate student Joe Cooke, former president of the Oxford University (OUCA) Conservative Party Association in a Rolls-Royce, sporting a silver suit and a cane with the silver grip.
In other universities, conservative students say they are treated as “Turk’s heads” for the introduction of higher tuition fees.
Luke Black, a 20-year-old vice-president of Nottingham University’s Conservative Party Association, stated in a Sunday newspaper that “there’s a growing lefting trend in universities.
People assume we’re like the Bullingdon Club without even getting to know each other.”
Samuel Roberts, a 21-year-old history student at Corpus Christi who unveiled the motion for greater protection, says this environment is “uncomfortable,” while Stephanie Cherill, a 19-year-old OUCA elected president, says the attitude of JCR members toward center-right people has worsened.
“This poses a threat to the environment of intellectual debate, as well as to the well-being of members,” he says.
During my first few weeks at Oxford, I was more alone than one.
I went into college in September 1986, I was a terribly shy 18-year-old girl.
Hatred of the Conservative Party was at its highest.
In the previous year, the university had voted to deny Margaret Thatcher, a former student, an "honoris cause" degree after the cuts in higher education funding.
The atmosphere would have shuddered any Stalinist in horror.
Already during the first days of the week of welcoming the new university students, when the new students meet and know the professors, I received a sample of the bitterness that awaited me.
I discovered that the professors not only turned a blind eye to the mockery that was made of the conservative university students, but they participated in it willingly.
The policy of the miners’ strike, the privatization and the government’s opposition to anti-apartheid sanctions in South Africa reached the wooden halls of the seminar.
The first one I attended, 18th-century French texts were translated into English and I was not prepared for what came next.
"Miss Wyatt," said Professor Harry Pitt (already deceased), "please translate the first paragraph."
Don't say one.
Pitt, a short man with a face that looked like a cake dough, knew well how to expel bad bile.
“Is it that Thatcherists refuse to learn French or are they just fools?” he asked.
The other college students laughed.
My eyes were burning with the tears I was trying to contain.
"I suggest that you go to French classes in your spare time, as long as you're not too busy getting to know people," Pitt said.
I went back to my room like a soul in sorrow.
That night I sat alone at dinner time in college; then I felt a slight slap on my back.
He was a sophomore English student named James who introduced himself as a member of the OUCA.
"I know who you are," he said nicely.
"I'm afraid that's how it's going.
They mess with anyone who's suspicious of being Tory.
It's very complicated for me, but they know that your father is close to Margaret Thatcher, so for you it'll be even worse.
Many newcomer Tories pretend to be Labour."
Later, in a local pub, I tried cowardly to hide.
I insisted that I didn't agree with everything Mrs. Thatcher said.
This trick proved useless.
A first-year student of Philosophy, Politics and Economics who, paradoxically, had studied at Eton, said: "You are the daughter of a fascist pig.
You're polluted."
Other students adopted that song.
I became the dirty perverted.
"How do the Tories have sex?" one asked.
"They beat each other, don't they?"
I felt in the same way that homosexuals should have felt before the liberal law of the 60’s.
Could one day lead a normal life in Oxford?
Would I be forced to meet people who thought like me only after dusk?
Would I have to become labor and repress my natural tendencies?
The three years awaiting me were presented as a purgatory of ostracism and isolation.
The only opening professor Tory was Norman Stone, Professor of Contemporary History destined for my college.
They hated him for being not only from the Conservative party, but also thatcher’s adviser on foreign policy and one of the people who wrote his speeches.
I was barely over there.
He detested the place because he considered it provincial and insignificant and for its adherence to the Marxist-deterministic view of history.
In 1997 he accepted a professorship at Bilkent University in Ankara, Turkey.
"You'ren't gonna be happy here," he said.
I began to travel daily from Oxford to my parents’ house in London, finding refuge in my most open metropolitan-minded family and friends.
I told my father I hated Oxford and why.
She didn't believe me.
When he studied there in the 40’s, all political views were accepted.
"But if it's the best place in the world," he said sadly.
"They would never do that, not there among my dear dream needles.
Even my communist friends always had an impeccable attitude."
His sore eyes began to cloud.
"Give him another chance.
I'm sure they've done it just to joke.
My heart would break if you left it.”
Exhausted from so many trips to London, my emotional resilience was deteriorating.
A friend of mine, who also supported the Conservative Party, had succumbed to pressure and given up his creed.
The following week, during a seminary in which another professor of history had hinted, seriously, that I was an "enemy of the people", I decided to do the same.
Red with shame inside, I admitted that my parents had "brainwashed me" and called them "old fools."
The truce lasted a short time.
It was my father who made the last nail in the coffin of my university career at Oxford.
At that time my father wrote two weekly columns in Murdoch’s press.
My door was locked.
I was snuggled in fear and, after five minutes, my pursuers gave up.
When they left I packed my suitcase and took the first train to London.
I've never returned.
Maybe you'll call me weak weeping.
But no 18-year-old should be subjected to such a level of intimidation and hostility in an educational institution.
What is even more tragic is that it was Oxford, which has not only formed 14 Prime Ministers of the Conservative Party but, to this date, it hides behind an undeserved reputation for equality and freedom of thought.
"Valentino prefers elegance to notoriety"
On the occasion of the exhibition "Valentino: Master of Sewing", which was inaugurated this week in London, ABC speaks with Naty Abascal, Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisza and other famous clients of the Italian designer
Somerset House, former residence of Elizabeth I of England, is the only place in the British capital worthy of hosting an exhibition on Valentino Garavani.
The designer has recognized it during the inauguration of "Valentino: Master of Sewing", a retrospective apotheosique that brings together more than 130 haute couture dresses created by her "maison" during the last 50 years.
“I love this palace,” he says with his unmistakable Italian accent.
This exhibition is the culmination of a story whose only protagonist is "il signorre" Garavani, but which could not have been written without his distinguished clients.
Valentino has always been fascinated by the rarefied and distant world of nobility.
In the first room of this exhibition, open until March 3, private letters and photos abound with the signature of the most pomegranate of the aristocracy, from Princess Salimah Aga Khan to Lord Snowdon, through Princess Marie-Chantal of Greece and Margaret of England.
Valentino teaches these personal memories as trophies of his social rise: of humble dressmaker of the city of Voghera, in northern Italy, an idol of the international "jet-set".
Being in love with royalty has nothing wrong.
“At least they don’t turn off their whistles on your magnificent carpet like some beautiful pop music celebrities do,” Baroness Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisza says.
In the 60's and 70's, we both lived in the Alps and were good friends.
Valentino is a spectacular host and entertains with generosity and elegance.
"We all loved to be invited to his chalet in Gstaad," explains the ex-wife of "Heini" Thyssen, an intimate of forgotten beauties such as Marella Agnelli or Eugenie Niarchos.
Valentino has always preferred elegance to notoriety.
Still, it's a star.
Valeria Mazza, wearing a Valentino
Argentine model Valeria Mazza also does not forget the charisma of the dressmaker.
Many years ago, after a parade in Piazza di Spagna, in Rome, we went to have dinner at his apartment.
There were twenty people, including Sharon Stone and John Kennedy Jr.
In every detail of the floor, the decoration, the food, the music, in everything it looked and felt its spirit.
“Each of the guests made us feel important and dear,” recalls the “top model,” which began working with him during Paris Haute Couture Week in 1995.
“It’s never out of style because their designs are works of art,” he says.
Alcurnia Parade
Garavani’s life is not a story of obsessions, but of well-reciprocated loves.
He loves well-educated people and better degrees, and they love him.
One of Somerset House’s galleries has been transformed into a glamorous, sixty-meter-long catwalk that proposes a change of roles: the visitor takes the place of the models and must parade down the catwalk to contemplate a dream “audience” worn by Valentino’s masterpieces, such as the dress that Jackie Kennedy chose for his wedding to Aristotles Onassis, the costumes used by Monica Vi
In that public of mannequins shine names such as Sibilla of Luxembourg, Gloria von Thurn und Taxis, Mette-Marit of Norway, Rosary of Bulgaria or Sofia of Habsburg.
Naty Abascal and the dressmaker in 2006
Many of those clients say that the first Valentino is like first love, "impossible to forget."
I remember him perfectly.
It was a trousers, a shirt, a "gillette" vest and a jacket from the autumn-winter 1971-1972 collection.
“It was a gift he gave me,” says Naty Abascal, one of the designer’s muses.
"I prefer him over others for his femininity, for his great love for women, because it enhances our beauty," adds the former Duchess of Feria.
I really like the colors he uses, they have great light and they are "donors" to the face.
Proportions are perfect.
Fashion princess and prescriber Patricia della Giovampaola d'Arenbergtano may soon forget the first time she wore an Valentino.
When I was a teenager and lived in Italy, I dreamed of becoming the age and the occasion to be able to wear one of their evening costumes...
The time finally arrived in the late 90's.
I bought my first Valentino dress to wear at a party at my cousin's castle, Prince Edouard de Ligne.
It was a red suit, with a flipped skirt, drapped "corsage" and cleavage word of honor.
“It was a dream come true,” says Rodrigo d’Arenberg’s widow.
"Valentino is indifferent to fashion, his obsession is the timeless," explains this Italian aristocrat who lives between Paris, New York and Buenos Aires.
Princess D'Arenberg keeps her dresses from the dressmaker with "the utmost care ... because a dress is not only a dress, it is also the set of memories that it entails."
The "King" of Fashion
The grand finale of Somerset House's exhibition is Marie-Chantal Miller's briding suit at her wedding to Paul of Greece in 1995.
It took four months of work and 25 "ragazze" (as the dressmaker calls his seamstresses) to make that ivory silk suit with bead inlays, twelve different types of lace and a tail of four and a half meters.
According to journalist Suzy Menkes, the highest authority of the specialized press, that dress represents a milestone of the haute couture of the end of the 20th century, "the return of the customers of high society".
Obnubilated for years with the "savoir-être" of the elite, now Valentino is his best specimen.
Cavaliere di Gran Croce (the highest-ranking distinction in Italy), Cavaliere del Lavoro, Commandeur de L'Ordre des Arts et des Lettres, decorated with the Legion of Honour, Garavani accrues as many honors as any of the husbands of their clients.
“I’ve always been struck by his refinement, his calm, his neat and perfect appearance,” D’Arenberg acknowledges.
The last time I saw it was a month ago at a gala dinner at the Musée d'Orsay.
He was at Countess Jacqueline de Ribes' table, a great friend of mine.
"It was impeccable, time doesn't pass for him."
If a princess says so...
Toughest Trade in the World: Kawah Ijen’s Human Mules
For four euros, the porters of the Indonesian volcano risk life and health by carrying 70 kilos of sulphur through steep stone trails
There are people for whom their work is hell and another that literally works in hell.
It is the case of Anto Wijaya, one of 400 miners who make a lift by taking sulfur from the Kawah Ijen volcano, east of the Indonesian island of Java.
To do this, it has to descend every day to the bottom of its crater, where the sulphurous gas emanating from the bowels of the Earth solidifies when it comes into contact with air.
After ripping out large sulphur rocks, which in total weigh about 70 kilos, it carries them in two bamboo baskets that load on its shoulders through rugged stone paths.
They are only 250 meters to the top of the volcano, which rises to 2,386 meters of altitude, but the exhausted porters take more than 40 minutes to ascend at the pace of a turtle, guarding the balance and slowly measuring its steps so as not to slip and fall by the precipice.
They know that any mischief could cost them their lives, as it happened to a French tourist who fell years ago by the cliffs of the Kawah Ijen.
Kawah Ijen miners earn 5 euro cents for every kilo of sulphur they extract
Once above, they make their way among tourists who photograph them as if they were circus monkeys and, fatigously carrying the heavy baskets, they walk three kilometers to the scale that a mining company has placed a little lower, at 1,850 meters high.
It is PT Candi Ngrimbi, a company that explodes since 1960 the volcano and, never better said, to its workers, to which it pays 662 Indonesian rupees (5 euro cents) for each kilo of sulphur.
He then sells it for 10,000 rupees (83 euro cents) to the petrochemical industry, since this mineral is widespread in everyday life and is used to make matches, fireworks, cosmetics, dynamite and even to bleach sugar.
"As it is normal to carry 70 kilos, we take out about 46,000 rupees (3.8 euros) on each trip," explains Anto, who usually makes three sizes daily.
It takes three hours in each one and ends up ground, but they allow him to gather 138,000 rupees (11.5 euros) at the end of the day.
Although it seems a misery for such inhuman effort, it is triple what it would gain on the field.
"The wages of the miners are very high here, where the collection of coffee is paid to 15,000 rupees (1.2 euros) the day and the average monthly salary is two million rupees (167 euros)", clarifies the porter, who previously worked as a bricklayer on the tourist island of Bali.
There, his salary was 75,000 rupees (6.2 euros) a day and the pit was not as hard, but Anto has returned with his family to Banyuwangi, a village close to the volcano, for a compelling reason that, in Indonesia, is as blunt as sulfur: "I married a young woman from Bali, where they are Hindus, and I have brought her to Java to convert to Islam."
Anto has asthma, breathes with difficulty, coughs constantly and his eyes are irritated by toxic gases
At the age of 27, Anto has been playing his skin for three in the Kawah Ijen, whose sulphur has already begun to take his toll despite covering his face with a mask and special glasses.
He has asthma, breathes with difficulty, coughs constantly and his eyes are irritated by the toxic gases that the volcano fires.
It's the price you have to pay to get your dream.
"I will work two more years because I want to open a store or study Spanish or French," he promises in more than acceptable English.
Punished by life, this friendly and intelligent young man could be a tour guide, waiter or hotel receptionist, but instead does the work of a mule.
Sharing an unclean wooden hut with other porters, he wakes up every day at two in the morning because the sulphur does not stilt at night, when its characteristic yellow color turns blue and shines in the middle of darkness.
Defying the shadows, Anto descends into the crater lighting with a small lantern attached to his hull, which he himself has bought with his money.
About 400 porters load the sulphur baskets on their shoulders from the bottom of the crater
Despite its pending benefits, the mining company has not mechanized sulphur extraction to save costs or supply any equipment to porters, who work on their own and so much weight.
In fact, they do not even see part of the 30,000 rupees (2.5 euros) of surcharge per camera that, along with the entrance of 15,000 rupees (1.2 euros), the guards of this natural park charge the tourists who come to photograph the volcano and its human mules.
“This job is for the beasts and not for the people,” protests Madrusin, a 42-year-old wealthy porter who has been in the Kawah Ijen for three decades, since leaving school.
Capable of lifting up to 110 kilos, he assures that he will endure working "all he can" because he needs the money to educate his three children, between 18 and 10 years old.
I won't retire, I'll die here because the volcano has been my whole life
Although sulphur burns its throat and stings the eyes when the wind suddenly changes and traps miners in the thick columns that come out of the volcano, they are so hard that none complain of suffering serious diseases ... beyond, of course, their usual respiratory problems, osteoarthritis, knee pains and sores on the shoulders, which have been malformed by the weight of the baskets.
Balancing the basket on his back, Unainik can only carry 50 kilos at the age of 53.
Every day, he and his companions rip 15 tons of sulphur from the volcano, which three trucks move to Tamansari’s warehouse, 18 kilometers away along a goat road between the weeds.
"I won't retire, I will die here because the volcano has been my entire life," Unainik proclaims opening his mouth well, where he lacks several teeth.
Of his five sons, the eldest, 30 years old, he also works loading sulfur.
Time passes, but misery perpetuates from generation to generation one of the toughest trades in the world: that made by the human mules of Kawah Ijen.
Singapore is looking for babies to save the economy
Singaporeans blame the career, stress and cost of property and education for not having children.
"Singapore's population needs to grow."
I'm a patriotic husband, you're my patriotic wife, let's fulfill our civic duty and believe life!
It may seem implausible that these verses are part of a peppermint pill ad, but despite that — or perhaps because of that — the video went viral on YouTube in Singapore earlier this year.
The phrases are part of a rap that uses local references such as "Let's put a bao (bun) in the oven" to laugh at Singapore's birth rate.
The advertising company that made the video, BBH, has the hope that with the ad they will manage to draw attention to the problem in a fun way.
Its creative director, Douglas Hamilton, says he wanted to use the power of music so that people fulfill “their national duty.”
This is something purely from the internet, so we had to make it funny and funny.
It's the biggest problem in this country.
We’re the worst in the world to reproduce ourselves, so we felt that this was an issue we had to address.
We knew that the government had tried many things, such as throwing perfumes with pheromones or organizing speed-dating evenings.
Many of those ideas may have been creative, but they didn’t necessarily work.
So we thought, why don't we make as creative as possible to solve this problem, which is composing a rap?
1.2 children
The one who doesn't take it so lightly is the government of Singapore.
Spend US$1,300 a year on policies to encourage citizens to have more children.
A government package for marriages and parents grants up to US$15,000 per child, extends maternity leave and distributes tax benefits.
But all of this has had little effect.
Singapore is a rich, high-tech state city in Southeast Asia, also known for the conservatism of its leaders and its strict social controls.
Singapore’s birth rate, according to its national population division, currently stands at 1.2 children per woman.
The last time it was above 2 – known as a replacement rate – was in 1976.
So why don’t Singaporeans have more children?
Tan Wei Ming, director of Marriage and Family Policies in the National Population Division, says it’s due to a “better education” and “a wider range of career opportunities.”
“That has allowed people to have a wider range of options in terms of living goals and priorities, beyond getting married and starting a family,” he explains.
These changes in social norms have contributed to an increase in singleness and delayed marriage and birth, which results in a decline in the birth rate in Singapore.
In the meantime, an immigration policy that aims to dramatically increase immigration to deal with population decline has created resentment among the local population.
In Singapore there are websites where xenophobia barely disguised against many new immigrants, in particular the Chinese, abound that criticize for keeping wages low and not integrating.
Rising immigration is also seen as one of the reasons Singapore’s governing party last year experienced its worst election outcome since independence.
Since the elections there has been an attempt to correct the problem, with the highest quotas and levies for foreign workers.
Unexpected Consequences
While the drop in the birth rate has known effects on a nation’s economic growth, tax revenues, health costs and immigration policies, Singapore’s example is also having some unexpected consequences.
The government tries not to build so many small houses.
For example, it has begun to affect the real estate sector.
The authority of urban development has gone on to control the number of small apartments, known as "shoe box", that can be built in certain areas of the city.
These apartments have areas of 46 square meters and have had a lot of sales success.
However, there are concerns that they can promote a single lifestyle and discourage developers who want to build large, family homes.
But Lim Yew Soon, managing director of the real estate company EL Developers, says its “shoe boxes” sell much faster than larger units.
They are more popular, in the sense that units sell out days, even weeks, faster than larger units.
This makes it much better for our cash flow.
However, he admits that the new rules give clearer guidance to the developers, who used to be hindered if they wanted to offer too many small units in a project.
Too stressed
Singapore is a state city.
While these new rules may be a step towards increasing the national birth rate, talking to Singaporeans working in the center’s financial district, it doesn’t seem like they won’t have much effect.
“People are very stressed, houses are expensive and so is education, so many people are postponing it,” says a young executive.
Other people may have children.
"But it's important for me to count on my own money and time," says another 20-year-old.
Men and women mention their career, stress, and the cost of property and education as the motives that prevent them from having children.
So, as much as the government tries to push its citizens to have children, when it comes to making babies, it’s Singaporeans who have the final say.
The private off-line is private online
privacy.
According to the Dictionary of the Royal Spanish Academy it is the quality of private or "the field of private life that has the right to protect from any interference".
What is private for a child under the age of 16?
How do you apply this definition in your everyday life and on social media?
Do you understand the dangers you’re exposed that you’re ventiling on the Internet that you probably wouldn’t share offline?
elPeriódico interviewed five boys aged between ten and 15, frequent users of the network.
In four of the cases, they associated the term to “something very mine,” on the personal level, and “on the user’s keyname,” when they applied it to social networks.
“My deepest secrets wouldn’t be uploaded to a post,” says Jorge, a ten-year-old, when he tries to explain the meaning of private on sites such as Facebook, Twitter, Hotmail and Windows Live Messenger, where he’s had accounts for two years.
"They're very secret secrets, my mom can meet them, but not everyone," he says.
In FB I upload beautiful images or games.
I also entertain with my acquaintances.
"I wouldn't share a picture that's not mine, or someone who's making a fool of myself," he says.
The child acknowledges that it is wrong to publish obscene photographs, of naked people, of crimes or to write humiliating or violent comments.
Jorge claims to meet the 35 friends he has in FB and his nine followers on Twitter.
Most are relatives.
The mother is included, she has the key to one of the accounts.
I opened Twitter to express myself and write interesting tweets.
"I don't know if they answer me, I just upload them," he adds.
"Social media is entertaining, I can talk to distant relatives or my friends quickly," he says.
She doesn't hesitate to answer that she would never accept a request from an unknown person.
I wouldn't even listen to someone to recommend a stranger.
The 14-year-old case of Joseph is different.
The teenager has open accounts on Hotmail, Facebook, My Space and Ask, in the latter he admits not to know 20 of the people added on his list of friends.
"It doesn't scare me, because we have something in common, music for example," he says.
According to the boy, none have been hinted or asked for his home address or telephone number.
"If they pressured me or demanded it from me, just take them out of my account," he acknowledges.
Jose became a follower of Ask, after reading a recommendation on Twitter.
The boy is no stranger the experiences of what is now known as cyberbullying.
An acquaintance of one of my friend was being extorted on a social network.
They threatened him and demanded money from him.
“He never knew who he was,” he says.
The victim, according to Joseph, did not close the account.
"He just gave him privacy."
It then explains a number of steps to set up the account securely.
Unlike Jorge, this boy would upload photos of acquaintances in uncomfortable or embarrassing situations.
I would, if I like it or I want it.
"Although I know that that's cyberbullying," he says.
Key Questions
Marielos Porras, an English teacher and graduate in Education and Learning, believes that in order to guide children and adolescents it should be understood that the purpose of social networks is to inform.
"The Internet emerged as a means to search for information, but with the appearance of these sites they changed the rules of the game," he says.
Porras points out that academic Marc Prensky, a master’s degree in Pedagogy from Yale University and author of the work Natives and Digital Immigrants, coined these terms to explain the phenomenon.
The digital natives are them, the children and young people who were born with technology.
“We’re the digital immigrants we have to teach them when we’re still learning,” he says.
He says that the issue is complex, "because we are asking them to have a definite criterion about what is convenient or not to disseminate, publish or say at an age that maturity does not give for that."
"Also that they are selective when what matters most to them is to be popular, to have thousands of friends without thinking about the consequences," he adds.
According to the specialist, the most effective way to teach what is privacy to children and adolescents is through questions that make them reflect.
"To tell them not to do it doesn't work," he adds.
Below, Porras lists a few options: There are things you wouldn’t tell a stranger, why do you do it on the internet?
Or, would you like them to post a photo of you like you posted about a friend?
Do you know what the others post about you?
When you tag holiday photos, did you ask other people for permission to label them?
And one more, everyone needs to know what you're doing each moment?
Another point is to make them see that they must act online as they do offline.
They're the same rules.
"One outside the Internet acts with respect, morality and other principles, it must still be on social networks," he says.
Surveillance
Estuardo Guardia, university professor, primary school teacher and educational consultant, comments that it is essential for parents to read social media policies conscientiously.
By understanding each of the subsections, they have solid foundations to talk with their children about the implications of opening an account on the Internet.
"For example, age and what is allowed to share or publish," he says.
According to Guardia, it’s key to remind the children of “don’t talk to strangers.”
Unasur Summit concludes without releasing the Lima Declaration
The 6th Presidential Summit of the South American Union of Nations (UNASUR) concluded today in Peru without making public the Lima Declaration, previously announced and theoretically signed by the seven assistant leaders.
Efe repeatedly tried to access the document signed at the 6th Ordinary Meeting of Heads of State and Government of Unasur, but sources of Foreign Ministry and the Presidency initially pointed out that they were going to deliver it after the closure, but later claimed that at some point it will be published on the website of the Peruvian Government.
When consulted by the text, they pointed out that the content had been exposed by the president of Peru, Ollanta Humala, during a brief statement to the press.
Journalists ' access to Summit information was restricted at all times.
During the day only the soundless video of the presidential assembly was broadcast in the press room with the message "private session, restricted audio".
The little information that circulated among the reporters was delivered by the press spokesmen of some of the governments of Unasur attending the appointment, although not the Peruvian.
The only document released during the day was the list of assistant presidents, which caused the unrest of hundreds of journalists from various national and international media who asked for greater data.
Subsequently, the Presidency of Peru sent an email to the media with the "final declaration" of the summit, but it is the pronouncement of Humala, and not of the official document that closed the summit.
Last October, Peru was the host of the III South America-Arab Countries (ASPA) Summit, and on that occasion, despite being insistently requested by the press, the so-called Lima Declaration that they had previously announced was not disseminated.
On ASPA’s official website it can be corroborated that the document was published last Tuesday.
In both international appointments, the Peruvian authorities endeavoured to ensured transmission systems for all journalists but limited the collection of information to the maximum.
The summit also concluded with the joint commitment of Chile and Peru to accept a ruling by the Court of The Hague that directs a border dispute between both countries.
The presidents of Peru, Ollanta Humala, and of Chile, Sebastián Piñera, met during the regional meeting and ratified that they will respect the decision of the (ICJ), which begins on Monday in The Hague to listen to the arguments of both parties, in the process that Lima presented against Santiago.
"We will comply and execute the sentence that defines the differences that today we are bringing to this international court," Humala said, along with his Chilean counterpart.
"Chile has been, is and will continue to be a country respectful of international law, the peaceful resolution of disputes, treaties and international tribunals," Piñera added, and greeted Humala with a handshake, next to the flags of the two countries.
The confirmation of both presidents that they will submit to the ICJ occurred after Colombia denounced this week the Bogota Pact by which it had agreed to submit to the rulings of that international court, following a decision on maritime limits with Nicaragua that it considered seriously wrong.
The summit was held with the inatestions of the presidents of Brazil, Dilma Rouseff; Venezuela, Hugo Chávez; Bolivia, Evo Morales; and Argentina, Cristina Kirchner.
Paraguay, suspended from Unasur since 2011 after the dismissal of its former president Fernando Lugo, did not participate in the appointment.
Host President Ollanta Humala was tasked with opening the deliberations in the morning and closing the summit, shortly after noon in Lima.
The president read the final document in which he reported that 16 agreements were adopted and the lines of action were fixed for 31 projects among South American countries for a total of 17 billion dollars in investments.
Among the agreements adopted he mentioned that UNASUR countries will take "significant steps towards the goal of a South American citizenry for which we are expanding the agreements on residence."
He reported that actions are being promoted to improve "cooperation in the fight against citizen insecurity and against transnational organized crime, actions to make medicines more accessible, low-priced internet access in all corners of South America, and to jointly and efficiently address the risks of natural disasters."
With a Europe in crisis, "the economic consolidation (of Latin America) must not have a triumphalist matrix but must serve to expand our productive matrix and glimpse a better future for our peoples," Humala added.
"We have decided to privilege a group of 31 emblematic projects that will improve the connection of the spaces of South America, especially in rural areas and borders ... joining our countries and generating new economic circuits," the Peruvian president said in a readed message.
Among these projects, he mentioned that five correspond to Peru, and they are located on the transverse axes of their territory, from the coast to Brazil, and two point to a greater connection with Ecuador, although he did not give greater details.
In addition, the final document mentioned the political situation in Paraguay.
"We hope that the electoral process in that country will serve for its reincorporation into the Union of South American Nations", from which it is currently excluded.
The need for Latin America to remain as a region of prosperity and peace, integrated, and of good relations between neighbours, was another of the issues underlined by the summit.
In this regard, the president of Colombia, Juan Manuel Santos, said before starting his participation in the regional event that he hoped to meet with his pair of Nicaragua, Daniel Ortega, on Saturday in Mexico to respectfully address the maritime dispute after an ICJ ruling, questioned by Bogotá.
"Tomorrow (Saturday) you may have a meeting with President Daniel Ortega," Santos said.
"We are going to review all these paths, they are not exclusive, even the treaty with Nicaragua will require a conversation with Nicaragua," he said.
"With President Ortega, I look forward to telling you that we handle this in the most civilized and respectful way possible," Santos added.
Santos and Ortega would meet on Saturday in Mexico where they plan to attend the inauguration of the new president of that country, Enrique Peña Nieto.
Likewise, as part of the summit, the bloc’s defense ministers previously met to approve the 2013 Plan of Action, which seeks to strengthen dialogue and defense consensus in the region.
Argentina, Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Brazil, Uruguay, Venezuela, Chile, Guyana, Suriname and Paraguay make up UNASUR, although the last country is suspended.
Peru has the pro-tempore presidency of the regional bloc.
"South America must learn from Europe to integrate a citizenship": Rafael Correa
The president of Ecuador, Rafael Correa, affirmed today that the creation of a common citizenship is a goal in which "South America, in this case yes, must learn from Europe."
Correa, who participated in the 6th Presidential Summit of the Union of South American Nations (UNASUR), held in Lima, told state TV Peru that Europeans "killed themselves in World War II" and in other conflicts, "but now they are practically a homeland."
He defended, in that sense, the project of establishing South American citizenship encouraged by Unasur member countries.
"You have to achieve the free mobility of citizens and workers by any South American country, as already happens with the members of the Andean Community, but there are still reactionary sectors that want to return us to the past," he warned.
The Ecuadorian president was, in addition, in favor of the recomposition of the Organization of American States (OAS) under the premise of reducing the influence of the Anglo-Saxon states and taking into account those who have signed the San José pact on human rights.
Those who give chair never commit to anything and, instead, we South Americans sign everything.
"It is incomprehensible that the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights is in Washington under U.S. funding," WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange said, referring to political asylum.
Correa assured that he is not sorry for that decision because with it he has not betrayed his principles, but has respected his "deep democratic and human rights values."
He added that, at the time, "there were well-founded suspicions that Assange was extradited to a third country and his process was not respected."
In addition, he criticized the Swedish court for requiring him to submit to the interrogation in his territory for an alleged sexual crime when "the Swedish legislation itself allows it to be carried out by videoconference, which could be done from the Ecuadorian embassy in London."
Correa said there's a risk that Assange's physical and mental health will deteriorate.
"I haven't spoken to him since he's in our embassy, but the ambassador informed me that I had a small lung problem, nothing serious," he said.
What there is is the danger of deteriorating your physical and mental health by being enclosed in a small space without being able to exercise outdoors.
"That complicates anyone's health," he added.
Correa pointed out that the solution to asylum granted to Assange since June at the Ecuadorian embassy in London, by granting a lifesave that allows him to travel to Ecuador, is in the hands of Britain, Sweden and the European legal authorities and stressed that there have been conversations with London in search of a solution to the lockdown of the founder of WikiLeaks.
We do not negotiate with human rights, that word we do not use in this case, but there have been permanent conversations.
"The solution to this problem is in the hands of Britain, Sweden and the European legal authorities because Assange's lawyer, Baltazar Garzón, is doing a number of procedures in different European bodies," he said.
“If Britain gives the safe passage tomorrow this is over.”
And if Sweden, as its legislation perfectly allows and as it has done in other cases, interrogates Mr Assange at the embassy of Ecuador in London or interrogates him via Skype tomorrow this problem ends.
Correa took the opportunity to reaffirm himself as a defender of freedom of the press and qualified that what he does not tolerate is "the mediocrity, the bad faith and the lie, which distort freedom of expression."
"The greatest enemies of freedom of the press are not evil and perverse politicians but bad journalists based on profit, blackmail and extortion," he said.
In this regard, it was pleased that it is no longer these journalists "neither the bankers nor the hegemonic and bourgeois countries that dominate Ecuador", and said that if re-elected "it will delve into the revolution to continue the right path and direction".
Correa also supported the decision to keep the veto of Paraguay in Unasur at least until its next elections, arguing that the body "must be firm and not tolerate opportunism and couplism clothed with legality" because in reality it "shattered the legitimacy of Paraguayan democracy."
In addition, the Ecuadorian president considered "perfectly relevant" the desire of his Colombian counterpart, Juan Manuel Santos, to now negotiate with Nicaragua the maritime limits between both countries after the ruling of the International Court of Justice of The Hague, which benefited Nicaraguan maritime sovereignty.
At the moment, this failure is not complied with.
It's an issue between a South American and a Central American country.
Conflict is unavoidable, but they must be overcome by the will to walk together.
You have to process them extensively in order to overcome them and move forward.
He also hoped for a good conclusion of the maritime boundary litigation held in the same court Peru and Chile and said that "it is correct for Latin America to seek international bodies if the two countries commit to accept ruling however hard it may be."
Referring to the eventuality presented as a candidate in the upcoming presidential elections in Ecuador to look for a third consecutive term, he said that he sees that possibility "with a lot of optimism and joy, although sometimes it is quite hard."
Correa assured that if he loses the February 2013 elections he will retire from public life.
Personally I have never been interested in power, but in situations as unjust as that of Ecuador that socio-economic poverty can only be corrected from political power.
"My political movement believed that it was me who guaranteed that probable victory, because we have to accept that reponsibility," he said.
To win it would be my last term in the presidency and from there we came out of public life.
If we lose the same.
"It's a decision," he said.
Correa also referred to the new health treatment of Venezuelan President Hugo Chavez in Cuba.
I have just spoken to Venezuelan Vice President Nicolas Maduro and he tells me that he traveled to a treatment that was already planned, of routine, that he was expected to pass the campaign to return to Cuba.
"It doesn't mean a relapse in President Chavez's state of health," he said.
The Ecuadorian head of state participated today in Lima in the VI Summit of Heads of State and Government of the Union of South American Nations (UNASUR) that concluded with calls for greater regional integration to sustain progress, equality and security.
AIDS deaths today are a cause of late detection
Fabrizio was 21 years of age when he was confirmed the result of the test: AIDS positive.
"It was as if I dropped a bomb," he says when recalling the moment of the announcement, that the doctor was trying to make "smoother", without obvious success.
The boy hid it from his family.
He decided to take care only of his illness and began to find out about it; with such an effort that he already celebrated his 43rd birthday.
It is, without a doubt, one of the oldest patients in the HIV Unit of the Civil Hospital of Guadalajara (HCG), to which he arrived in 1994 after several battles.
Fabrizio has been living with human immunodeficiency (HIV) virus for 22 years, somewhat difficult to imagine in the early 1990s, when there were many doubts, few treatment options and more stigma.
Then, even the director of an IMSS clinic avoided saying goodbye to him "because it brought a cut".
Having AIDS was synonymous with death.
Now it is possible to survive this syndrome and do it with quality of life.
However, unaware of his illness, even many people arrive when the virus has already wreaked havoc, "exhausted" their immune system and are victims of opportunistic infections.
31 years after the appearance of AIDS in the world, at least of the first cases reported, "the great achievement means at the moment that the survival of a patient who initiates treatment in a timely manner and the survival of the general population, is exactly the same," said the head of the HCG HIV Unit, Jaime Andrade Villanueva, after referring that this information was endorsed in April of this year in a prestigious scientific publication.
Infectologist and HIV/AIDS expert, Andrade Villanueva commented that since 2008, scientists had concluded that AIDS was not a fatal sentence, but the years of survival and quality of life depend on the degree of affectation to the immune system that patients present when initiating diagnosis, with better expectation for those who are not drug users: up to 30 years with a count of 200 CD4 and 50 years, when they report 500 CD4.
In simple words, this means that whoever receives the diagnosis of HIV positive at 25 years of age, under these terms "as long as he stays in control he will be able to live without problems up to the age of 75," the interviewee explained.
To measure this advance, it should be remembered that the life expectancy of Mexicans today is 76 years on average.
While mortality has dropped significantly in recent years, in the case of Mexico, the number of people dying from AIDS dropped from 6,678 cases in 2007 to 4,862 in 2011 (UNAIDS annual report), it is also true that since the onset of AIDS, 60 percent of patients in the national database have died.
In Jalisco alone, 255 people died in 2011, and 187 deaths until May of this year; however it is ensured that there is universal access to antiretroviral drugs since 2005.
- Why is there still deaths?
- It seems to me that the problem isn't access to treatment.
That's how I visualize it, that's how it happened in our hospital.
At least in the last 12 years we have not had drug shortages the problem is that patients arrive very advanced because they do not know their state of infection, that is, in late stages of the disease.
He gave a blunt fact: "Nine out of ten patients arrive when they already have some opportunistic infection what has to be done to have a greater impact on global mortality, is to make earlier diagnoses and, therefore, they have to offer detection tests in a massive way, to all people who require it."
Specialists and officials of the State Council for the Prevention of AIDS in Jalisco (Coesida), also the patients themselves, such as Fabrizio, who went to get tested for a particular laboratory, only motivated because a friend had done it and, despite his young age, he was already in the stage of AIDS and even suffered sarcoma from Kaposi, a cancerous tumor that is one of the common complications.
Everything changes when you know you have AIDS.
Some think they're going to die and don't want to know anything.
"If I'm already going to die, I'd better burst three times a week," they say not in my case.
The change was to improve, I eat well, I exercise, I take the medications.
To date, his parents only know that he suffered from cancer.
I live a normal life, just like anyone.
"I work, I have a lot of activities, I travel, I have an active sexual life, but co-responsible, I take care of myself and the other person," said Fabrizio, who agreed to share his privacy with MILENIO JALISCO to motivate with his testimony those who today, in the framework of World AIDS Day, are afraid.
That they get tested, if they had a risk, that the sooner they know if they are HIV positive, it is better and if they already have the diagnosis, that they learn that they can live like anyone, being responsible.
That, his message, sums up the motto of battle to AIDS this 2012.
Condoms Behind the Counter
There are the gaps between the health programs and the ordinary citizen, said Ricardo Salazar, a tapatío journalist who has assumed the cause around HIV.
And the greatest is in prevention.
In the places dedicated to this task "the distribution of condoms has indeed increased, before they gave us one or two, now they give us packages of one hundred, and that is very well but it turns out that there are those today, still, do not have access to a condom," he said.
Among those most vulnerable to new infections are adolescents.
"What do you want them for?" is a common question, with snores and value judgments of social workers, school counselors, pharmacies employees and health personnel, to whom adolescents do not want to expose themselves, said the communicator.
He proposed to reorient this ineffective distribution; that the condoms are not only behind the counters and those one hundred are in public bathroom dispatchers of the places frequented by young people.
It's not motivating promiscuity.
It’s not about paying for their chelas and the motel, as Governor Emilio Gonzalez responded by asking him if there would be condom delivery in his administration.
