Hector "Hetin" Reyes: "Basketball has been my life"
Basketball globetrotter Héctor "Hetin" Reyes spent more than 60 years linked to the sport of the tabloncillo and, thanks to it, traveled the whole world.
Few people in Puerto Rico have a mental collection of the history of local basketball as broad as Héctor "Hetin" Reyes has.
And it is that before a stroke confined him to a wheelchair in 2008, Reyes was immersed in the sport for more than 60 years, he was as a player of minor categories, National Upper Basketball player, proxy and leader in the BSN with the Cowboys of Bayamón or president of the Basketball Federation.
"I put a lot of hats in basketball throughout my life, including several at the same time, such as when I was president of the BSN, general manager of the National Team and federal president during the 90s," Reyes recalled during a First Hour visit to his home in Bayamón, where he resides with Isabel, his faithful wife for more than 50 years.
"Basketball has been my life."
Reyes doesn't exaggerate doing that assertion.
The walls of his house, almost all decorated with photos and memorabilies that denote its extensive trajectory, thus attesting.
Bayamon at heart
Of all, those he treasures with greater emotion are those who recall his passage through the Cowboys from the middle of the 1950s as a player until 1982, when he turned 15 serving as co-possessessed or proxy of the franchise.
"Those were my best years, the ones I enjoyed the most because I had the opportunity to be part of eight Cowboy championships since 1967, whether it was as a proxy, co-powerer or manager.
There were many years of satisfaction, including the five championships run from 1971 to 1975.
And then I said goodbye to one in 1981, Jerome Mincy's debut year at the BSN.
From then on, "Cuco" Ortiz, who was a great administrator," Reyes said.
I remember Gene Bartow, who had directed here and was at the University of Alabama (Birmingham), told me, "I've got you a very strong, 6"7" stature player.
Do you want it?"
And that was the beginning of Mincy, one of the best players Puerto Rico has had.
Bartow then recommended sniper Raymond Gausse, who was nativized and was one of our best shooters.
I remember he said that if Mincy had given Bayamon a championship, Gausse would help bring another one.
The Cowboy championship with Gausse enjoyed it, but already in the distance, because for 1988 it was already figured of mandamás federativo.
For that time he preferred to enjoy his executions and Mincy's in the National Team.
I remember when we first beat the United States during the Pre-Olympic in Mexico in 1989.
Then came the 1990 World Cup, where we arrived fourth place and it should have been bronze if not for that Canadian referee who made us repeat the final play for the second time, Reyes said.
Is this 1990 World Cup group the best National Team you’ve ever seen?
He's among the best, just like the one who beat the Dream Team at the 2004 Olympics.
But my favorite was that of the Pan-Americans in Cuba in 1991, when we won gold and gave a peel to the U.S. team, which was quite similar to the one that won us bronze in the world championship.
That team not only repeated Mincy, Gausse, Ramon Rivas, Fico López and "Piculín" (Ortiz), but also had (Javier) "Toñito" Colón and James Carter young, the Leon brothers (Francisco and Edgar) and Mario "Quixote" Morales, who had not been able to be in 90 due to a knee injury.
A team that perhaps was not the best in terms of personnel, but gave us a gold medal and a great joy was that of the 1995 Pre-Olympic in Neuquén, Argentina.
With role-players like "Canito" Nieves, Pablo Alicea and a youthful Rolando Hourruitiner in place of the players suspended by the mess of the Pan-American of Mar del Plata, we won gold against all odds.
What was Puerto Rico’s best player?
Without a doubt, that has been Piculín Ortiz.
Their international tournament-level numbers are impressive.
No one in Puerto Rico has mastered at that level like Piculín did.
And that without counting his trajectory in the different leagues he played.
Who was the best Puerto Rican leader?
That's very difficult.
We have had a very good litter that includes Julio Toro, Flor Meléndez, Carlos Morales, Raymond Dalmau, Armandito Torres.
Of the young people, I really like Leo Arill's work.
What do you consider your greatest achievement in the federation?
Having been part of the most glorious era of the National Team between 1988 and 1995 and that at the beginning of the 90s the BSN had up to 17 teams in a season.
What did you have left to do?
There were things I would have wanted to implement, such as the regionalization of minor categories.
For example, that Ponce boys will only play in their area and only face teams from other corners of the Island in the national playoffs.
Right now children travel and play too much unnecessarily.
At least I see the fruit of compulsory certifications and course for leaders, table officers and referees.
That pleases me.
What are you doing now?
All I do is listen to music, watch music videos of my time on YouTube, enjoy my grandchildren and occasionally go to basketball games.
And of course, enjoy the company of my wife, Isabel, who has always been with me.
Actor Larry Hagman dies
Larry Hagman, born September 21, 1931, in Forth Worth (Texas), became world famous for his central role as John Ross Ewing, better known as "J.R.", in the television series "Dallas", in which he embodied an unscrupulous, malicious and manipulative businessman.
Larry Hagman, whose role as oil tycoon predator J.R. Ewing in the television series "Dallas" became a symbol of greed in the 1980's, has died.
I was 81 years old.
Hagman, who returned this year as J.R. in a new season of "Dallas," died Friday afternoon due to cancer complications, according to a family statement provided to The Associated Press by Warner Bros.
"Larry was back in his beloved hometown of Dallas, representing again the iconic role he liked most," the family said.
Larry’s family and their closest friends were with him in Dallas for the Thanksgiving holiday.
Linda Gray, who played the role of her wife in the original series and in the sequel, was with Hagman when she died in a Dallas hospital, her publicist Jeffrey Lane said.
It brought joy to everyone I knew.
He was creative, generous, funny, funny, caring and talented, and I will miss him so much.
"He was an original person and he lived his life to the maximum," Gray said in a statement.
Hagman was diagnosed with liver cirrhosis in 1992 and acknowledged that he had drunk heavily for years.
In 1995 she had a malignant tumor on her liver and underwent a transplant.
Years before "Dallas", Hagman became famous on television as a decent guy in the light comedy "I Dream of Jeannie," broadcast on NBC from 1965 to 1970.
In it she played Captain Tony Nelson, an astronaut whose life is disturbed when she encounters an attractive genius, played by Barbara Eden, and takes her to live with him home.
He also starred in two comedies who had little time on air, "The Good Life" (NBC, 1971-72) and "Here We Go Again" (ABC, 1973).
His film work included well-received critical roles in "The Group", "Harry and Tonto" and "Primary Colors".
But it was his masterful portrayal of the enchantingly detestable J.R. that led Hagman to his ultimate stardom.
The CBS drama series about the Ewing clan and other characters in its orbit was on the air from April 1978 to May 1991.
"Who shot J.R.?", designed to generate expectation around a full episode of emotions in which Hagman's character is almost killed, generated international speculation and risky millions of dollars in bookmakers.
It also helped give the series an audience record at the time.
When the response was revealed in a November 1980 episode, an average of 41 million viewers tuned it in and turned "Dallas" into the second most-watched entertainment show in history, behind the end of "MASH" in 1983, which had 50 million viewers.
It was J.R.’s sister-in-law, Kristin (played by Mary Crosby) who shot him.
J.R. pregnanted her and then threatened to brand her as a prostitute unless she left the city, but others also had reason to attack her.
Hagman portrayed Ewing as an insatiable corrupt with a charismatic smile: a cheating businessman and an infidel husband who tried to get his alcoholic wife, Sue Ellen (Linda Gray), interned.
"I know what I want on J.R," Hagman said in 1988.
You should say, "Here lies the honest citizen J.R. Ewing."
This is the only business he lost.
Victoria Principal, co-star of the original series, recalled Hagman Friday as someone “huge, on and off the screen.”
It is unforgettable, and irreplaceable, for millions of admirers in the world, and in the hearts of each of us, that we were lucky enough to know and love him.
Ten episodes of the new edition of "Dallas" were broadcast a few months ago with great success for the TNT channel.
He had already finished recording five of the chapters for the second season and a sixth was in process, the network reported.
Immediately there was no statement from Warner or TNT about how Hagman's loss will handle the series.
From Fort Worth, Texas, he was the son of actress and singer Mary Martin, who starred in classic works such as "South Pacific" and "Peter Pan."
Martin was still a teenager when she had it in 1931 during her marriage to lawyer Ben Hagman.
He tested luck on New York theatrical stages in the early 1950s, and then served in the Air Force from 1952 to 1956 in England.
While she was there she met the young Swedish designer Maj Axelsson and married her.
The couple had two sons, Preston and Heidi, and lived for a long time in the Californian city of Malibu, where many celebrities reside.
In 2001 he titled his memoir "Hello Darlin': Tall (and Absolutely True) Tales about My Life".
"I didn't put anything there that I believed would hurt someone or affect them in some way," she told the Associated Press at the time.
After the liver transplant he became a promoter of organ donation and volunteered in a hospital to assist fearful patients.
"I advise them, I value them, I meet with them when they come for their surgeries, and then," he said in 1996.
I'm trying to offer some consolation, like "don't fear, it's going to be a little uncomfortable for a short time, but they're going to be okay."
He was also an anti-smoking activist and participated in several campaigns.
They begin course that explores the "End of the World"
Every week, students explore apocalyptic topics such as nuclear war, zombies, viruses and germs, and global warming.
This semester, when Professor Stuart Charmé decided to teach an end-of-the-world course, he knew he had an irresistible hook: The end of the "long countdown" of the Mayan Calendar, on December 21, to which many people have clunged as proof that the end of the world is coming.
But Charmé had no idea what awaited him in the next couple of months: The cataclysmic Hurricane Sandy, a fiscal precipice that some have called “deudarmagedon” and a growing conflict involving Israel, the place where Christian theorists of the end of time believe the Apocalypse will begin.
“I didn’t realize this was going to be the most apocalyptic semester there has ever been,” Charmé told Rutgers-Camden (New Jersey) students this week.
If you look at what’s been going on in the world today like we’re 30 days away and counting, this has been a really good period.
And remember that bad is good for those of apocalyptic mentality.
And he’s not the only teacher offering courses on the “end of the world” this semester, in theory the last of history.
In Temple, Adjunct Professor Barry Vacker is teaching the "Media, Culture and the End of the World" course.
Every week, students explore apocalyptic topics such as nuclear war, zombies, viruses and germs, and global warming.
“We looked at why these ideas proliferate over time,” he said, and how they offer hypothetical scenarios that guide certain human behavior.
If nuclear material falls into the hands of terrorists, for example, war could break out.
This month, the students analyzed films with apocalyptic themes and explored how they compare to real-life examples.
“I’ve tried to inform students about what’s possible, probable, credible and impossible,” Vacker said.
On Pennsylvania State University’s main campus, Latin American History Professor Matthew Restall and colleague Amara Solari, an adjunct professor of Art History and Anthropology, have teamed up to teach a course, entitled only “The End of the World.”
“We don’t add ‘2012’ to always have the option to teach the course again, in case the world doesn’t end,” Restall said.
Despite "imminent ruin", students must study, generate projects and take final exams.
At Penn State, the final exam will be taken on the eve of the Apocalypse, which leaves students no choice but to work “until the same night the world is supposed to end,” Restall said.
The courses were quite popular.
“It saturated in two hours,” Restall said of his course for high-average students, which was filled with 35 students.
We received emails for weeks and weeks before the start of the semester, from people who asked if there was still room.
The students, for their part, say that the course is one of the most interesting.
“I’m fascinated to see what people do to comfort themselves,” said Bridgid Robinson, a 23-year-old Religion and Sociology student at Haddonfield, New Jersey, at Rutgers-Camden.
The apocalyptic, secular or religious mentality is just a matter of consolation or the absence thereof.
Will Wekesa, a 25-year-old Nursing and Nursing career student, said he saw all the apocalyptic films.
"I've never heard of a class that could teach," he said.
I enjoy it.
But no student interviewed — let alone a teacher — claimed to believe the Dec. 21 expiration date.
“Our first project was about the Mayan prophecy and in a certain way we discredited it,” said Julie Zeglen, a 21-year-old senior in Temple and West Chester.
The Mayans never predicted the end of the world; it’s just a key point on the calendar, Restall said.
But he noted that there is an apocalyptic anxiety in Western culture, which goes back several centuries, in which people react to changes around them predicting the end of the world.
The Internet has caused a boom in these speculations.
"Otherwise people don't think about this," he said.
Mostly in the English-speaking world.
Joseph Dougherty, a La Salle University religion professor who teaches courses in the Philippines this year, quickly answered the question of whether he knew about any course on the "end of the world" there.
“The Philippines does not participate in the end of the world,” he wrote, hinting at an exception of superior authority.
We've got a pardon from the Pope.
Restall noted that over the years there has been talk of many days of the final judgment, and said that if nothing happens on December 21, "people will immediately begin to think about the next date" or to philosophize that December 21 is the beginning of a seven-year period after which the world will end.
Students and teachers are taking the date lightly.
Several said they plan to go to "end-of-the-world" parties.
"Maybe I'll call some friends to laugh together," said Samira Ford, a 20-year-old communications student.
